{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LEaXTxJTAC4PNQxwTYgZyZ","type":"tool_result","content":"847\t## 9. Lift, lower, and the laws\n848\t\n849\t**Lift** (reference machine): refuse unless quiescent; number cursor cells (§6);\n850\twalk waits, parked records, the slice, module stores, the initial free\n851\tenvironment (continuation §6.3, §7.1), the result and each install\n852\tchild, emitting rows and the census; refuse by path as UCF\n853\t§7.5.4 and §7.2.1 specify. **Amended after 6b (2026-10-13; continuation\n854\t§7.1 B4):** the merged order is quiescence and safepoint, kind, free\n855\tenvironment, the walk (which fixes the segments, contexts and module\n856\tstores), code, the per-context dependency closure over live code, tree\n857\tcoverage from the composition's `:source-index`, frame validation of\n858\tthe assembled task, then canonical numbering and emit; the slice is the\n859\twhole root store and every parked record; custody is nil. The nested carrier's value table\n860\t(`:yin.k/values`) has no counterpart, sharing being Merkle; its cursor cell\n861\ttable becomes the census.\n862\t\n863\t**Lower**: §8 in order, then restore.\n864\t\n865\tThe laws, with the domains the Architect's finding 12 requires:\n866\t\n867\t- **Canonical round trip.** For every valid, canonical (§6-numbered)\n868\t  version-3 state `S` under the one profile of §2 **for which `lower(S)`\n869\t  succeeds** (adequate composition, resource and admission inputs; a\n870\t  refusal is not a counterexample), and with no execution between lower\n871\t  and lift, `lift(lower(S)) = S` by root id under the **fresh-receiver\n872\t  reconstruction context**: the receiver's id counter is 0 so\n873\t  `max(local, carried)` is the carried value; occurrence, origin and the\n874\t  custody arm are supplied to the lift from the restored task's own\n875\t  record, not minted; cursor-cell bodies and op-ids are carried verbatim; the\n876\t  lift's §6 numbering undoes resource remapping. Outside that context\n877\t  (a receiver with a larger counter, or any step taken) the law does not\n878\t  apply and native equivalence is the claim.\n879\t- **Native observational equivalence.** `lower(lift(m))` need not equal\n880\t  `m` as native state (fresh ids, seals, private keys); under equivalent\n881\t  external inputs it must preserve the next observable step, the wait\n882\t  set and its order, the result, and the effect trace.\n883\t- **Cross-engine.** Running the corpus to each safepoint on an engine\n884\t  that implements canonical semantic-VM execution yields, under\n885\t  controlled composition identities (occurrence, origin, resource\n886\t  descriptors fixed by the harness), the same version-3 row set the\n887\t  reference machine yields.\n888\t\n889\tThe UCF §7.11 harness gains one assertion per law. The Stage D/E corpus,\n890\tre-encoded as version-3 rows, is the oracle for the first law.\n891\t\n892\t## 10. Questions the review answered, and what remains\n893\t\n894\tAnswers 1–6 are the codex Architect's (round 1); where the Architect\n895\tmarked a point as an owner decision it is labelled so.\n896\t\n897\t1. **Occurrence inside the hash.** Kept inside, following the governing\n898\t   rule and binding custody context to the published checkpoint. An\n899\t   occurrence-independent execution-state address is an owner-level\n900\t   protocol decision; if ever pursued it must sit beside, not replace, a\n901\t   fully authenticated checkpoint address, and it does not substitute\n902\t   for the census.\n903\t2. **Packing.** Individual immutable content rows plus a self-contained\n904\t   transport pack. When a pack is addressed it gets its own address and\n905\t   canonical encoding, distinct from the semantic root id; cursor-cell ids\n906\t   remain root-scoped inside it. Same answer as code-as-tuples §2.1\n907\t   should receive; the loader interface is resolved explicitly, not by\n908\t   layout.\n909\t3. **The nested grammar.** Rows are the carrier authority.\n910\t4. **Contract stamp.** The code contract carried is `\"v4\"`, the\n911\t   register-shaped machine's; the carrier version is 3; the two are\n912\t   independent, and neither names a legacy value.\n913\t5. **Reified and parked.** Sharing immutable `:act` rows is acceptable;\n914\t   the two keep distinct outer kinds (§5.1) and distinct lowering. A\n915\t   reified capture has no pending; a parked reference resolves in task\n916\t   context.\n917\t6. **Foreign-engine value profiles.** Required before an implementation\n918\t   brief claims execution conformance for an engine **outside the three\n919\t   repository lanes** (the semantic-register kernel and the stack and\n920\t   register kernels, whose reconstruction is the layouts of\n921\t   `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md` §4 and needs no value\n922\t   profile), and not drafted here: admitted values, numeric behaviour,\n923\t   key equality, metadata, resource and profile requirements, refusal\n924\t   sets, and safepoint reconstruction obligations. Slot losslessness\n925\t   alone does not establish execution parity; version 3 carries the\n926\t   canonical frame; a stack or register kernel reconstructs it through\n927\t   its layout (continuation §4), which is heterogeneous reconstruction,\n928\t   not translation between native formats; foreign-engine *value*\n929\t   profiles for engines outside this repository remain a separate\n930\t   document.\n931\t\n932\tAnswers 7–9 are the Architect's (round 2) and are adopted (7 and 8 are\n933\tdeleted 2026-10-11: there are no other profile tables and no legacy\n934\treader):\n935\t\n936\t9. **Skeleton placeholder.** Safe only if the full value-union tag is\n937\t   retained in the skeleton, which §6 now requires (`[:cursor-cell <class>]`,\n938\t   never a bare class); and a placeholder does not canonicalize alias\n939\t   topology, which is why §6 is now defined as the output of the\n940\t   discovery-order procedure, which is invariant under renaming and host\n941\t   enumeration.\n942\t\n943\t10. **Receiver work bound** (Architect, round 3; adopted as a\n944\t    requirement, with the limits left to implementation and\n945\t    composition). Before an implementation brief, the receiver must have\n946\t    a bound on decoded bytes, rows, cursor cells, traversal depth and\n947\t    traversal-and-spelling work for §6 (its `:work` unit counts positions\n948\t    and representation bytes, charged before any spelling is encoded),\n949\t    with elapsed time as an optional\n950\t    operational limit on top. Exceeding a local budget produces a\n951\t    structured resource or capability refusal before any attachment, in\n952\t    the qualified-unsupported family `datom.world.md` already provides;\n953\t    it never labels a valid state `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`, never\n954\t    substitutes host enumeration order for the canonical walk, and never\n955\t    accepts an unfinished check. The\n956\t    exact limits and their refusal mapping are implementation and\n957\t    composition decisions and must not alter canonical bytes.\n958\t\n959\t**Proposals, not consequences.** The following are design choices this\n960\tdocument makes; they follow neither uniquely from the owner's three\n961\trulings nor from the six invariants, and an implementation brief must\n962\tname them as choices being accepted: a new body version (3) rather than\n963\tanother carrier identification; the canonical representative of §6 as\n964\tdefined by discovery order, and the `:unresolved-cursor-cells` refusal;\n965\tthe wire unit and pack framing of §3; the\n966\texplicit fork-policy spelling of §5.5; one engine-less profile\n967\t(continuation §2.1); the explicit root `free-env` (continuation §6.3);\n968\tthe byte-string and metadata restrictions of §4; binding the row digest to\n969\tthe carrier version rather than to a named algorithm (§3; the owner\n970\truled 2026-10-10 that rows use the same digest as code addresses, which\n971\tis Jing's default, rather than a separate SHA-256 pin); and positional\n972\trows over EAV (§1). Preserving the complete FFI\n973\tenvelope (§5.3) is *not* on this list: it is the inherited behaviour,\n974\tand narrowing it would have been the new protocol decision.\n975\t\n976\tStill open after this revision: none beyond the work-bound limits\n977\t(§10.10).\n978\t\n979\t## 11. What this does not change\n980\t\n981\tCode identity (A), safepoints,\n982\tpending-wait semantics and the r5 required keys, install-child rules,\n983\tthe dependency closure and satisfaction check, the isolated store, the\n984\tprivate resource table, module-store instancing, scheduler state, the\n985\tcustody header and admission rules, fencing and operation ids, the\n986\toutcome families, and the existing refusal kinds (three lift kinds and three\n987\t`:yin.k/undecodable` kinds are added, §8). The carrier is a new body\n988\tversion for the same facts. The\n989\tArchitect's round-1 statement is the acceptance bar: tuple transport\n990\tviolates none of the six invariants once polymorphic references and\n991\tcontext edges are specified and validated, and §4, §3 and §8 are where\n992\tthey are."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"23e67cb0-f183-4c2e-b3d6-2e5b6c44b814","uuid":"c7b0b6ae-cb70-4b63-889c-3bdfa2becfcc","timestamp":"2026-10-13T08:31:37.400Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7plan/docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md","content":"## 9. Lift, lower, and the laws\n\n**Lift** (reference machine): refuse unless quiescent; number cursor cells (§6);\nwalk waits, parked records, the slice, module stores, the initial free\nenvironment (continuation §6.3, §7.1), the result and each install\nchild, emitting rows and the census; refuse by path as UCF\n§7.5.4 and §7.2.1 specify. **Amended after 6b (2026-10-13; continuation\n§7.1 B4):** the merged order is quiescence and safepoint, kind, free\nenvironment, the walk (which fixes the segments, contexts and module\nstores), code, the per-context dependency closure over live code, tree\ncoverage from the composition's `:source-index`, frame validation of\nthe assembled task, then canonical numbering and emit; the slice is the\nwhole root store and every parked record; custody is nil. The nested carrier's value table\n(`:yin.k/values`) has no counterpart, sharing being Merkle; its cursor cell\ntable becomes the census.\n\n**Lower**: §8 in order, then restore.\n\nThe laws, with the domains the Architect's finding 12 requires:\n\n- **Canonical round trip.** For every valid, canonical (§6-numbered)\n  version-3 state `S` under the one profile of §2 **for which `lower(S)`\n  succeeds** (adequate composition, resource and admission inputs; a\n  refusal is not a counterexample), and with no execution between lower\n  and lift, `lift(lower(S)) = S` by root id under the **fresh-receiver\n  reconstruction context**: the receiver's id counter is 0 so\n  `max(local, carried)` is the carried value; occurrence, origin and the\n  custody arm are supplied to the lift from the restored task's own\n  record, not minted; cursor-cell bodies and op-ids are carried verbatim; the\n  lift's §6 numbering undoes resource remapping. Outside that context\n  (a receiver with a larger counter, or any step taken) the law does not\n  apply and native equivalence is the claim.\n- **Native observational equivalence.** `lower(lift(m))` need not equal\n  `m` as native state (fresh ids, seals, private keys); under equivalent\n  external inputs it must preserve the next observable step, the wait\n  set and its order, the result, and the effect trace.\n- **Cross-engine.** Running the corpus to each safepoint on an engine\n  that implements canonical semantic-VM execution yields, under\n  controlled composition identities (occurrence, origin, resource\n  descriptors fixed by the harness), the same version-3 row set the\n  reference machine yields.\n\nThe UCF §7.11 harness gains one assertion per law. The Stage D/E corpus,\nre-encoded as version-3 rows, is the oracle for the first law.\n\n## 10. Questions the review answered, and what remains\n\nAnswers 1–6 are the codex Architect's (round 1); where the Architect\nmarked a point as an owner decision it is labelled so.\n\n1. **Occurrence inside the hash.** Kept inside, following the governing\n   rule and binding custody context to the published checkpoint. An\n   occurrence-independent execution-state address is an owner-level\n   protocol decision; if ever pursued it must sit beside, not replace, a\n   fully authenticated checkpoint address, and it does not substitute\n   for the census.\n2. **Packing.** Individual immutable content rows plus a self-contained\n   transport pack. When a pack is addressed it gets its own address and\n   canonical encoding, distinct from the semantic root id; cursor-cell ids\n   remain root-scoped inside it. Same answer as code-as-tuples §2.1\n   should receive; the loader interface is resolved explicitly, not by\n   layout.\n3. **The nested grammar.** Rows are the carrier authority.\n4. **Contract stamp.** The code contract carried is `\"v4\"`, the\n   register-shaped machine's; the carrier version is 3; the two are\n   independent, and neither names a legacy value.\n5. **Reified and parked.** Sharing immutable `:act` rows is acceptable;\n   the two keep distinct outer kinds (§5.1) and distinct lowering. A\n   reified capture has no pending; a parked reference resolves in task\n   context.\n6. **Foreign-engine value profiles.** Required before an implementation\n   brief claims execution conformance for an engine **outside the three\n   repository lanes** (the semantic-register kernel and the stack and\n   register kernels, whose reconstruction is the layouts of\n   `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md` §4 and needs no value\n   profile), and not drafted here: admitted values, numeric behaviour,\n   key equality, metadata, resource and profile requirements, refusal\n   sets, and safepoint reconstruction obligations. Slot losslessness\n   alone does not establish execution parity; version 3 carries the\n   canonical frame; a stack or register kernel reconstructs it through\n   its layout (continuation §4), which is heterogeneous reconstruction,\n   not translation between native formats; foreign-engine *value*\n   profiles for engines outside this repository remain a separate\n   document.\n\nAnswers 7–9 are the Architect's (round 2) and are adopted (7 and 8 are\ndeleted 2026-10-11: there are no other profile tables and no legacy\nreader):\n\n9. **Skeleton placeholder.** Safe only if the full value-union tag is\n   retained in the skeleton, which §6 now requires (`[:cursor-cell <class>]`,\n   never a bare class); and a placeholder does not canonicalize alias\n   topology, which is why §6 is now defined as the output of the\n   discovery-order procedure, which is invariant under renaming and host\n   enumeration.\n\n10. **Receiver work bound** (Architect, round 3; adopted as a\n    requirement, with the limits left to implementation and\n    composition). Before an implementation brief, the receiver must have\n    a bound on decoded bytes, rows, cursor cells, traversal depth and\n    traversal-and-spelling work for §6 (its `:work` unit counts positions\n    and representation bytes, charged before any spelling is encoded),\n    with elapsed time as an optional\n    operational limit on top. Exceeding a local budget produces a\n    structured resource or capability refusal before any attachment, in\n    the qualified-unsupported family `datom.world.md` already provides;\n    it never labels a valid state `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`, never\n    substitutes host enumeration order for the canonical walk, and never\n    accepts an unfinished check. The\n    exact limits and their refusal mapping are implementation and\n    composition decisions and must not alter canonical bytes.\n\n**Proposals, not consequences.** The following are design choices this\ndocument makes; they follow neither uniquely from the owner's three\nrulings nor from the six invariants, and an implementation brief must\nname them as choices being accepted: a new body version (3) rather than\nanother carrier identification; the canonical representative of §6 as\ndefined by discovery order, and the `:unresolved-cursor-cells` refusal;\nthe wire unit and pack framing of §3; the\nexplicit fork-policy spelling of §5.5; one engine-less profile\n(continuation §2.1); the explicit root `free-env` (continuation §6.3);\nthe byte-string and metadata restrictions of §4; binding the row digest to\nthe carrier version rather than to a named algorithm (§3; the owner\nruled 2026-10-10 that rows use the same digest as code addresses, which\nis Jing's default, rather than a separate SHA-256 pin); and positional\nrows over EAV (§1). Preserving the complete FFI\nenvelope (§5.3) is *not* on this list: it is the inherited behaviour,\nand narrowing it would have been the new protocol decision.\n\nStill open after this revision: none beyond the work-bound limits\n(§10.10).\n\n## 11. What this does not change\n\nCode identity (A), safepoints,\npending-wait semantics and the r5 required keys, install-child rules,\nthe dependency closure and satisfaction check, the isolated store, the\nprivate resource table, module-store instancing, scheduler state, the\ncustody header and admission rules, fencing and operation ids, the\noutcome families, and the existing refusal kinds (three lift kinds and three\n`:yin.k/undecodable` kinds are added, §8). The carrier is a new body\nversion for the same facts. The\nArchitect's round-1 statement is the acceptance bar: tuple transport\nviolates none of the six invariants once polymorphic references and\ncontext edges are specified and validated, and §4, §3 and §8 are where\nthey are.","numLines":146,"startLine":847,"totalLines":993}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01LEaXTxJTAC4PNQxwTYgZyZ","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
