{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011Cfyvc12xx9JuxkFtDRGqk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CgfihKxZ5mHPJUxN4dJ8Bp","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7plan/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md","offset":1715,"limit":480},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":18705,"cache_read_input_tokens":56271,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":18705},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"23e67cb0-f183-4c2e-b3d6-2e5b6c44b814","uuid":"ffc65bed-402e-4bca-8227-cabdc8d8fa3c","timestamp":"2026-10-13T08:31:43.243Z","request_id":"req_011CfyvbzkbqiVjAFmkGJrMN","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01CgfihKxZ5mHPJUxN4dJ8Bp":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7plan/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md","offset":1715,"limit":480}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CgfihKxZ5mHPJUxN4dJ8Bp","type":"tool_result","content":"1715\t## §7.7 Ownership arbitration — custody as a lease over datoms (blocker 4)\n1716\t\n1717\t### 7.7.1 The race, named\n1718\t\n1719\tThe emitter parks, lifts, appends the UCF value to a medium, and keeps its\n1720\tlocal `:parked` record. Three readers see the value. The emitter's own wait\n1721\tset wakes because the stream it was waiting on delivered. Now four machines\n1722\tbelieve they may continue one computation. Nothing in DaoStream can stop\n1723\tthem: a stream has no privileged reader, there is no destructive read, and\n1724\t\"the hard part of a take was never the removal but the *exclusion* of\n1725\tcompeting takers, which is a lease over datoms\" (`dao.stream.md`,\n1726\t*Explicitly Absent*).\n1727\t\n1728\tUCF adopts that sentence as its design. **Resumption is a take. A take is a\n1729\twrite. Exclusion is a lease.** Nothing new is added to the stream contract,\n1730\tand no key is added to any result map.\n1731\t\n1732\t### 7.7.2 Custody facts, and their dispatch keys\n1733\t\n1734\tCustody is negotiated on an **arbitration medium** — a `dao.space` whose\n1735\tfacts are ordinary datoms — using `dao.lease.md`'s vocabulary unchanged,\n1736\twith the checkpoint **occurrence** as the leased subject. The UCF-authored\n1737\tfacts are dispatch-keyed exactly as lease facts are (`dao.lease.md`,\n1738\t*Vocabulary*: a reader switches on `:dao.lease/status`; a fact carrying it\n1739\tis a lease fact, one carrying neither is ignored):\n1740\t\n1741\t| Fact | Dispatch | Author | Required keys |\n1742\t|---|---|---|---|\n1743\t| offer | `:yin.k/custody :yin.k/offered` | emitter | `:yin.k/occurrence`, `:yin.k/id` (this snapshot variant), `:yin.k/policy`, `:yin.k/medium` (the carrier identity) |\n1744\t| checkpoint done | `:yin.k/custody :yin.k/resumed` | holder | `:yin.k/occurrence`, `:dao.lease/lease`, `:yin.k/result` — the address of the successor's occurrence value or of a `:yin.k/result` |\n1745\t\n1746\tLease facts themselves keep their own table verbatim — proposal\n1747\t(`:dao.lease/proposed`, with `:dao.lease/subject = {:yin.k/occurrence O}`),\n1748\tgrant (`:dao.lease/accepted`, with `:dao.lease/lease :dao.lease/holder\n1749\t:dao.lease/subject :dao.lease/duration`, and `:dao.lease/max` optional),\n1750\trefusal, release, renewal, lapse — each dispatched on `:dao.lease/status`\n1751\tor `:dao.lease/event`, each with `dao.lease.md`'s required keys, unchanged.\n1752\tA lease judge reading the same medium switches on the lease keys and\n1753\tignores the `:yin.k/custody` facts; a UCF reader switches on\n1754\t`:yin.k/custody` and reads lease facts for authority. Both `:yin.k/custody`\n1755\tfacts are **evidence, not authority**: an offer creates no claim, and a\n1756\t`:resumed` record is a holder's report — completion is the grantor's ledger\n1757\ttransition, not the report (§7.7.6). Authority is exactly `dao.lease.md`'s:\n1758\t**only grantor-authored facts establish terms.**\n1759\t\n1760\tThe grantor's own facts on that ledger (M-next C, slice C5), each\n1761\twritten in the published attribute order of `yin.vm.ucf.ledger`:\n1762\t\n1763\t| Fact | Dispatch | Author | Keys, in order |\n1764\t|---|---|---|---|\n1765\t| admitted offer | `:yin.k/custody :yin.k/offered` | authority | `:yin.k/custody`, `:yin.k/occurrence`, `:yin.k/id`, `:yin.k/policy`, `:yin.k/medium`, `:yin.k/baseline` (the operation baseline the inspector derived from the variant's bytes) |\n1766\t| grant | `:dao.lease/status :dao.lease/accepted` | authority | `:dao.lease/status`, `:dao.lease/lease`, `:dao.lease/proposal`, `:dao.lease/subject`, `:dao.lease/holder`, `:dao.lease/duration`, `:dao.lease/max` (optional) |\n1767\t| grant binding | `:yin.k/custody :yin.k/bound` | authority | `:yin.k/custody`, `:yin.k/occurrence`, `:dao.lease/lease`, `:dao.lease/holder`, `:yin.k/epoch` (7.7.8) |\n1768\t\n1769\tThe admitted offer is not the emitter's offer: it shares the\n1770\tdispatch value but is authored by the authority and carries\n1771\t`:yin.k/baseline`, and it records one admitted snapshot variant. The\n1772\temitter's offer stays evidence. A grant and its binding are one\n1773\ttransaction.\n1774\t\n1775\tSlices C6, C7 and C10 add these grantor facts, in the same order rule:\n1776\t\n1777\t| Fact | Dispatch | Author | Keys, in order |\n1778\t|---|---|---|---|\n1779\t| epoch change | `:yin.k/custody :yin.k/reclaimed` | authority | `:yin.k/custody`, `:yin.k/occurrence`, `:dao.lease/lease`, `:yin.k/epoch` (the occurrence's epoch after the reclaim, 7.7.8) |\n1780\t| refusal | `:yin.k/custody :yin.k/refused` | authority | `:yin.k/custody`, `:yin.k/proposer`, `:dao.lease/proposal` (7.7.8) |\n1781\t| fenced admission | `:yin.k/custody :yin.k/fenced` | authority | `:yin.k/custody`, `:yin.k/op-id`, `:yin.k/incarnation`, `:yin.k/epoch` (the lease and epoch that admitted the op id) |\n1782\t| quarantine | `:yin.k/custody :yin.k/quarantined` | authority | `:yin.k/custody`, `:yin.k/occurrence`, `:yin.k/op-id` (the op id whose intent conflicted) |\n1783\t| input | `:yin.k/custody :yin.k/input` | authority | `:yin.k/custody`, `:yin.k/occurrence`, `:dao.lease/lease`, `:yin.k/input-seq`, `:yin.k/source`, `:yin.k/observed` (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.2.2) |\n1784\t\n1785\tA lapse and its epoch change are one transaction, and so are the two\n1786\thalves of a refusal (7.7.8). A fenced admission commits in one\n1787\ttransaction with its op id's dedup record; an intent conflict commits\n1788\tonly the quarantine.\n1789\t\n1790\tSlice C8's completion facts, as recorded by the authority:\n1791\t\n1792\t| Fact | Dispatch | Author | Keys, in order |\n1793\t|---|---|---|---|\n1794\t| recorded report | `:yin.k/custody :yin.k/resumed` | authority | `:yin.k/custody`, `:yin.k/occurrence`, `:dao.lease/lease`, `:yin.k/result`, `:yin.k/successor` (for a continuation only: the occurrence the authority verified) |\n1795\t| closure | `:yin.k/custody :yin.k/completed` | authority | `:yin.k/custody`, `:yin.k/occurrence`, `:dao.lease/lease` (the released lease) |\n1796\t| edge | `:yin.k/custody :yin.k/succeeded` | authority | `:yin.k/custody`, `:yin.k/occurrence`, then exactly one of `:yin.k/successor` (the successor edge) and `:yin.k/result` (the terminal edge to a halted result's address, 7.7.6) |\n1797\t\n1798\tThe recorded report is the holder's report after the authority\n1799\tverified it (7.7.8). It names no author: its author is the lease's\n1800\tholder, which the ledger already holds. It is still evidence;\n1801\tcompletion is the closure. A closure and its edge commit in the\n1802\ttransaction of the lease's `:release` lapse, between the lapse and\n1803\tits epoch change.\n1804\t\n1805\tThe subject of every grant is the **occurrence**, never `:yin.k/id`:\n1806\tsnapshot variants and retries of one park share one occurrence, so no pair\n1807\tof encodings can hold independent grants, and an equal-content copy minted\n1808\tby coincidence is a different occurrence and a different computation\n1809\t(§7.3.4).\n1810\t\n1811\t### 7.7.3 The authority: what the grantor possesses\n1812\t\n1813\t`dao.lease.md` is explicit that a judge must be composed **inside the\n1814\tboundary that possesses the resource** and must reclaim *by acting on what\n1815\tit itself holds*; a judge that possesses nothing is outside the contract.\n1816\tThe first draft designated \"the composition-designated arbiter for the\n1817\tcarrier medium,\" defaulting to the emitter — an arbiter relative to a\n1818\tcarrier, possessing an offer, which is a copy of content: the same\n1819\toccurrence carried on a second medium would acquire a second arbiter, and\n1820\tneither arbiter controls execution of anything. This section replaces that\n1821\twith a grounded design.\n1822\t\n1823\t**The possessed resource is the occurrence's execution ledger**: the set of\n1824\tdatoms about one occurrence id on the arbitration `dao.space` — its offer,\n1825\tgrants, renewals, lapses, and resumed reports, plus the occurrence's\n1826\t**admission epoch** (§7.7.5). The **grantor is the boundary that transacts\n1827\tthat space** — its transactor. Possession is exact and of the right kind:\n1828\t\n1829\t- the ledger's datom base lives inside the transactor's boundary, and\n1830\t  admission to it is the transactor's alone — no other boundary can write\n1831\t  those datoms, which is why grantor-authored facts are worth more than\n1832\t  holder reports;\n1833\t- the **reclaim** is the grantor acting on what it holds: recording the\n1834\t  lapse on its own base and advancing the occurrence's epoch datom. It\n1835\t  needs no cooperation from any holder and no reach into any carrier;\n1836\t- the arbiter is **not medium-relative**: the binding\n1837\t  occurrence → arbitration space is minted at first export and travels *in\n1838\t  the value* (`:yin.k/arbitration`, §7.2), so every carrier, every snapshot\n1839\t  variant, and every copy names the same one authority. The same content on\n1840\t  two media does not create a second arbiter, because the authority is named\n1841\t  by the occurrence, not discovered from wherever a value happened to land;\n1842\t- **one holder per occurrence is the grantor's policy, enforced where\n1843\t  policy lives**: `dao.lease` does not impose it, and UCF does not add to\n1844\t  the vocabulary — the grantor's ledger admits at most one live lease per\n1845\t  occurrence (a second grant requires the prior lease reclaimed first,\n1846\t  which `dao.lease.md`'s own validity rules make the grantor the sole\n1847\t  author of). Exclusive custody is thus a property of the ledger the\n1848\t  grantor possesses, not an aspiration over streams it does not.\n1849\t\n1850\tWhat is possessed, concretely, is the *right to admit effects as this\n1851\toccurrence* — the ledger is where epochs and dedup-stamped admissions are\n1852\trecorded, which is what §7.7.5's fencing consults. A composition that\n1853\tcannot wire a `dao.space` transactor as authority cannot offer\n1854\t`:yin.k/exclusive` custody at all; it composes `:yin.k/fork` only. That is\n1855\tan honest capability limit, not a gap to paper over.\n1856\t\n1857\t### 7.7.4 The exporting state: fencing the source before publication\n1858\t\n1859\tEmitter-as-candidate was the first draft's whole answer to the\n1860\tsource-wakeup race, and it does not close the publication window. The\n1861\tlifecycle published first and made the emitter a candidate afterward, but\n1862\tnothing ever removed the emitter's *local* eligibility: its driver may\n1863\tstill poll the wait set — and polling a blocked writer **performs the\n1864\tappend** (the engine, a `:put` entry retries `append!` on every\n1865\tpoll) — may restore a woken entry from the ready queue, may satisfy a\n1866\tdirect `:resume`, and may retry a blocked write, all after the value is in\n1867\tother machines' hands. Delaying register restoration is too late; the\n1868\tappend is the effect.\n1869\t\n1870\tUCF therefore defines an explicit **exporting** transition that precedes\n1871\tany publication, and the lift driver performs it as one step over the\n1872\tmachine value:\n1873\t\n1874\t1. **Quiesce**: the ready-queue must be empty (§7.6.3).\n1875\t2. **Detach the wait set**: every wait entry moves from the machine's\n1876\t   wait-set into the export record. The machine's wait-set is now empty,\n1877\t   so *there is nothing to poll* — no poll, no append, no effect. The\n1878\t   entries are not lost: the export record retains them verbatim; they are\n1879\t   the source of the `:yin.k/pending` variants of §7.4.3.\n1880\t3. **Neutralize control**: the record's control and K are already absent\n1881\t   (the machine is parked or blocked); direct `:resume` resolves against\n1882\t   `:parked`, and the parked records referenced by reachable code travel\n1883\t   inside the export record — a local `:resume` against them is refused\n1884\t   while exporting (the restore function observes the exporting machine\n1885\t   and declines), because the parked id now names a checkpoint that may be\n1886\t   granted elsewhere.\n1887\t4. **Publish, then offer**: append segments and the value body; record\n1888\t   `:yin.k/offered` on the arbitration medium. Only now do readers exist,\n1889\t   and the source was fenced before any of them did.\n1890\t\n1891\tThe exporting machine is pure data with an empty wait-set and a retained\n1892\texport record — no engine change, no callback, no timer; the drivers of the\n1893\tworld observe the state as they observe `ready-for-ingress?` (§3.1), and a\n1894\tdriver that polls an exporting machine's wait set anyway is a host assembly\n1895\tdefect of the same kind as wiring a refusing deposit destination.\n1896\t\n1897\t**Failure recovery and idempotent retry.** If publication fails — a full\n1898\tcarrier, an unreachable arbitration medium — the machine stays exporting\n1899\tand the composition retries; a retry re-encodes or reuses the encoded value\n1900\tand **reuses the occurrence** (§7.3.4), so no retry mints a second runnable\n1901\tcheckpoint. Abandoning the export reinstates the export record into the\n1902\tmachine (wait entries return to the wait-set, parked records to `:parked`)\n1903\tand the task resumes as a purely local one — the sole paths back to local\n1904\texecution are *abort export* (before the offer is recorded) and *a grant to\n1905\tthe emitter itself* (after); there is no third.\n1906\t\n1907\t(M-next D8/D9, as built.) The exporting machine carries one gate key,\n1908\t`:yin.k/gate`, with the modes `:running`, `:exporting` and `:ended`:\n1909\tunder `:running` internal computation proceeds and stream observations\n1910\tand effects are deferred to the driver; under `:exporting` and `:ended`\n1911\tnothing is observed, no install child is advanced, a direct resume is\n1912\trefused, and every public apply refuses a late result. The root's mode\n1913\tis stamped on each install child at its creation and before each run;\n1914\ta child carries the mode only. A machine entering exporting refuses\n1915\tthe custody holds -- a wait entry with reason `:observe`, an entry or\n1916\tcursor cell carrying `:yin.k/held`, a cursor cell carrying `:yin.k/unminted`,\n1917\ta machine with a nonempty `:yin.k/closes` queue, and a\n1918\t`:link-request` entry without `:cursor` -- as `:yin.k/non-portable`,\n1919\tkind `:reason-mismatch`, with the holding task's path; the lift\n1920\trefuses the same holds on its own behalf. A task is not at a liftable\n1921\tsafepoint while it holds any of these.\n1922\t\n1923\tThe lift's custody input is one **header** argument -- supplied to\n1924\t`holder.export/prepare [machine record serve! header]`, stored in the\n1925\texport record as `:header`, and passed to `export-task` through its\n1926\t`:header` option -- `nil` or:\n1927\t\n1928\t    {:yin.k/occurrence  O\n1929\t     :yin.k/arbitration {:dao.stream/identity i\n1930\t                         :dao.stream/descriptor d}\n1931\t     :yin.k/origin      {:yin.k/occurrence P\n1932\t                         :dao.lease/lease L\n1933\t                         :yin.k/emitter a}  ; absent on a first export\n1934\t     :yin.k/next-op-seq n\n1935\t     :yin.k/enrolled    #{stream-identity ...}}\n1936\t\n1937\tA `nil` header lifts today's version-0 fork; a header lifts version 1,\n1938\texclusive, in the canonical codec, with `:yin.k/policy\n1939\t:yin.k/exclusive` added by the lift. The occurrence is minted once by\n1940\tthe driver and persisted before prepare is called; `:yin.k/enrolled`\n1941\tis the composition's declaration from the ledger reader; it is never\n1942\tencoded into the UCF body, but the header stays in the retained export\n1943\trecord. The live record can contain authentic closures and is not itself\n1944\ta canonical data object. `holder.export/freeze [machine prepared-record]`\n1945\tencodes a separate content-addressed export-recovery object, tagged\n1946\t`:yin.k/export-recovery` with format version 1, using the handoff value/cursor-cell\n1947\tcodec. It returns recovery bytes/address beside the exact handoff body\n1948\tbytes/address; both are stored durably before the journal's fenced event.\n1949\tRecovery retains the local enrolled declaration, prior gate, ordered waits,\n1950\tparked continuations, issue stamps, descriptors and dependency closure.\n1951\tThe enrolled declaration still never travels in a published handoff body.\n1952\t\n1953\t`holder.export/rehydrate-fenced` validates the recovery object and its\n1954\tembedded body before administrative attachment. It creates authentic\n1955\tfresh-receiver-owned values and preserves resource aliases and distinct\n1956\tcursor cells without program reads, cursor minting, writes, closes, initialization\n1957\tor execution. Every task stays exporting, with waits outside scheduler\n1958\tqueues. The prior gate is retained only as data; recovery is neither grant\n1959\tadmission nor permission to abort back into execution. Runtime composition\n1960\tfunctions and handles are the environment, not the canonical journal.\n1961\tIncomplete preparations cannot manufacture a missing snapshot on restart.\n1962\tRecovery also retains a complete codec snapshot when legacy fork bytes\n1963\tomit transitive dependencies; the published version-0 bytes stay unchanged.\n1964\tFor version 1, the module-store fixed point is a correctness repair:\n1965\tcanonical ordering uses a separate scratch census and did not populate\n1966\tthe emitted dependency table. Bodies that previously omitted transitive\n1967\tmodule stores now contain them and have different content addresses; this\n1968\tis not a new handoff version or closure format. The transitive-store test\n1969\tpins the old one-pass body and corrected body bytes by their addresses.\n1970\t\n1971\tThe driver's exit persists recovery and body, journals the fence, attempts\n1972\tthe stable successor offer, reports that exact successor/body, carries\n1973\trelease of the matching origin lease, observes the authoritative closure\n1974\tand successor edge, then retries the same offer until admitted. The\n1975\trecovery object is stored first and a store that refuses stops the step\n1976\tbefore the body is attempted; a retry re-brackets from the recovery\n1977\tobject, idempotent through the store. An\n1978\t`:awaiting-completion` offer answer does not block reporting. A halt reports\n1979\tits result body and observes the terminal edge, with no successor offer.\n1980\tEvery external attempt has a durable intent before delivery and an\n1981\tacknowledgment afterward; an unmatched intent retains unknown delivery.\n1982\tA non-admitted report answer either retries -- `:suspended` resends the\n1983\tidentical request -- or ends the run (r3 1.6): the terminal refusals\n1984\tgate the machine `:ended`, append one diagnostic and carry the cleanup\n1985\trelease, which closes nothing by itself. A legal abort journals\n1986\tone terminal `:aborted` record before local execution is handed back,\n1987\tand the journal fold treats that record as closing the export. A holder\n1988\trechecks tenure across that append -- dao.lease's own `holding?`, with\n1989\tthe grant's max cap and its stopped latches -- and tenure that died\n1990\tunder it ends the run instead of restoring the machine; the cleanup\n1991\trelease opens with the next step, after the terminal record.\n1992\tReopened holders do not regain tenure from persisted clock readings:\n1993\tthey reconcile, release and re-enter candidacy, never resume execution.\n1994\t\n1995\tKind and\n1996\theader agree at lift: blocked and parked roots carry occurrence,\n1997\tarbitration, counter and policy, and origin when present; a halted\n1998\troot carries origin only, and a header-bearing halt without an origin\n1999\tis refused -- it is not automatically downgraded (a halt with no\n2000\torigin at all is a version-0 result under a `nil` header); install\n2001\tchildren carry no header. Before answering `:ok`, a version-1 root\n2002\tlift runs `checkpoint/inspect` on its own bytes and address, then\n2003\t`handoff/validate-body` on the decoded body; install children do not\n2004\tindependently undergo the root custody inspection. Any refusal\n2005\treturns as the lift's refusal and nothing is published. A lift under a\n2006\theader refuses `:yin.k/non-portable`, kind `:op-seq-exhausted`, at the\n2007\t2^52-1 counter of a blocked or parked root (a halted root ignores that\n2008\theader value), kind `:unprotected-pending` for a retained `:put`,\n2009\t`:ffi-request` or `:link-request` entry, in the root or an install\n2010\tchild, with no operation id whose target identity is enrolled, and\n2011\t`:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream for an operation id on an\n2012\tunenrolled target.\n2013\t\n2014\tAbort is permitted only before any possibly accepted offer attempt, or\n2015\tupon authoritative evidence that this occurrence has never been\n2016\tadmitted and cannot still become admitted from an outstanding attempt;\n2017\ta refusal of one request is not such evidence. As implemented,\n2018\t`holder.export/abort` reads the composition's persisted `attempts`\n2019\tsequence -- each attempt's append outcome under `:append` -- and\n2020\taccepts abort only with no attempts or when every attempt's outcome is\n2021\tin the closed set `#{:dao.stream/full :dao.stream/invalid-value\n2022\t:dao.stream/closed :dao.stream/refused}`; `transport-error` and a\n2023\tmissing answer refuse. A holder exporting a successor may abort only\n2024\tunder current valid tenure, implemented as `{:now n :bound b :live\n2025\ttrue}` with `:live` supplied by ledger evidence that the lease is the\n2026\toccurrence's active lease at its epoch; at or past the bound, and\n2027\twithout the evidence, abort is refused. After the lease has ended its\n2028\told local machine is never restored.\n2029\t\n2030\t(M-next D15a, as built.) The driver steps this state through a\n2031\tcustody seam, not `:phase` alone: `control-step` carries custody\n2032\tprogress and the established offer/report/release/closure brackets,\n2033\t`program-step` prepares and captures the export, `stop` latches the\n2034\tdriver, and `owed-control-write?` answers the control cadence. A\n2035\tcandidate that accepted a grant but has not lowered it stays in\n2036\t`:activating` and still stops at its own lease bound. The inbox\n2037\tretention machine and the unavailable-lane semantics are in 7.11.\n2038\t\n2039\t### 7.7.5 Fencing effects: epochs, operation ids, and what exactly-once costs\n2040\t\n2041\t`dao.lease.md` states it plainly: **the absence of a `:lapsed` fact is\n2042\tnever evidence of tenure.** The first draft's fencing — stamp effects with\n2043\tthe lease id, let consumers consult the medium and drop effects whose\n2044\tincarnation has a `:lapsed` record — fails in every direction the review\n2045\tnamed: a partitioned consumer accepts stale effects (it cannot see the\n2046\tlapse); a delayed legitimate effect is discarded after release; repeated\n2047\teffects within one lease share one incarnation and are indistinguishable; a\n2048\treplacement holder repeats an effect its predecessor committed; and apply\n2049\tids correlate requests with responses, they do not suppress duplicates.\n2050\tLapse-record filtering is correlation, not fencing. What UCF requires\n2051\tinstead:\n2052\t\n2053\t**Authority epochs, checked atomically with commitment.** The occurrence's\n2054\tledger carries an epoch datom, advanced by the grantor on every reclaim\n2055\t(7.7.3); only the possessing boundary writes it. Exactly-once admission\n2056\tis available only at an enrolled transactional consumer. Its epoch check,\n2057\toperation-intent check, durable dedup record, and side-effect commitment\n2058\tshare one atomic boundary against the admission resource, never a remote\n2059\tcheck followed by a local act. A stale epoch is refused at commitment.\n2060\tA consumer unable to join that boundary declares at-least-once delivery\n2061\tor fail-stop behavior; UCF does not infer exactly-once from a lease stamp.\n2062\t\n2063\t**Stable operation ids, intent, and durable dedup.** Every fenced effect\n2064\tcarries `:yin.k/op-id {:yin.k/occurrence O :yin.k/seq n}`, assigned in\n2065\temission order. Re-grant may reuse an id after a nondeterministic input\n2066\thas changed, such as a kept cursor returning `gap` after eviction. The\n2067\tsame id therefore does not prove the same operation. An enrolled consumer\n2068\tcompares the canonical intent (effect kind, target identity, and payload)\n2069\twith its durable `{op-id -> {intent result}}` record in the atomic\n2070\tadmission transition. An equal id and intent returns the recorded result\n2071\twithout a second commit; an equal id with different intent refuses and\n2072\tcommits nothing. A crash re-grant must replay durably recorded inputs or\n2073\tfail-stop when a divergent intent cannot be reconciled. Without that\n2074\tinput discipline and transactional enrollment, the composition declares\n2075\tat-least-once behavior rather than exactly-once effects.\n2076\t\n2077\t**Where the stamp rides.** Program values are program data; UCF never\n2078\tedits them. A resumed incarnation running under a lease emits through a\n2079\t**fenced writer** the composition wires: a writer that wraps each appended\n2080\tvalue in an envelope `{:yin.k/envelope ... :yin.k/incarnation <lease>\n2081\t:yin.k/op-id ... :yin.k/value <the program value, verbatim>}`. An enrolled\n2082\tconsumer derives intent from the actual effect kind, destination identity,\n2083\tand payload at admission. A bare stream is *declared* unprotected, and a\n2084\tcomposition that needs exactly-once delivery through it has wired the\n2085\twrong thing. Fencing is opt-in per stream; the resource enforces it.\n2086\tNo callback tells a lapsed holder to stop; it stops at\n2087\tits own lease bound (`dao.lease.md`, *The holder*), and what it did past\n2088\tthe bound is distinguishable after the fact by epoch and op-id.\n2089\tSection 7.7.8 publishes the envelope's grammar, which adds the epoch to\n2090\tthe keys named here, and the sequence state behind the operation id.\n2091\t\n2092\t**Durable completion of a checkpoint.** Publication of a successor is not\n2093\tcompletion. The holder's exit sequence is: append the successor value to\n2094\tthe carrier, append `:yin.k/resumed` (evidence) to the arbitration medium,\n2095\tappend `:dao.lease/released`. Completion is the **grantor's ledger\n2096\ttransition** -- a `:release` or `:policy` lapse of the current live lease\n2097\twith an accepted report atomically records, in order, the lapse,\n2098\toccurrence-completed, exact successor or terminal edge, and occurrence-reclaimed.\n2099\tThe occurrence must be unclosed and unquarantined, epoch advancement supported,\n2100\tand the successor unique. The actual lapse cause is retained: policy completion\n2101\tis not release carriage. The closure and edge require the matching lapse earlier\n2102\tin that same transaction; a report alone is not completion. A crash after an\n2103\taccepted report therefore completes on eligible policy reclaim during reopen.\n2104\tPublication without an accepted report leaves an orphan successor, not a grant;\n2105\tthe grantor may re-grant the last recorded occurrence. For enrolled consumers, durable input replay and\n2106\tatomic intent comparison prevent a divergent same-id effect from\n2107\tcommitting. Without those gates, the re-grant is at-least-once or\n2108\tfail-stop, not harmless exactly-once recovery. The orphan remains\n2109\tqueryable history (7.7.6); a holder report is evidence, while the grantor\n2110\tledger is authority.\n2111\t\n2112\t**Partition policy, stated honestly.** A fenced consumer that cannot reach\n2113\tthe authority (epoch unreadable) must **suspend protected effect\n2114\tcommitment** — fail closed — or the composition has chosen at-least-once\n2115\tdelivery with after-the-fact dedup reconciliation; which one is a declared\n2116\tproperty of the consumer, and a consumer that neither checks nor declares\n2117\tis not exactly-once and may not claim to be. A holder partitioned from the\n2118\tarbitration medium runs to its own lease bound and its effects are\n2119\tafter-the-fact distinguishable, which is the guarantee `dao.lease.md`\n2120\tactually makes; UCF does not promise more. A grantor that lost its ledger\n2121\treclaims and re-grants (`dao.lease.md`, *Restart*); **permanent** authority\n2122\tloss breaks exclusive custody irreparably — the ledger was the resource —\n2123\tand repair is a governance act outside this protocol. The arbitration\n2124\tmedium's durability is therefore a composition duty owed before\n2125\t`:yin.k/exclusive` is offered at all (`dao.lease.md`, *Composition\n2126\tduties*: a judge whose durability matches the resource).\n2127\t\n2128\t### 7.7.6 Successors and chains\n2129\t\n2130\tA resumed continuation runs to its next safepoint and either halts or parks\n2131\tagain. Either way it produces a successor value — a `:yin.k/continuation`\n2132\tor a `:yin.k/result` — whose `:yin.k/origin` carries the predecessor's\n2133\t`:yin.k/occurrence` and the lease it ran under. Each park is a **new\n2134\toccurrence**: minted fresh by the running holder, chained to its\n2135\tpredecessor, and itself the subject of the next custody cycle. The holder\n2136\tappends the successor, records `:yin.k/resumed` with the successor's\n2137\taddress, and releases. The chain of occurrences linked by `:yin.k/origin`\n2138\tis the migratory computation's history, queryable on the arbitration\n2139\tmedium: where it ran, under which grant, what it became. A rollback is a\n2140\tre-offer of an earlier link; effects past that link need compensation, not\n2141\trecomputation (`streams-all-the-way-down.md` §6.4) — and §7.7.5's op-ids\n2142\tare what compensation reads to find what was done.\n2143\t\n2144\t(Amendment r6.) The rollback above is not an automatic transition. A\n2145\tclosed occurrence never regains tenure and receives no further grant\n2146\t(7.7.8). Re-offering an earlier link, and compensating for effects\n2147\tpast it, is a governance act outside automatic successor admission.\n2148\t\n2149\t(M-next C, slice C8.) A halted result completes its occurrence as a\n2150\tcontinuation does, but the closure's edge is a terminal edge to the\n2151\tresult's address (7.7.2). A result is not an occurrence: it is never\n2152\toffered, admitted or granted, no ancestry runs through it, and the\n2153\tchain ends there.\n2154\t\n2155\t### 7.7.7 Restart\n2156\t\n2157\tA grantor that lost its ledger reclaims and re-grants (`dao.lease.md`,\n2158\t*Restart*: reclaim first, then grant afresh — a new grant alone does not\n2159\tend the prior tenure). Its inventory of possessed resources is the set of\n2160\toccurrence ledgers it transacts; its recoverable holder for each is the\n2161\t`:dao.lease/holder` of the last grant, both of which are facts on (or the\n2162\trecoverable state of) the space it possesses. Both are queries, which is\n2163\twhy custody lives on a `dao.space` and not on the carrier stream.\n2164\t\n2165\t(M-next C, slice C6.) An authority that reopens its durable ledger\n2166\truns these steps in order, and serves nothing before the last:\n2167\t\n2168\t1. Open the ledger and fold it.\n2169\t2. Reclaim every tenure it shows live, cause `:policy`, in grant\n2170\t   order, each lapse with its epoch change as one transaction; an eligible\n2171\t   accepted report also records closure and its exact edge in that transaction\n2172\t   (7.7.8). Nothing is regranted.\n2173\t3. Rebuild the judge from the ledger after those reclaims: its seen\n2174\t   facts from every recorded grant and lapse (a lapse of cause\n2175\t   `:release` is the release; no separate release is recorded), its\n2176\t   answered proposals from the recorded grants and refusals.\n2177\t4. Wire the proposal media from their oldest anchor, so a proposal\n2178\t   drained before its occurrence could be granted is judged again.\n2179\t\n2180\tA reclaim that does not commit refuses the whole open; reopen is\n2181\tretried, and replays the ledger to the same state. The reclaim is\n2182\tthe adapter contract of `dao.lease.md`, *Composition duties*.\n2183\tBecause reopen reclaims, a holder's admission retry after a crash\n2184\tanswers `:stale` (7.7.8 step 3); its committed result is recovered\n2185\tfrom the outcome projection (7.9). An authority opened without\n2186\treclaim replays the recorded result.\n2187\t\n2188\t(M-next C, slice C12.) Enrollment carries no request identity. After\n2189\tan uncertain answer the composition rereads the projection and\n2190\tenrolls only when the derived target identity is absent; a blind\n2191\tretry enrolls a second target.\n2192\t\n2193\t### 7.7.8 Fenced custody, version 1: sequence, envelope, epoch binding\n2194\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"23e67cb0-f183-4c2e-b3d6-2e5b6c44b814","uuid":"1d5d5abd-218a-43be-b668-e34c7f08f31f","timestamp":"2026-10-13T08:31:44.277Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7plan/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md","content":"## §7.7 Ownership arbitration — custody as a lease over datoms (blocker 4)\n\n### 7.7.1 The race, named\n\nThe emitter parks, lifts, appends the UCF value to a medium, and keeps its\nlocal `:parked` record. Three readers see the value. The emitter's own wait\nset wakes because the stream it was waiting on delivered. Now four machines\nbelieve they may continue one computation. Nothing in DaoStream can stop\nthem: a stream has no privileged reader, there is no destructive read, and\n\"the hard part of a take was never the removal but the *exclusion* of\ncompeting takers, which is a lease over datoms\" (`dao.stream.md`,\n*Explicitly Absent*).\n\nUCF adopts that sentence as its design. **Resumption is a take. A take is a\nwrite. Exclusion is a lease.** Nothing new is added to the stream contract,\nand no key is added to any result map.\n\n### 7.7.2 Custody facts, and their dispatch keys\n\nCustody is negotiated on an **arbitration medium** — a `dao.space` whose\nfacts are ordinary datoms — using `dao.lease.md`'s vocabulary unchanged,\nwith the checkpoint **occurrence** as the leased subject. The UCF-authored\nfacts are dispatch-keyed exactly as lease facts are (`dao.lease.md`,\n*Vocabulary*: a reader switches on `:dao.lease/status`; a fact carrying it\nis a lease fact, one carrying neither is ignored):\n\n| Fact | Dispatch | Author | Required keys |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| offer | `:yin.k/custody :yin.k/offered` | emitter | `:yin.k/occurrence`, `:yin.k/id` (this snapshot variant), `:yin.k/policy`, `:yin.k/medium` (the carrier identity) |\n| checkpoint done | `:yin.k/custody :yin.k/resumed` | holder | `:yin.k/occurrence`, `:dao.lease/lease`, `:yin.k/result` — the address of the successor's occurrence value or of a `:yin.k/result` |\n\nLease facts themselves keep their own table verbatim — proposal\n(`:dao.lease/proposed`, with `:dao.lease/subject = {:yin.k/occurrence O}`),\ngrant (`:dao.lease/accepted`, with `:dao.lease/lease :dao.lease/holder\n:dao.lease/subject :dao.lease/duration`, and `:dao.lease/max` optional),\nrefusal, release, renewal, lapse — each dispatched on `:dao.lease/status`\nor `:dao.lease/event`, each with `dao.lease.md`'s required keys, unchanged.\nA lease judge reading the same medium switches on the lease keys and\nignores the `:yin.k/custody` facts; a UCF reader switches on\n`:yin.k/custody` and reads lease facts for authority. Both `:yin.k/custody`\nfacts are **evidence, not authority**: an offer creates no claim, and a\n`:resumed` record is a holder's report — completion is the grantor's ledger\ntransition, not the report (§7.7.6). Authority is exactly `dao.lease.md`'s:\n**only grantor-authored facts establish terms.**\n\nThe grantor's own facts on that ledger (M-next C, slice C5), each\nwritten in the published attribute order of `yin.vm.ucf.ledger`:\n\n| Fact | Dispatch | Author | Keys, in order |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| admitted offer | `:yin.k/custody :yin.k/offered` | authority | `:yin.k/custody`, `:yin.k/occurrence`, `:yin.k/id`, `:yin.k/policy`, `:yin.k/medium`, `:yin.k/baseline` (the operation baseline the inspector derived from the variant's bytes) |\n| grant | `:dao.lease/status :dao.lease/accepted` | authority | `:dao.lease/status`, `:dao.lease/lease`, `:dao.lease/proposal`, `:dao.lease/subject`, `:dao.lease/holder`, `:dao.lease/duration`, `:dao.lease/max` (optional) |\n| grant binding | `:yin.k/custody :yin.k/bound` | authority | `:yin.k/custody`, `:yin.k/occurrence`, `:dao.lease/lease`, `:dao.lease/holder`, `:yin.k/epoch` (7.7.8) |\n\nThe admitted offer is not the emitter's offer: it shares the\ndispatch value but is authored by the authority and carries\n`:yin.k/baseline`, and it records one admitted snapshot variant. The\nemitter's offer stays evidence. A grant and its binding are one\ntransaction.\n\nSlices C6, C7 and C10 add these grantor facts, in the same order rule:\n\n| Fact | Dispatch | Author | Keys, in order |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| epoch change | `:yin.k/custody :yin.k/reclaimed` | authority | `:yin.k/custody`, `:yin.k/occurrence`, `:dao.lease/lease`, `:yin.k/epoch` (the occurrence's epoch after the reclaim, 7.7.8) |\n| refusal | `:yin.k/custody :yin.k/refused` | authority | `:yin.k/custody`, `:yin.k/proposer`, `:dao.lease/proposal` (7.7.8) |\n| fenced admission | `:yin.k/custody :yin.k/fenced` | authority | `:yin.k/custody`, `:yin.k/op-id`, `:yin.k/incarnation`, `:yin.k/epoch` (the lease and epoch that admitted the op id) |\n| quarantine | `:yin.k/custody :yin.k/quarantined` | authority | `:yin.k/custody`, `:yin.k/occurrence`, `:yin.k/op-id` (the op id whose intent conflicted) |\n| input | `:yin.k/custody :yin.k/input` | authority | `:yin.k/custody`, `:yin.k/occurrence`, `:dao.lease/lease`, `:yin.k/input-seq`, `:yin.k/source`, `:yin.k/observed` (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.2.2) |\n\nA lapse and its epoch change are one transaction, and so are the two\nhalves of a refusal (7.7.8). A fenced admission commits in one\ntransaction with its op id's dedup record; an intent conflict commits\nonly the quarantine.\n\nSlice C8's completion facts, as recorded by the authority:\n\n| Fact | Dispatch | Author | Keys, in order |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| recorded report | `:yin.k/custody :yin.k/resumed` | authority | `:yin.k/custody`, `:yin.k/occurrence`, `:dao.lease/lease`, `:yin.k/result`, `:yin.k/successor` (for a continuation only: the occurrence the authority verified) |\n| closure | `:yin.k/custody :yin.k/completed` | authority | `:yin.k/custody`, `:yin.k/occurrence`, `:dao.lease/lease` (the released lease) |\n| edge | `:yin.k/custody :yin.k/succeeded` | authority | `:yin.k/custody`, `:yin.k/occurrence`, then exactly one of `:yin.k/successor` (the successor edge) and `:yin.k/result` (the terminal edge to a halted result's address, 7.7.6) |\n\nThe recorded report is the holder's report after the authority\nverified it (7.7.8). It names no author: its author is the lease's\nholder, which the ledger already holds. It is still evidence;\ncompletion is the closure. A closure and its edge commit in the\ntransaction of the lease's `:release` lapse, between the lapse and\nits epoch change.\n\nThe subject of every grant is the **occurrence**, never `:yin.k/id`:\nsnapshot variants and retries of one park share one occurrence, so no pair\nof encodings can hold independent grants, and an equal-content copy minted\nby coincidence is a different occurrence and a different computation\n(§7.3.4).\n\n### 7.7.3 The authority: what the grantor possesses\n\n`dao.lease.md` is explicit that a judge must be composed **inside the\nboundary that possesses the resource** and must reclaim *by acting on what\nit itself holds*; a judge that possesses nothing is outside the contract.\nThe first draft designated \"the composition-designated arbiter for the\ncarrier medium,\" defaulting to the emitter — an arbiter relative to a\ncarrier, possessing an offer, which is a copy of content: the same\noccurrence carried on a second medium would acquire a second arbiter, and\nneither arbiter controls execution of anything. This section replaces that\nwith a grounded design.\n\n**The possessed resource is the occurrence's execution ledger**: the set of\ndatoms about one occurrence id on the arbitration `dao.space` — its offer,\ngrants, renewals, lapses, and resumed reports, plus the occurrence's\n**admission epoch** (§7.7.5). The **grantor is the boundary that transacts\nthat space** — its transactor. Possession is exact and of the right kind:\n\n- the ledger's datom base lives inside the transactor's boundary, and\n  admission to it is the transactor's alone — no other boundary can write\n  those datoms, which is why grantor-authored facts are worth more than\n  holder reports;\n- the **reclaim** is the grantor acting on what it holds: recording the\n  lapse on its own base and advancing the occurrence's epoch datom. It\n  needs no cooperation from any holder and no reach into any carrier;\n- the arbiter is **not medium-relative**: the binding\n  occurrence → arbitration space is minted at first export and travels *in\n  the value* (`:yin.k/arbitration`, §7.2), so every carrier, every snapshot\n  variant, and every copy names the same one authority. The same content on\n  two media does not create a second arbiter, because the authority is named\n  by the occurrence, not discovered from wherever a value happened to land;\n- **one holder per occurrence is the grantor's policy, enforced where\n  policy lives**: `dao.lease` does not impose it, and UCF does not add to\n  the vocabulary — the grantor's ledger admits at most one live lease per\n  occurrence (a second grant requires the prior lease reclaimed first,\n  which `dao.lease.md`'s own validity rules make the grantor the sole\n  author of). Exclusive custody is thus a property of the ledger the\n  grantor possesses, not an aspiration over streams it does not.\n\nWhat is possessed, concretely, is the *right to admit effects as this\noccurrence* — the ledger is where epochs and dedup-stamped admissions are\nrecorded, which is what §7.7.5's fencing consults. A composition that\ncannot wire a `dao.space` transactor as authority cannot offer\n`:yin.k/exclusive` custody at all; it composes `:yin.k/fork` only. That is\nan honest capability limit, not a gap to paper over.\n\n### 7.7.4 The exporting state: fencing the source before publication\n\nEmitter-as-candidate was the first draft's whole answer to the\nsource-wakeup race, and it does not close the publication window. The\nlifecycle published first and made the emitter a candidate afterward, but\nnothing ever removed the emitter's *local* eligibility: its driver may\nstill poll the wait set — and polling a blocked writer **performs the\nappend** (the engine, a `:put` entry retries `append!` on every\npoll) — may restore a woken entry from the ready queue, may satisfy a\ndirect `:resume`, and may retry a blocked write, all after the value is in\nother machines' hands. Delaying register restoration is too late; the\nappend is the effect.\n\nUCF therefore defines an explicit **exporting** transition that precedes\nany publication, and the lift driver performs it as one step over the\nmachine value:\n\n1. **Quiesce**: the ready-queue must be empty (§7.6.3).\n2. **Detach the wait set**: every wait entry moves from the machine's\n   wait-set into the export record. The machine's wait-set is now empty,\n   so *there is nothing to poll* — no poll, no append, no effect. The\n   entries are not lost: the export record retains them verbatim; they are\n   the source of the `:yin.k/pending` variants of §7.4.3.\n3. **Neutralize control**: the record's control and K are already absent\n   (the machine is parked or blocked); direct `:resume` resolves against\n   `:parked`, and the parked records referenced by reachable code travel\n   inside the export record — a local `:resume` against them is refused\n   while exporting (the restore function observes the exporting machine\n   and declines), because the parked id now names a checkpoint that may be\n   granted elsewhere.\n4. **Publish, then offer**: append segments and the value body; record\n   `:yin.k/offered` on the arbitration medium. Only now do readers exist,\n   and the source was fenced before any of them did.\n\nThe exporting machine is pure data with an empty wait-set and a retained\nexport record — no engine change, no callback, no timer; the drivers of the\nworld observe the state as they observe `ready-for-ingress?` (§3.1), and a\ndriver that polls an exporting machine's wait set anyway is a host assembly\ndefect of the same kind as wiring a refusing deposit destination.\n\n**Failure recovery and idempotent retry.** If publication fails — a full\ncarrier, an unreachable arbitration medium — the machine stays exporting\nand the composition retries; a retry re-encodes or reuses the encoded value\nand **reuses the occurrence** (§7.3.4), so no retry mints a second runnable\ncheckpoint. Abandoning the export reinstates the export record into the\nmachine (wait entries return to the wait-set, parked records to `:parked`)\nand the task resumes as a purely local one — the sole paths back to local\nexecution are *abort export* (before the offer is recorded) and *a grant to\nthe emitter itself* (after); there is no third.\n\n(M-next D8/D9, as built.) The exporting machine carries one gate key,\n`:yin.k/gate`, with the modes `:running`, `:exporting` and `:ended`:\nunder `:running` internal computation proceeds and stream observations\nand effects are deferred to the driver; under `:exporting` and `:ended`\nnothing is observed, no install child is advanced, a direct resume is\nrefused, and every public apply refuses a late result. The root's mode\nis stamped on each install child at its creation and before each run;\na child carries the mode only. A machine entering exporting refuses\nthe custody holds -- a wait entry with reason `:observe`, an entry or\ncursor cell carrying `:yin.k/held`, a cursor cell carrying `:yin.k/unminted`,\na machine with a nonempty `:yin.k/closes` queue, and a\n`:link-request` entry without `:cursor` -- as `:yin.k/non-portable`,\nkind `:reason-mismatch`, with the holding task's path; the lift\nrefuses the same holds on its own behalf. A task is not at a liftable\nsafepoint while it holds any of these.\n\nThe lift's custody input is one **header** argument -- supplied to\n`holder.export/prepare [machine record serve! header]`, stored in the\nexport record as `:header`, and passed to `export-task` through its\n`:header` option -- `nil` or:\n\n    {:yin.k/occurrence  O\n     :yin.k/arbitration {:dao.stream/identity i\n                         :dao.stream/descriptor d}\n     :yin.k/origin      {:yin.k/occurrence P\n                         :dao.lease/lease L\n                         :yin.k/emitter a}  ; absent on a first export\n     :yin.k/next-op-seq n\n     :yin.k/enrolled    #{stream-identity ...}}\n\nA `nil` header lifts today's version-0 fork; a header lifts version 1,\nexclusive, in the canonical codec, with `:yin.k/policy\n:yin.k/exclusive` added by the lift. The occurrence is minted once by\nthe driver and persisted before prepare is called; `:yin.k/enrolled`\nis the composition's declaration from the ledger reader; it is never\nencoded into the UCF body, but the header stays in the retained export\nrecord. The live record can contain authentic closures and is not itself\na canonical data object. `holder.export/freeze [machine prepared-record]`\nencodes a separate content-addressed export-recovery object, tagged\n`:yin.k/export-recovery` with format version 1, using the handoff value/cursor-cell\ncodec. It returns recovery bytes/address beside the exact handoff body\nbytes/address; both are stored durably before the journal's fenced event.\nRecovery retains the local enrolled declaration, prior gate, ordered waits,\nparked continuations, issue stamps, descriptors and dependency closure.\nThe enrolled declaration still never travels in a published handoff body.\n\n`holder.export/rehydrate-fenced` validates the recovery object and its\nembedded body before administrative attachment. It creates authentic\nfresh-receiver-owned values and preserves resource aliases and distinct\ncursor cells without program reads, cursor minting, writes, closes, initialization\nor execution. Every task stays exporting, with waits outside scheduler\nqueues. The prior gate is retained only as data; recovery is neither grant\nadmission nor permission to abort back into execution. Runtime composition\nfunctions and handles are the environment, not the canonical journal.\nIncomplete preparations cannot manufacture a missing snapshot on restart.\nRecovery also retains a complete codec snapshot when legacy fork bytes\nomit transitive dependencies; the published version-0 bytes stay unchanged.\nFor version 1, the module-store fixed point is a correctness repair:\ncanonical ordering uses a separate scratch census and did not populate\nthe emitted dependency table. Bodies that previously omitted transitive\nmodule stores now contain them and have different content addresses; this\nis not a new handoff version or closure format. The transitive-store test\npins the old one-pass body and corrected body bytes by their addresses.\n\nThe driver's exit persists recovery and body, journals the fence, attempts\nthe stable successor offer, reports that exact successor/body, carries\nrelease of the matching origin lease, observes the authoritative closure\nand successor edge, then retries the same offer until admitted. The\nrecovery object is stored first and a store that refuses stops the step\nbefore the body is attempted; a retry re-brackets from the recovery\nobject, idempotent through the store. An\n`:awaiting-completion` offer answer does not block reporting. A halt reports\nits result body and observes the terminal edge, with no successor offer.\nEvery external attempt has a durable intent before delivery and an\nacknowledgment afterward; an unmatched intent retains unknown delivery.\nA non-admitted report answer either retries -- `:suspended` resends the\nidentical request -- or ends the run (r3 1.6): the terminal refusals\ngate the machine `:ended`, append one diagnostic and carry the cleanup\nrelease, which closes nothing by itself. A legal abort journals\none terminal `:aborted` record before local execution is handed back,\nand the journal fold treats that record as closing the export. A holder\nrechecks tenure across that append -- dao.lease's own `holding?`, with\nthe grant's max cap and its stopped latches -- and tenure that died\nunder it ends the run instead of restoring the machine; the cleanup\nrelease opens with the next step, after the terminal record.\nReopened holders do not regain tenure from persisted clock readings:\nthey reconcile, release and re-enter candidacy, never resume execution.\n\nKind and\nheader agree at lift: blocked and parked roots carry occurrence,\narbitration, counter and policy, and origin when present; a halted\nroot carries origin only, and a header-bearing halt without an origin\nis refused -- it is not automatically downgraded (a halt with no\norigin at all is a version-0 result under a `nil` header); install\nchildren carry no header. Before answering `:ok`, a version-1 root\nlift runs `checkpoint/inspect` on its own bytes and address, then\n`handoff/validate-body` on the decoded body; install children do not\nindependently undergo the root custody inspection. Any refusal\nreturns as the lift's refusal and nothing is published. A lift under a\nheader refuses `:yin.k/non-portable`, kind `:op-seq-exhausted`, at the\n2^52-1 counter of a blocked or parked root (a halted root ignores that\nheader value), kind `:unprotected-pending` for a retained `:put`,\n`:ffi-request` or `:link-request` entry, in the root or an install\nchild, with no operation id whose target identity is enrolled, and\n`:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream for an operation id on an\nunenrolled target.\n\nAbort is permitted only before any possibly accepted offer attempt, or\nupon authoritative evidence that this occurrence has never been\nadmitted and cannot still become admitted from an outstanding attempt;\na refusal of one request is not such evidence. As implemented,\n`holder.export/abort` reads the composition's persisted `attempts`\nsequence -- each attempt's append outcome under `:append` -- and\naccepts abort only with no attempts or when every attempt's outcome is\nin the closed set `#{:dao.stream/full :dao.stream/invalid-value\n:dao.stream/closed :dao.stream/refused}`; `transport-error` and a\nmissing answer refuse. A holder exporting a successor may abort only\nunder current valid tenure, implemented as `{:now n :bound b :live\ntrue}` with `:live` supplied by ledger evidence that the lease is the\noccurrence's active lease at its epoch; at or past the bound, and\nwithout the evidence, abort is refused. After the lease has ended its\nold local machine is never restored.\n\n(M-next D15a, as built.) The driver steps this state through a\ncustody seam, not `:phase` alone: `control-step` carries custody\nprogress and the established offer/report/release/closure brackets,\n`program-step` prepares and captures the export, `stop` latches the\ndriver, and `owed-control-write?` answers the control cadence. A\ncandidate that accepted a grant but has not lowered it stays in\n`:activating` and still stops at its own lease bound. The inbox\nretention machine and the unavailable-lane semantics are in 7.11.\n\n### 7.7.5 Fencing effects: epochs, operation ids, and what exactly-once costs\n\n`dao.lease.md` states it plainly: **the absence of a `:lapsed` fact is\nnever evidence of tenure.** The first draft's fencing — stamp effects with\nthe lease id, let consumers consult the medium and drop effects whose\nincarnation has a `:lapsed` record — fails in every direction the review\nnamed: a partitioned consumer accepts stale effects (it cannot see the\nlapse); a delayed legitimate effect is discarded after release; repeated\neffects within one lease share one incarnation and are indistinguishable; a\nreplacement holder repeats an effect its predecessor committed; and apply\nids correlate requests with responses, they do not suppress duplicates.\nLapse-record filtering is correlation, not fencing. What UCF requires\ninstead:\n\n**Authority epochs, checked atomically with commitment.** The occurrence's\nledger carries an epoch datom, advanced by the grantor on every reclaim\n(7.7.3); only the possessing boundary writes it. Exactly-once admission\nis available only at an enrolled transactional consumer. Its epoch check,\noperation-intent check, durable dedup record, and side-effect commitment\nshare one atomic boundary against the admission resource, never a remote\ncheck followed by a local act. A stale epoch is refused at commitment.\nA consumer unable to join that boundary declares at-least-once delivery\nor fail-stop behavior; UCF does not infer exactly-once from a lease stamp.\n\n**Stable operation ids, intent, and durable dedup.** Every fenced effect\ncarries `:yin.k/op-id {:yin.k/occurrence O :yin.k/seq n}`, assigned in\nemission order. Re-grant may reuse an id after a nondeterministic input\nhas changed, such as a kept cursor returning `gap` after eviction. The\nsame id therefore does not prove the same operation. An enrolled consumer\ncompares the canonical intent (effect kind, target identity, and payload)\nwith its durable `{op-id -> {intent result}}` record in the atomic\nadmission transition. An equal id and intent returns the recorded result\nwithout a second commit; an equal id with different intent refuses and\ncommits nothing. A crash re-grant must replay durably recorded inputs or\nfail-stop when a divergent intent cannot be reconciled. Without that\ninput discipline and transactional enrollment, the composition declares\nat-least-once behavior rather than exactly-once effects.\n\n**Where the stamp rides.** Program values are program data; UCF never\nedits them. A resumed incarnation running under a lease emits through a\n**fenced writer** the composition wires: a writer that wraps each appended\nvalue in an envelope `{:yin.k/envelope ... :yin.k/incarnation <lease>\n:yin.k/op-id ... :yin.k/value <the program value, verbatim>}`. An enrolled\nconsumer derives intent from the actual effect kind, destination identity,\nand payload at admission. A bare stream is *declared* unprotected, and a\ncomposition that needs exactly-once delivery through it has wired the\nwrong thing. Fencing is opt-in per stream; the resource enforces it.\nNo callback tells a lapsed holder to stop; it stops at\nits own lease bound (`dao.lease.md`, *The holder*), and what it did past\nthe bound is distinguishable after the fact by epoch and op-id.\nSection 7.7.8 publishes the envelope's grammar, which adds the epoch to\nthe keys named here, and the sequence state behind the operation id.\n\n**Durable completion of a checkpoint.** Publication of a successor is not\ncompletion. The holder's exit sequence is: append the successor value to\nthe carrier, append `:yin.k/resumed` (evidence) to the arbitration medium,\nappend `:dao.lease/released`. Completion is the **grantor's ledger\ntransition** -- a `:release` or `:policy` lapse of the current live lease\nwith an accepted report atomically records, in order, the lapse,\noccurrence-completed, exact successor or terminal edge, and occurrence-reclaimed.\nThe occurrence must be unclosed and unquarantined, epoch advancement supported,\nand the successor unique. The actual lapse cause is retained: policy completion\nis not release carriage. The closure and edge require the matching lapse earlier\nin that same transaction; a report alone is not completion. A crash after an\naccepted report therefore completes on eligible policy reclaim during reopen.\nPublication without an accepted report leaves an orphan successor, not a grant;\nthe grantor may re-grant the last recorded occurrence. For enrolled consumers, durable input replay and\natomic intent comparison prevent a divergent same-id effect from\ncommitting. Without those gates, the re-grant is at-least-once or\nfail-stop, not harmless exactly-once recovery. The orphan remains\nqueryable history (7.7.6); a holder report is evidence, while the grantor\nledger is authority.\n\n**Partition policy, stated honestly.** A fenced consumer that cannot reach\nthe authority (epoch unreadable) must **suspend protected effect\ncommitment** — fail closed — or the composition has chosen at-least-once\ndelivery with after-the-fact dedup reconciliation; which one is a declared\nproperty of the consumer, and a consumer that neither checks nor declares\nis not exactly-once and may not claim to be. A holder partitioned from the\narbitration medium runs to its own lease bound and its effects are\nafter-the-fact distinguishable, which is the guarantee `dao.lease.md`\nactually makes; UCF does not promise more. A grantor that lost its ledger\nreclaims and re-grants (`dao.lease.md`, *Restart*); **permanent** authority\nloss breaks exclusive custody irreparably — the ledger was the resource —\nand repair is a governance act outside this protocol. The arbitration\nmedium's durability is therefore a composition duty owed before\n`:yin.k/exclusive` is offered at all (`dao.lease.md`, *Composition\nduties*: a judge whose durability matches the resource).\n\n### 7.7.6 Successors and chains\n\nA resumed continuation runs to its next safepoint and either halts or parks\nagain. Either way it produces a successor value — a `:yin.k/continuation`\nor a `:yin.k/result` — whose `:yin.k/origin` carries the predecessor's\n`:yin.k/occurrence` and the lease it ran under. Each park is a **new\noccurrence**: minted fresh by the running holder, chained to its\npredecessor, and itself the subject of the next custody cycle. The holder\nappends the successor, records `:yin.k/resumed` with the successor's\naddress, and releases. The chain of occurrences linked by `:yin.k/origin`\nis the migratory computation's history, queryable on the arbitration\nmedium: where it ran, under which grant, what it became. A rollback is a\nre-offer of an earlier link; effects past that link need compensation, not\nrecomputation (`streams-all-the-way-down.md` §6.4) — and §7.7.5's op-ids\nare what compensation reads to find what was done.\n\n(Amendment r6.) The rollback above is not an automatic transition. A\nclosed occurrence never regains tenure and receives no further grant\n(7.7.8). Re-offering an earlier link, and compensating for effects\npast it, is a governance act outside automatic successor admission.\n\n(M-next C, slice C8.) A halted result completes its occurrence as a\ncontinuation does, but the closure's edge is a terminal edge to the\nresult's address (7.7.2). A result is not an occurrence: it is never\noffered, admitted or granted, no ancestry runs through it, and the\nchain ends there.\n\n### 7.7.7 Restart\n\nA grantor that lost its ledger reclaims and re-grants (`dao.lease.md`,\n*Restart*: reclaim first, then grant afresh — a new grant alone does not\nend the prior tenure). Its inventory of possessed resources is the set of\noccurrence ledgers it transacts; its recoverable holder for each is the\n`:dao.lease/holder` of the last grant, both of which are facts on (or the\nrecoverable state of) the space it possesses. Both are queries, which is\nwhy custody lives on a `dao.space` and not on the carrier stream.\n\n(M-next C, slice C6.) An authority that reopens its durable ledger\nruns these steps in order, and serves nothing before the last:\n\n1. Open the ledger and fold it.\n2. Reclaim every tenure it shows live, cause `:policy`, in grant\n   order, each lapse with its epoch change as one transaction; an eligible\n   accepted report also records closure and its exact edge in that transaction\n   (7.7.8). Nothing is regranted.\n3. Rebuild the judge from the ledger after those reclaims: its seen\n   facts from every recorded grant and lapse (a lapse of cause\n   `:release` is the release; no separate release is recorded), its\n   answered proposals from the recorded grants and refusals.\n4. Wire the proposal media from their oldest anchor, so a proposal\n   drained before its occurrence could be granted is judged again.\n\nA reclaim that does not commit refuses the whole open; reopen is\nretried, and replays the ledger to the same state. The reclaim is\nthe adapter contract of `dao.lease.md`, *Composition duties*.\nBecause reopen reclaims, a holder's admission retry after a crash\nanswers `:stale` (7.7.8 step 3); its committed result is recovered\nfrom the outcome projection (7.9). An authority opened without\nreclaim replays the recorded result.\n\n(M-next C, slice C12.) Enrollment carries no request identity. After\nan uncertain answer the composition rereads the projection and\nenrolls only when the derived target identity is absent; a blind\nretry enrolls a second target.\n\n### 7.7.8 Fenced custody, version 1: sequence, envelope, epoch binding\n","numLines":480,"startLine":1715,"totalLines":3333}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01CgfihKxZ5mHPJUxN4dJ8Bp","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011Cfyvc12xx9JuxkFtDRGqk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01AcwRJ1t6PxWQXDF9tGo2f3","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7plan/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md","offset":2193,"limit":400},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":18705,"cache_read_input_tokens":56271,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":18705},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"23e67cb0-f183-4c2e-b3d6-2e5b6c44b814","uuid":"d564b6ca-4d1f-4147-a18e-e28f89186592","timestamp":"2026-10-13T08:31:44.693Z","request_id":"req_011CfyvbzkbqiVjAFmkGJrMN","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01AcwRJ1t6PxWQXDF9tGo2f3":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7plan/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md","offset":2193,"limit":400}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AcwRJ1t6PxWQXDF9tGo2f3","type":"tool_result","content":"2193\t### 7.7.8 Fenced custody, version 1: sequence, envelope, epoch binding\n2194\t\n2195\t(Amendment r5, 2026-10-04; `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.3 item 2.) This\n2196\tsection publishes the grammar that 7.7.5 argues for. The reasons stay\n2197\tin 7.7.5; the runtime, ledger, and step contract stay in\n2198\t`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.2. Nothing here adds a key to the lease\n2199\tvocabulary or to a DaoStream outcome map. Every rule applies to\n2200\tversion-1 bodies only (7.2.1).\n2201\t\n2202\t**Exact integers.** `:yin.k/epoch`, `:yin.k/seq` and\n2203\t`:yin.k/next-op-seq` are nonnegative exact integers no greater than\n2204\t2^52-1 (4503599627370495), the bound within which every host compares\n2205\tand increments exactly (`dao.lease.md`, *Keys*, uses the same one).\n2206\tThe kind is the canonical codec's integer kind: a float is not an\n2207\tinteger even when it is integral. A value outside the range or of\n2208\tanother kind makes its carrier defective: a body is\n2209\t`:yin.k/undecodable`, an envelope or a binding establishes nothing.\n2210\tNo counter wraps, saturates silently, or restarts.\n2211\t\n2212\t**Operation sequence state.** A task under a grant holds one counter,\n2213\t`:yin.k/next-op-seq`, on its root. Install children draw from it.\n2214\t\n2215\t1. *Assign.* Before the first attempt of a write to an enrolled\n2216\t   stream, the fenced writer takes `n`, the counter's value, forms\n2217\t   the id `{:yin.k/occurrence P :yin.k/seq n}` with `P` the\n2218\t   occurrence its grant names, stores the id on the wait entry, and\n2219\t   sets the counter to `n+1`. The three happen as one step over the\n2220\t   machine value, before any append.\n2221\t2. *Retain.* The id stays on the entry while the write is unfinished:\n2222\t   a `full` path, an append of unknown effect, an acceptance with no\n2223\t   admission outcome yet, or a `:suspended` outcome. A retry reuses\n2224\t   the id and never takes another.\n2225\t3. *Carry.* At a park the id travels in the pending (7.4.3) and the\n2226\t   counter in the header (7.2.1). The successor's counter is the\n2227\t   holder's counter at that park, so the sequence continues along\n2228\t   the chain and never restarts at a new occurrence.\n2229\t4. *Restore.* Lower sets the fresh task's counter to the carried\n2230\t   value exactly. There is no local value to take a maximum with:\n2231\t   the receiving task is new (contrast `:yin.k/id-counter`, 7.6.3).\n2232\t5. *Regrant.* A holder granted the same checkpoint again starts from\n2233\t   the same bytes, so from the same counter and the same carried\n2234\t   ids. Re-executed writes take the same sequence numbers in the\n2235\t   same order as long as its inputs replay; where they do not, the\n2236\t   intent comparison below catches the divergence.\n2237\t6. *Exhaust.* The largest assignable sequence is 2^52-2. A counter\n2238\t   equal to 2^52-1 is exhausted: the writer assigns nothing and\n2239\t   appends nothing, the write stays an undischarged wait, and a lift\n2240\t   of that task refuses `:yin.k/non-portable`, kind\n2241\t   `:op-seq-exhausted`. Recovery is outside this protocol.\n2242\t\n2243\t**The fenced envelope.** A fenced writer wraps each value it appends\n2244\ttoward an enrolled consumer:\n2245\t\n2246\t```clojure\n2247\t{:yin.k/envelope    :yin.k/fenced-v1   ; dispatch key; closed\n2248\t :yin.k/incarnation L                  ; the writer's :dao.lease/lease\n2249\t :yin.k/epoch       e                  ; from L's grant binding\n2250\t :yin.k/op-id       {:yin.k/occurrence P :yin.k/seq n}\n2251\t :yin.k/value       v}                 ; the program value, verbatim\n2252\t```\n2253\t\n2254\t- All five keys are required. `:yin.k/op-id` has exactly its two\n2255\t  keys. A consumer ignores other qualified keys, and they are never\n2256\t  part of intent. An envelope that fails these rules, or dispatches\n2257\t  on a value other than `:yin.k/fenced-v1`, is defective (below).\n2258\t- **Inside program data: only `:yin.k/value`.** It is the value the\n2259\t  program wrote, unchanged and uninterpreted. A program value that\n2260\t  looks like an envelope is payload: it is wrapped like any other\n2261\t  and never read as one.\n2262\t- **Outside program data: everything else.** The envelope exists\n2263\t  between the fenced writer and the enrolled boundary and nowhere\n2264\t  else. It is never in a frame, an environment, a store, or a body;\n2265\t  a pending carries the retained value and the id beside it, and\n2266\t  the writer rebuilds the envelope at each retry from the id and\n2267\t  its current binding. What the consumer's commit makes visible\n2268\t  downstream is the consumer's contract; UCF requires only that the\n2269\t  payload it commits is `:yin.k/value`.\n2270\t- The envelope names no target, no effect kind, and no intent. The\n2271\t  consumer takes the target identity and the effect kind from the\n2272\t  boundary the envelope arrived at, and computes intent itself.\n2273\t- The envelope carries no credential. The holder is authenticated\n2274\t  by the attribution the composition supplies (`dao.lease.md`,\n2275\t  *Composition duties*). An envelope whose attributed author is not\n2276\t  the holder that the binding of `L` names is a fact about that\n2277\t  author: it is defective.\n2278\t\n2279\t**Defective envelopes.** A defective envelope commits no effect and\n2280\tcreates no dedup record. It yields no admission outcome: an outcome\n2281\twould be attributed to the holder its fields claim, and those fields\n2282\tare exactly what cannot be trusted. The consumer instead appends one\n2283\tstructured diagnostic to the diagnostic stream the composition\n2284\tsupplies:\n2285\t\n2286\t```clojure\n2287\t{:yin.k/diagnostic :yin.k/defective-envelope\n2288\t :yin.k/defect     :malformed       ; closed, below\n2289\t :yin.k/target     i                ; the boundary's stream identity\n2290\t :yin.k/author     a                ; resolved attribution, if any\n2291\t :yin.k/claimed    {...}}           ; readable envelope fields\n2292\t```\n2293\t\n2294\t- The closed defect set is `:malformed` for invalid envelope\n2295\t  structure, unsupported envelope dispatch or version, invalid\n2296\t  numeric fields, or intent that cannot be canonically encoded;\n2297\t  `:unbound-lease` when no valid binding establishes the claimed\n2298\t  incarnation; `:wrong-author` when attribution is absent, invalid,\n2299\t  or does not identify the bound holder; and `:foreign-op-id` when\n2300\t  authoritative scope validation fails.\n2301\t- (M-next C, slice C7.) An op id whose `:yin.k/seq` is 2^52-1, a\n2302\t  value no writer can assign, is `:malformed`.\n2303\t- Enrollment belongs to the target boundary, while a writer's\n2304\t  authorization comes from attribution and binding. There is no\n2305\t  \"unenrolled writer\" defect: an unenrolled boundary cannot run\n2306\t  this admission protocol or claim its guarantee, so it produces\n2307\t  neither outcomes nor these diagnostics.\n2308\t- Readers dispatch on the `:yin.k/diagnostic` key, never on the\n2309\t  presence of an id. The diagnostic adds nothing to a lease fact or\n2310\t  to a DaoStream outcome map.\n2311\t- The composition supplies the diagnostic stream explicitly.\n2312\t  Failure or backpressure while publishing a diagnostic never\n2313\t  permits the rejected effect or creates an admission outcome;\n2314\t  diagnostic publication failure remains an explicit driver\n2315\t  outcome.\n2316\t- `:yin.k/claimed` holds whichever of `:yin.k/incarnation`,\n2317\t  `:yin.k/epoch` and `:yin.k/op-id` could be read, as claims. They\n2318\t  are nested so that no reader correlating on a top-level op id can\n2319\t  take the diagnostic for an answer. The payload is never echoed.\n2320\t- The map carries neither `:yin.k/admission` nor `:yin.k/status`. A\n2321\t  driver discharges nothing and ends nothing on a diagnostic.\n2322\t\n2323\t**Intent.** Intent is the vector `[effect-kind target-identity\n2324\tpayload]`: the keyword naming the boundary's operation, the target's\n2325\t`:dao.stream/identity`, and the envelope's `:yin.k/value`. This\n2326\tamendment enrolls one effect kind, `:yin.k/append`, a stream append;\n2327\tevery write the reference machine retains is one. Two intents are\n2328\tequal when their canonical bytes are equal. FFI and link correlation\n2329\tids are inside the payload and are compared with it; they are not\n2330\toperation ids.\n2331\t\n2332\t**The grant epoch binding.** The grantor publishes one more custody\n2333\tfact (7.7.2), authored by itself:\n2334\t\n2335\t```clojure\n2336\t{:yin.k/custody    :yin.k/bound\n2337\t :yin.k/occurrence O\n2338\t :dao.lease/lease  L\n2339\t :dao.lease/holder H\n2340\t :yin.k/epoch      e}\n2341\t```\n2342\t\n2343\t- The lease fact is unchanged. The binding is a separate fact, so a\n2344\t  lease judge, which switches on the lease keys, ignores it.\n2345\t- Unlike the offer and the resumed report, the binding is authority,\n2346\t  because the grantor authors it. It is valid only when its\n2347\t  attributed author is the transactor of the space the body's\n2348\t  `:yin.k/arbitration` names; when it was admitted in the same\n2349\t  transaction as a `:dao.lease/accepted` fact with the same lease,\n2350\t  the same holder, and the subject `{:yin.k/occurrence O}`; and when\n2351\t  it is the only binding for `L`. Any other binding establishes\n2352\t  nothing, and a grant on an occurrence with no valid binding\n2353\t  confers no fenced tenure.\n2354\t- (M-next C, slice C5.) *The only binding for `L`* means the only one\n2355\t  in the named authority's transaction history. A reader holding a\n2356\t  partial view can refute it, by finding a second binding, but never\n2357\t  establish it. In this composition the reader's records are the\n2358\t  authority's complete-retention journal read from its\n2359\t  `:dao.stream/oldest` anchor, so the check is completeness-backed,\n2360\t  and the authority's fold, which refuses a second binding, enforces\n2361\t  it. The author of records read from a stream whose\n2362\t  `:dao.stream/identity` equals the body's arbitration identity is\n2363\t  that identity. Descriptors are transport-relative and are never\n2364\t  compared.\n2365\t- A reader needs authenticated evidence that the grant and the\n2366\t  binding share one transaction of that authority: a transaction\n2367\t  identity within the named authority's provenance domain,\n2368\t  attributed to it. Equal `t` values read off arbitrary media prove\n2369\t  nothing. M-next C supplies the evidence and shows that a remote\n2370\t  reflection preserves it; a medium that cannot supply it cannot\n2371\t  offer `:yin.k/exclusive`.\n2372\t- **How a reader learns the epoch.** Only from a valid binding. A\n2373\t  holder reads it beside its grant before it activates, and stamps\n2374\t  it into its envelopes. A consumer reads the authority's current\n2375\t  state inside its admission transition. An epoch a holder\n2376\t  advertises, in an envelope or anywhere else, is a claim to be\n2377\t  checked, never a source.\n2378\t- **The counter.** Each occurrence has one epoch in its ledger\n2379\t  (7.7.3). It is 0 when the occurrence is first admitted. Every\n2380\t  reclaim of a lease on the occurrence, whatever its cause, raises\n2381\t  it by exactly one in the transaction that records the\n2382\t  `:dao.lease/lapsed` fact, before any new grant. A grant binds the\n2383\t  epoch current at that grant, so the first grant binds 0 and the\n2384\t  grant after `k` reclaims binds `k`. A successor is a new\n2385\t  occurrence and starts at 0.\n2386\t- (M-next C, slice C6.) **The epoch change** is the grantor's fact\n2387\t\n2388\t  ```clojure\n2389\t  {:yin.k/custody    :yin.k/reclaimed\n2390\t   :yin.k/occurrence O\n2391\t   :dao.lease/lease  L\n2392\t   :yin.k/epoch      e}             ; the epoch after the reclaim\n2393\t  ```\n2394\t\n2395\t  committed in the transaction that records `L`'s\n2396\t  `:dao.lease/lapsed` fact. Every lapse has exactly one, and the\n2397\t  ledger refuses a lapse without it or it without its lapse. At the\n2398\t  bound it is still written, carrying the unchanged epoch.\n2399\t- (M-next C, slice C6.) **A refusal** is a pair committed in one\n2400\t  transaction: the plain `:dao.lease/rejected` lease fact, verbatim\n2401\t  `dao.lease` (it names no proposer), and the grantor's\n2402\t\n2403\t  ```clojure\n2404\t  {:yin.k/custody      :yin.k/refused\n2405\t   :yin.k/proposer     P\n2406\t   :dao.lease/proposal pid}\n2407\t  ```\n2408\t\n2409\t  A lease judge switches on the lease keys and ignores the refused\n2410\t  fact; a reader learns which proposal a refusal answered, keyed\n2411\t  `[proposer proposal-id]`, from the refused fact alone. The ledger\n2412\t  refuses either half\n2413\t  without the other. A proposal id is answered at most once, so a\n2414\t  candidate refused, or answered `:yin.k/not-holder` (7.8), proposes\n2415\t  again with a fresh proposal id.\n2416\t- **Restart.** The epoch never decreases, is never reset, and is\n2417\t  never reused. A grantor that restarts recovers the epoch from its\n2418\t  durable ledger and reclaims, which raises it, before it grants\n2419\t  (7.7.7). A grantor that cannot recover the epoch grants nothing\n2420\t  for that occurrence: 7.7.7's reclaim-and-regrant presumes the\n2421\t  epoch survived. A new authority never starts an old occurrence at\n2422\t  0. Recovery from lost epoch or dedup state is governance, outside\n2423\t  this protocol (7.7.5). (M-next C, slice C6.) Reopen runs 7.7.7's\n2424\t  steps, rebuilding the judge after the reclaims: a reclaim that does\n2425\t  not commit refuses the open, and reopen is retried.\n2426\t- **Exhaustion.** Epoch 2^52-1 is usable until a reclaim would\n2427\t  increment it: a grant bound at that value is valid, and its\n2428\t  holder's effects are admitted like any other's. That reclaim ends\n2429\t  the tenure, leaves the epoch unchanged, and permanently exhausts\n2430\t  the occurrence. Thereafter admission returns `:suspended` before\n2431\t  tenure checking, and no effect commits. Exhaustion is derived\n2432\t  from the ledger: a lapse recorded for the lease bound at 2^52-1.\n2433\t  An exhausted tenure cannot produce an eligible exclusive\n2434\t  successor: the authority accepts no completion and grants nothing\n2435\t  for the occurrence. Carrier bytes may remain as history, but a\n2436\t  publication cannot reopen custody. (M-next C, slices C6 and C8.)\n2437\t  In the ledger, that lapse's epoch change carries the lease's own\n2438\t  binding epoch, which only a lease bound at 2^52-1 can do; that is\n2439\t  the exhaustion. A report recorded at the bound is evidence: its\n2440\t  release exhausts the occurrence without completing it.\n2441\t- **Why a binding.** A remote holder cannot infer authoritative\n2442\t  tenure from what it observes: `:dao.lease/lapsed` does not cross\n2443\t  to it (`dao.lease.md`, *Carriage*), and its view of the ledger is\n2444\t  partial. The binding publishes the grant's authoritative epoch.\n2445\t\n2446\t**Admission.** An enrolled consumer admits each well-formed envelope\n2447\tby one atomic transition against the authority's admission resource\n2448\t(7.7.5). The checks run in this order and the first to fail decides\n2449\tthe outcome (7.9):\n2450\t\n2451\t1. *Authority.* The authority the consumer is enrolled with must be\n2452\t   readable. If it is unreachable, if its ledger for the occurrence\n2453\t   that `L` is bound to is not recoverable, or if that occurrence\n2454\t   is exhausted, the outcome is `:suspended`. An occurrence\n2455\t   quarantined after an intent conflict (below) answers the same.\n2456\t2. *Binding.* `L` has a valid binding, and the envelope's attributed\n2457\t   author is the holder it names. Otherwise the envelope is\n2458\t   defective: a diagnostic, and no outcome.\n2459\t3. *Tenure.* The bound occurrence is open, `L` is its active lease,\n2460\t   and `e` equals its current epoch, else `:stale`. Staleness is\n2461\t   decided before the dedup record is consulted: a stale envelope is\n2462\t   refused even when a record for its id exists.\n2463\t4. *Scope.* An operation assigned during the current occurrence\n2464\t   names that occurrence. An inherited operation must occur among\n2465\t   the retained pending operations of the authoritative checkpoint\n2466\t   granted to the holder, including its install children, and its\n2467\t   occurrence must be an ancestor through authoritative completion\n2468\t   records. Membership is derived from the accepted checkpoint, the\n2469\t   body the authority admitted for the granted occurrence; no\n2470\t   ancestry index and no pending-id index is stored. An id that\n2471\t   fails is defective, `:foreign-op-id`. For an inherited id, if\n2472\t   the authoritative checkpoint cannot be read and verified,\n2473\t   admission answers `:suspended` without changing tenure,\n2474\t   quarantine, or dedup state. Unavailable evidence is not evidence\n2475\t   of an invalid id, and this suspension comes after the tenure\n2476\t   check, so a stale holder is still answered `:stale`. The\n2477\t   checkpoint is selected by the authority's accepted record, never\n2478\t   by an envelope-supplied location. Current-occurrence ids require\n2479\t   no inherited-membership lookup. Keeping the accepted checkpoint\n2480\t   readable is a durability duty of the composition. (M-next C,\n2481\t   slice C9.) Ancestry is checked first, from the completion records\n2482\t   alone, before the checkpoint is read. A granted occurrence's\n2483\t   ancestors never change, so a non-ancestor is `:foreign-op-id`\n2484\t   permanently; only membership waits on a store that can be\n2485\t   repaired. Any offered variant whose bytes verify to the recorded\n2486\t   baseline is the checkpoint, because the variants share one\n2487\t   baseline. A store that is absent, closed or throws is unreadable.\n2488\t5. *Dedup.* With a record for the id: equal intent answers\n2489\t   `:replayed` with the recorded result and commits nothing;\n2490\t   different intent answers `:intent-conflict` and commits nothing.\n2491\t   With no record: the effect, its result, and the record\n2492\t   `{op-id -> {intent result}}` commit together, and the outcome is\n2493\t   `:committed`.\n2494\t\n2495\t**Results and uncertainty.** The recorded result is the effect's\n2496\tdefinitive outcome, as plain data; for a stream append it is the\n2497\ttarget's DaoStream outcome map, unchanged. A terminal refusal is a\n2498\tresult, recorded and replayed unchanged, but only when the atomic\n2499\tadmission boundary itself establishes it. An outcome that leaves the\n2500\teffect unknown, such as a `:dao.stream/transport-error` from a\n2501\ttransport whose failures are not declared clean, is not a result.\n2502\tUnknown transport acceptance establishes neither commitment nor\n2503\tabsence of commitment. The writer retains the id and retries through\n2504\tthe fenced admission boundary. The authority may already hold a\n2505\tcommitted result, which the retry must replay. An external effect\n2506\twhose uncertainty cannot be reconciled within that atomic boundary\n2507\tis outside this guarantee. Backpressure, by contrast, is no\n2508\tcommitment: it is the writer path's own `:dao.stream/full`, and\n2509\tnothing was appended.\n2510\t\n2511\t**Snapshot variants.** Snapshot variants of one occurrence must\n2512\tpreserve its operation baseline: the root next-operation sequence\n2513\tand the mapping of retained operation ids to canonical intents,\n2514\tincluding child operations. Equal id sets alone are not enough: a\n2515\tvariant could keep an id and substitute its payload. The authority\n2516\trefuses a conflicting variant; an unavailable comparison suspends\n2517\tvariant admission. (M-next C, slice C5.) The authority admits an\n2518\toffer in this order: inspect the bytes; under the authority lock,\n2519\tdecide purely; store the body only when the decision will commit;\n2520\tcommit. A refused, replayed or uninspectable offer stores nothing. A\n2521\tstore that fails answers `:suspended` and commits nothing; a body\n2522\tstored before a commit that then fails is a harmless orphan.\n2523\t(M-next C, slice C9.) These mechanics realize the suspension above:\n2524\tthe comparison's basis, the recorded baseline, is always in the open\n2525\tauthority's projection, so the only unavailable comparison is that\n2526\tfailing store.\n2527\t\n2528\t**One dedup namespace.** The dedup records form one logical namespace\n2529\tper arbitration admission resource, across every enrolled target that\n2530\ttakes part in the guarantee, keyed by the operation id alone. Target\n2531\tidentity is part of the intent, never of the key. So a replay that\n2532\tdiverges and sends an id to another target meets the first target's\n2533\trecord and answers `:intent-conflict`. A consumer that cannot join\n2534\tthat atomic resource keeps no private table in its place: it cannot\n2535\tclaim the cross-target guarantee, and its stream is not enrolled.\n2536\t\n2537\t**Enrollment.** That a target stream is enrolled is an attributed\n2538\tfact of the authority on the arbitration medium, keyed by the target\n2539\tstream's identity. No body carries it. M-next C defines its schema\n2540\tand ensures that a change of enrollment cannot alter the protection\n2541\tof an operation already retained under an id.\n2542\t\n2543\t**Completion and the successor chain.** Completion (7.7.5) establishes\n2544\ta single acyclic chain. The authority accepts at most one successor\n2545\tper predecessor and one predecessor per successor. The successor's\n2546\toccurrence is fresh, its `:yin.k/origin` names the predecessor and\n2547\tthe lease that ran it, and its `:yin.k/arbitration` identity is the\n2548\tpredecessor's, unchanged. Only that accepted completion is an edge:\n2549\ta published successor or a resumed report the authority did not\n2550\taccept is an orphan, and no ancestry runs through it. A closed\n2551\toccurrence receives no further grant.\n2552\t\n2553\t(M-next C, slice C8.) The first offer of a body with an origin is\n2554\trefused `:awaiting-completion` while the origin's lease is its\n2555\toccurrence's live lease. Otherwise it is refused `:orphan` unless it\n2556\tis the successor the predecessor's closure recorded, under the\n2557\tclosing lease, at the reported address. An origin naming an\n2558\toccurrence this ledger never saw is `:orphan`: a successor's\n2559\tarbitration is its predecessor's, so no predecessor lives on another\n2560\tauthority. A body with no origin is a first export and is\n2561\tunconstrained. A halted result completes through a terminal edge\n2562\t(7.7.6).\n2563\t\n2564\t**Quarantine.** An intent conflict that the authority itself\n2565\testablished, in an authenticated admission, quarantines the\n2566\toccurrence: it stays open, is never regranted automatically, and its\n2567\tadmissions answer `:suspended`. Recovery or compensation is a\n2568\tgovernance decision outside this protocol. An unauthenticated claim\n2569\tof a conflict quarantines nothing. (M-next C, slice C8.) A\n2570\tquarantined occurrence cannot complete: a report is refused, and a\n2571\trelease or policy lapse records only the lapse and its epoch change.\n2572\t\n2573\t**Completion recovery (D16-completion-recovery).** A recovered exit checks the\n2574\tauthoritative closure before any release retry or acknowledgment, comparing the\n2575\texact origin lease, reported body address and successor occurrence (or terminal\n2576\tresult). When it matches, finish the terminal exit or retry the same successor\n2577\toffer until admitted; do not obtain or invent release carriage for a lease\n2578\talready closed by policy reclaim. Unmatched journal intents remain history.\n2579\tUnavailable/incomplete ledger evidence suspends. An unreported reclaimed origin\n2580\treturns to ordinary checkpoint candidacy with its speculative successor\n2581\tineligible. Quarantine, exhaustion, or a historical accepted report followed by\n2582\ta policy lapse without closure terminates recovery with a diagnostic, rather\n2583\tthan waiting forever. The repair is prospective: no retroactive standalone\n2584\tclosure transaction over such old history is authorized. Repeated reopen neither\n2585\tduplicates an edge nor advances an already-reclaimed lease's epoch again.\n2586\t\n2587\t## §7.8 Lifecycle: lift on park, lower on resume\n2588\t\n2589\t**Lift** (emitter side; composition-driven, never automatic on every park):\n2590\t\n2591\t1. The machine is parked or blocked with a local record `r`, and its\n2592\t   ready-queue is empty, else `:yin.k/not-quiescent`."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"23e67cb0-f183-4c2e-b3d6-2e5b6c44b814","uuid":"1798dc96-5993-4835-b647-ca5e7904d7e0","timestamp":"2026-10-13T08:31:44.700Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7plan/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md","content":"### 7.7.8 Fenced custody, version 1: sequence, envelope, epoch binding\n\n(Amendment r5, 2026-10-04; `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.3 item 2.) This\nsection publishes the grammar that 7.7.5 argues for. The reasons stay\nin 7.7.5; the runtime, ledger, and step contract stay in\n`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.2. Nothing here adds a key to the lease\nvocabulary or to a DaoStream outcome map. Every rule applies to\nversion-1 bodies only (7.2.1).\n\n**Exact integers.** `:yin.k/epoch`, `:yin.k/seq` and\n`:yin.k/next-op-seq` are nonnegative exact integers no greater than\n2^52-1 (4503599627370495), the bound within which every host compares\nand increments exactly (`dao.lease.md`, *Keys*, uses the same one).\nThe kind is the canonical codec's integer kind: a float is not an\ninteger even when it is integral. A value outside the range or of\nanother kind makes its carrier defective: a body is\n`:yin.k/undecodable`, an envelope or a binding establishes nothing.\nNo counter wraps, saturates silently, or restarts.\n\n**Operation sequence state.** A task under a grant holds one counter,\n`:yin.k/next-op-seq`, on its root. Install children draw from it.\n\n1. *Assign.* Before the first attempt of a write to an enrolled\n   stream, the fenced writer takes `n`, the counter's value, forms\n   the id `{:yin.k/occurrence P :yin.k/seq n}` with `P` the\n   occurrence its grant names, stores the id on the wait entry, and\n   sets the counter to `n+1`. The three happen as one step over the\n   machine value, before any append.\n2. *Retain.* The id stays on the entry while the write is unfinished:\n   a `full` path, an append of unknown effect, an acceptance with no\n   admission outcome yet, or a `:suspended` outcome. A retry reuses\n   the id and never takes another.\n3. *Carry.* At a park the id travels in the pending (7.4.3) and the\n   counter in the header (7.2.1). The successor's counter is the\n   holder's counter at that park, so the sequence continues along\n   the chain and never restarts at a new occurrence.\n4. *Restore.* Lower sets the fresh task's counter to the carried\n   value exactly. There is no local value to take a maximum with:\n   the receiving task is new (contrast `:yin.k/id-counter`, 7.6.3).\n5. *Regrant.* A holder granted the same checkpoint again starts from\n   the same bytes, so from the same counter and the same carried\n   ids. Re-executed writes take the same sequence numbers in the\n   same order as long as its inputs replay; where they do not, the\n   intent comparison below catches the divergence.\n6. *Exhaust.* The largest assignable sequence is 2^52-2. A counter\n   equal to 2^52-1 is exhausted: the writer assigns nothing and\n   appends nothing, the write stays an undischarged wait, and a lift\n   of that task refuses `:yin.k/non-portable`, kind\n   `:op-seq-exhausted`. Recovery is outside this protocol.\n\n**The fenced envelope.** A fenced writer wraps each value it appends\ntoward an enrolled consumer:\n\n```clojure\n{:yin.k/envelope    :yin.k/fenced-v1   ; dispatch key; closed\n :yin.k/incarnation L                  ; the writer's :dao.lease/lease\n :yin.k/epoch       e                  ; from L's grant binding\n :yin.k/op-id       {:yin.k/occurrence P :yin.k/seq n}\n :yin.k/value       v}                 ; the program value, verbatim\n```\n\n- All five keys are required. `:yin.k/op-id` has exactly its two\n  keys. A consumer ignores other qualified keys, and they are never\n  part of intent. An envelope that fails these rules, or dispatches\n  on a value other than `:yin.k/fenced-v1`, is defective (below).\n- **Inside program data: only `:yin.k/value`.** It is the value the\n  program wrote, unchanged and uninterpreted. A program value that\n  looks like an envelope is payload: it is wrapped like any other\n  and never read as one.\n- **Outside program data: everything else.** The envelope exists\n  between the fenced writer and the enrolled boundary and nowhere\n  else. It is never in a frame, an environment, a store, or a body;\n  a pending carries the retained value and the id beside it, and\n  the writer rebuilds the envelope at each retry from the id and\n  its current binding. What the consumer's commit makes visible\n  downstream is the consumer's contract; UCF requires only that the\n  payload it commits is `:yin.k/value`.\n- The envelope names no target, no effect kind, and no intent. The\n  consumer takes the target identity and the effect kind from the\n  boundary the envelope arrived at, and computes intent itself.\n- The envelope carries no credential. The holder is authenticated\n  by the attribution the composition supplies (`dao.lease.md`,\n  *Composition duties*). An envelope whose attributed author is not\n  the holder that the binding of `L` names is a fact about that\n  author: it is defective.\n\n**Defective envelopes.** A defective envelope commits no effect and\ncreates no dedup record. It yields no admission outcome: an outcome\nwould be attributed to the holder its fields claim, and those fields\nare exactly what cannot be trusted. The consumer instead appends one\nstructured diagnostic to the diagnostic stream the composition\nsupplies:\n\n```clojure\n{:yin.k/diagnostic :yin.k/defective-envelope\n :yin.k/defect     :malformed       ; closed, below\n :yin.k/target     i                ; the boundary's stream identity\n :yin.k/author     a                ; resolved attribution, if any\n :yin.k/claimed    {...}}           ; readable envelope fields\n```\n\n- The closed defect set is `:malformed` for invalid envelope\n  structure, unsupported envelope dispatch or version, invalid\n  numeric fields, or intent that cannot be canonically encoded;\n  `:unbound-lease` when no valid binding establishes the claimed\n  incarnation; `:wrong-author` when attribution is absent, invalid,\n  or does not identify the bound holder; and `:foreign-op-id` when\n  authoritative scope validation fails.\n- (M-next C, slice C7.) An op id whose `:yin.k/seq` is 2^52-1, a\n  value no writer can assign, is `:malformed`.\n- Enrollment belongs to the target boundary, while a writer's\n  authorization comes from attribution and binding. There is no\n  \"unenrolled writer\" defect: an unenrolled boundary cannot run\n  this admission protocol or claim its guarantee, so it produces\n  neither outcomes nor these diagnostics.\n- Readers dispatch on the `:yin.k/diagnostic` key, never on the\n  presence of an id. The diagnostic adds nothing to a lease fact or\n  to a DaoStream outcome map.\n- The composition supplies the diagnostic stream explicitly.\n  Failure or backpressure while publishing a diagnostic never\n  permits the rejected effect or creates an admission outcome;\n  diagnostic publication failure remains an explicit driver\n  outcome.\n- `:yin.k/claimed` holds whichever of `:yin.k/incarnation`,\n  `:yin.k/epoch` and `:yin.k/op-id` could be read, as claims. They\n  are nested so that no reader correlating on a top-level op id can\n  take the diagnostic for an answer. The payload is never echoed.\n- The map carries neither `:yin.k/admission` nor `:yin.k/status`. A\n  driver discharges nothing and ends nothing on a diagnostic.\n\n**Intent.** Intent is the vector `[effect-kind target-identity\npayload]`: the keyword naming the boundary's operation, the target's\n`:dao.stream/identity`, and the envelope's `:yin.k/value`. This\namendment enrolls one effect kind, `:yin.k/append`, a stream append;\nevery write the reference machine retains is one. Two intents are\nequal when their canonical bytes are equal. FFI and link correlation\nids are inside the payload and are compared with it; they are not\noperation ids.\n\n**The grant epoch binding.** The grantor publishes one more custody\nfact (7.7.2), authored by itself:\n\n```clojure\n{:yin.k/custody    :yin.k/bound\n :yin.k/occurrence O\n :dao.lease/lease  L\n :dao.lease/holder H\n :yin.k/epoch      e}\n```\n\n- The lease fact is unchanged. The binding is a separate fact, so a\n  lease judge, which switches on the lease keys, ignores it.\n- Unlike the offer and the resumed report, the binding is authority,\n  because the grantor authors it. It is valid only when its\n  attributed author is the transactor of the space the body's\n  `:yin.k/arbitration` names; when it was admitted in the same\n  transaction as a `:dao.lease/accepted` fact with the same lease,\n  the same holder, and the subject `{:yin.k/occurrence O}`; and when\n  it is the only binding for `L`. Any other binding establishes\n  nothing, and a grant on an occurrence with no valid binding\n  confers no fenced tenure.\n- (M-next C, slice C5.) *The only binding for `L`* means the only one\n  in the named authority's transaction history. A reader holding a\n  partial view can refute it, by finding a second binding, but never\n  establish it. In this composition the reader's records are the\n  authority's complete-retention journal read from its\n  `:dao.stream/oldest` anchor, so the check is completeness-backed,\n  and the authority's fold, which refuses a second binding, enforces\n  it. The author of records read from a stream whose\n  `:dao.stream/identity` equals the body's arbitration identity is\n  that identity. Descriptors are transport-relative and are never\n  compared.\n- A reader needs authenticated evidence that the grant and the\n  binding share one transaction of that authority: a transaction\n  identity within the named authority's provenance domain,\n  attributed to it. Equal `t` values read off arbitrary media prove\n  nothing. M-next C supplies the evidence and shows that a remote\n  reflection preserves it; a medium that cannot supply it cannot\n  offer `:yin.k/exclusive`.\n- **How a reader learns the epoch.** Only from a valid binding. A\n  holder reads it beside its grant before it activates, and stamps\n  it into its envelopes. A consumer reads the authority's current\n  state inside its admission transition. An epoch a holder\n  advertises, in an envelope or anywhere else, is a claim to be\n  checked, never a source.\n- **The counter.** Each occurrence has one epoch in its ledger\n  (7.7.3). It is 0 when the occurrence is first admitted. Every\n  reclaim of a lease on the occurrence, whatever its cause, raises\n  it by exactly one in the transaction that records the\n  `:dao.lease/lapsed` fact, before any new grant. A grant binds the\n  epoch current at that grant, so the first grant binds 0 and the\n  grant after `k` reclaims binds `k`. A successor is a new\n  occurrence and starts at 0.\n- (M-next C, slice C6.) **The epoch change** is the grantor's fact\n\n  ```clojure\n  {:yin.k/custody    :yin.k/reclaimed\n   :yin.k/occurrence O\n   :dao.lease/lease  L\n   :yin.k/epoch      e}             ; the epoch after the reclaim\n  ```\n\n  committed in the transaction that records `L`'s\n  `:dao.lease/lapsed` fact. Every lapse has exactly one, and the\n  ledger refuses a lapse without it or it without its lapse. At the\n  bound it is still written, carrying the unchanged epoch.\n- (M-next C, slice C6.) **A refusal** is a pair committed in one\n  transaction: the plain `:dao.lease/rejected` lease fact, verbatim\n  `dao.lease` (it names no proposer), and the grantor's\n\n  ```clojure\n  {:yin.k/custody      :yin.k/refused\n   :yin.k/proposer     P\n   :dao.lease/proposal pid}\n  ```\n\n  A lease judge switches on the lease keys and ignores the refused\n  fact; a reader learns which proposal a refusal answered, keyed\n  `[proposer proposal-id]`, from the refused fact alone. The ledger\n  refuses either half\n  without the other. A proposal id is answered at most once, so a\n  candidate refused, or answered `:yin.k/not-holder` (7.8), proposes\n  again with a fresh proposal id.\n- **Restart.** The epoch never decreases, is never reset, and is\n  never reused. A grantor that restarts recovers the epoch from its\n  durable ledger and reclaims, which raises it, before it grants\n  (7.7.7). A grantor that cannot recover the epoch grants nothing\n  for that occurrence: 7.7.7's reclaim-and-regrant presumes the\n  epoch survived. A new authority never starts an old occurrence at\n  0. Recovery from lost epoch or dedup state is governance, outside\n  this protocol (7.7.5). (M-next C, slice C6.) Reopen runs 7.7.7's\n  steps, rebuilding the judge after the reclaims: a reclaim that does\n  not commit refuses the open, and reopen is retried.\n- **Exhaustion.** Epoch 2^52-1 is usable until a reclaim would\n  increment it: a grant bound at that value is valid, and its\n  holder's effects are admitted like any other's. That reclaim ends\n  the tenure, leaves the epoch unchanged, and permanently exhausts\n  the occurrence. Thereafter admission returns `:suspended` before\n  tenure checking, and no effect commits. Exhaustion is derived\n  from the ledger: a lapse recorded for the lease bound at 2^52-1.\n  An exhausted tenure cannot produce an eligible exclusive\n  successor: the authority accepts no completion and grants nothing\n  for the occurrence. Carrier bytes may remain as history, but a\n  publication cannot reopen custody. (M-next C, slices C6 and C8.)\n  In the ledger, that lapse's epoch change carries the lease's own\n  binding epoch, which only a lease bound at 2^52-1 can do; that is\n  the exhaustion. A report recorded at the bound is evidence: its\n  release exhausts the occurrence without completing it.\n- **Why a binding.** A remote holder cannot infer authoritative\n  tenure from what it observes: `:dao.lease/lapsed` does not cross\n  to it (`dao.lease.md`, *Carriage*), and its view of the ledger is\n  partial. The binding publishes the grant's authoritative epoch.\n\n**Admission.** An enrolled consumer admits each well-formed envelope\nby one atomic transition against the authority's admission resource\n(7.7.5). The checks run in this order and the first to fail decides\nthe outcome (7.9):\n\n1. *Authority.* The authority the consumer is enrolled with must be\n   readable. If it is unreachable, if its ledger for the occurrence\n   that `L` is bound to is not recoverable, or if that occurrence\n   is exhausted, the outcome is `:suspended`. An occurrence\n   quarantined after an intent conflict (below) answers the same.\n2. *Binding.* `L` has a valid binding, and the envelope's attributed\n   author is the holder it names. Otherwise the envelope is\n   defective: a diagnostic, and no outcome.\n3. *Tenure.* The bound occurrence is open, `L` is its active lease,\n   and `e` equals its current epoch, else `:stale`. Staleness is\n   decided before the dedup record is consulted: a stale envelope is\n   refused even when a record for its id exists.\n4. *Scope.* An operation assigned during the current occurrence\n   names that occurrence. An inherited operation must occur among\n   the retained pending operations of the authoritative checkpoint\n   granted to the holder, including its install children, and its\n   occurrence must be an ancestor through authoritative completion\n   records. Membership is derived from the accepted checkpoint, the\n   body the authority admitted for the granted occurrence; no\n   ancestry index and no pending-id index is stored. An id that\n   fails is defective, `:foreign-op-id`. For an inherited id, if\n   the authoritative checkpoint cannot be read and verified,\n   admission answers `:suspended` without changing tenure,\n   quarantine, or dedup state. Unavailable evidence is not evidence\n   of an invalid id, and this suspension comes after the tenure\n   check, so a stale holder is still answered `:stale`. The\n   checkpoint is selected by the authority's accepted record, never\n   by an envelope-supplied location. Current-occurrence ids require\n   no inherited-membership lookup. Keeping the accepted checkpoint\n   readable is a durability duty of the composition. (M-next C,\n   slice C9.) Ancestry is checked first, from the completion records\n   alone, before the checkpoint is read. A granted occurrence's\n   ancestors never change, so a non-ancestor is `:foreign-op-id`\n   permanently; only membership waits on a store that can be\n   repaired. Any offered variant whose bytes verify to the recorded\n   baseline is the checkpoint, because the variants share one\n   baseline. A store that is absent, closed or throws is unreadable.\n5. *Dedup.* With a record for the id: equal intent answers\n   `:replayed` with the recorded result and commits nothing;\n   different intent answers `:intent-conflict` and commits nothing.\n   With no record: the effect, its result, and the record\n   `{op-id -> {intent result}}` commit together, and the outcome is\n   `:committed`.\n\n**Results and uncertainty.** The recorded result is the effect's\ndefinitive outcome, as plain data; for a stream append it is the\ntarget's DaoStream outcome map, unchanged. A terminal refusal is a\nresult, recorded and replayed unchanged, but only when the atomic\nadmission boundary itself establishes it. An outcome that leaves the\neffect unknown, such as a `:dao.stream/transport-error` from a\ntransport whose failures are not declared clean, is not a result.\nUnknown transport acceptance establishes neither commitment nor\nabsence of commitment. The writer retains the id and retries through\nthe fenced admission boundary. The authority may already hold a\ncommitted result, which the retry must replay. An external effect\nwhose uncertainty cannot be reconciled within that atomic boundary\nis outside this guarantee. Backpressure, by contrast, is no\ncommitment: it is the writer path's own `:dao.stream/full`, and\nnothing was appended.\n\n**Snapshot variants.** Snapshot variants of one occurrence must\npreserve its operation baseline: the root next-operation sequence\nand the mapping of retained operation ids to canonical intents,\nincluding child operations. Equal id sets alone are not enough: a\nvariant could keep an id and substitute its payload. The authority\nrefuses a conflicting variant; an unavailable comparison suspends\nvariant admission. (M-next C, slice C5.) The authority admits an\noffer in this order: inspect the bytes; under the authority lock,\ndecide purely; store the body only when the decision will commit;\ncommit. A refused, replayed or uninspectable offer stores nothing. A\nstore that fails answers `:suspended` and commits nothing; a body\nstored before a commit that then fails is a harmless orphan.\n(M-next C, slice C9.) These mechanics realize the suspension above:\nthe comparison's basis, the recorded baseline, is always in the open\nauthority's projection, so the only unavailable comparison is that\nfailing store.\n\n**One dedup namespace.** The dedup records form one logical namespace\nper arbitration admission resource, across every enrolled target that\ntakes part in the guarantee, keyed by the operation id alone. Target\nidentity is part of the intent, never of the key. So a replay that\ndiverges and sends an id to another target meets the first target's\nrecord and answers `:intent-conflict`. A consumer that cannot join\nthat atomic resource keeps no private table in its place: it cannot\nclaim the cross-target guarantee, and its stream is not enrolled.\n\n**Enrollment.** That a target stream is enrolled is an attributed\nfact of the authority on the arbitration medium, keyed by the target\nstream's identity. No body carries it. M-next C defines its schema\nand ensures that a change of enrollment cannot alter the protection\nof an operation already retained under an id.\n\n**Completion and the successor chain.** Completion (7.7.5) establishes\na single acyclic chain. The authority accepts at most one successor\nper predecessor and one predecessor per successor. The successor's\noccurrence is fresh, its `:yin.k/origin` names the predecessor and\nthe lease that ran it, and its `:yin.k/arbitration` identity is the\npredecessor's, unchanged. Only that accepted completion is an edge:\na published successor or a resumed report the authority did not\naccept is an orphan, and no ancestry runs through it. A closed\noccurrence receives no further grant.\n\n(M-next C, slice C8.) The first offer of a body with an origin is\nrefused `:awaiting-completion` while the origin's lease is its\noccurrence's live lease. Otherwise it is refused `:orphan` unless it\nis the successor the predecessor's closure recorded, under the\nclosing lease, at the reported address. An origin naming an\noccurrence this ledger never saw is `:orphan`: a successor's\narbitration is its predecessor's, so no predecessor lives on another\nauthority. A body with no origin is a first export and is\nunconstrained. A halted result completes through a terminal edge\n(7.7.6).\n\n**Quarantine.** An intent conflict that the authority itself\nestablished, in an authenticated admission, quarantines the\noccurrence: it stays open, is never regranted automatically, and its\nadmissions answer `:suspended`. Recovery or compensation is a\ngovernance decision outside this protocol. An unauthenticated claim\nof a conflict quarantines nothing. (M-next C, slice C8.) A\nquarantined occurrence cannot complete: a report is refused, and a\nrelease or policy lapse records only the lapse and its epoch change.\n\n**Completion recovery (D16-completion-recovery).** A recovered exit checks the\nauthoritative closure before any release retry or acknowledgment, comparing the\nexact origin lease, reported body address and successor occurrence (or terminal\nresult). When it matches, finish the terminal exit or retry the same successor\noffer until admitted; do not obtain or invent release carriage for a lease\nalready closed by policy reclaim. Unmatched journal intents remain history.\nUnavailable/incomplete ledger evidence suspends. An unreported reclaimed origin\nreturns to ordinary checkpoint candidacy with its speculative successor\nineligible. Quarantine, exhaustion, or a historical accepted report followed by\na policy lapse without closure terminates recovery with a diagnostic, rather\nthan waiting forever. The repair is prospective: no retroactive standalone\nclosure transaction over such old history is authorized. Repeated reopen neither\nduplicates an edge nor advances an already-reclaimed lease's epoch again.\n\n## §7.8 Lifecycle: lift on park, lower on resume\n\n**Lift** (emitter side; composition-driven, never automatic on every park):\n\n1. The machine is parked or blocked with a local record `r`, and its\n   ready-queue is empty, else `:yin.k/not-quiescent`.","numLines":400,"startLine":2193,"totalLines":3333}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01AcwRJ1t6PxWQXDF9tGo2f3","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
