{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011wHTNQVyWVUnV5G1rT79DA","type":"tool_result","content":"2587\t## §7.8 Lifecycle: lift on park, lower on resume\n2588\t\n2589\t**Lift** (emitter side; composition-driven, never automatic on every park):\n2590\t\n2591\t1. The machine is parked or blocked with a local record `r`, and its\n2592\t   ready-queue is empty, else `:yin.k/not-quiescent`.\n2593\t2. **Enter exporting** (§7.7.4): detach the wait set into the export\n2594\t   record, neutralize direct `:resume`. From here the source is fenced; no\n2595\t   poll, retry, or local resume can perform an effect.\n2596\t3. `lift(export-record)`: confirm the record is at a safepoint (§7.4.1),\n2597\t   else `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; canonicalize every referenced segment and\n2598\t   compute its address (§7.3.2), else the refusal; encode (§7.5), else the\n2599\t   refusal; compute `:yin.k/requires` and `:yin.k/store` by the fixed point\n2600\t   (§7.6), recording the discovery status; stamp the contract (§7.3.3); on\n2601\t   first export, mint the occurrence; mint `:yin.k/id`.\n2602\t4. Append the canonical instruction vectors of every required segment to a\n2603\t   `dao.jing` intake pool (the payloads *are* the vectors, §7.3.4), then\n2604\t   the id-less value body to the carrier medium; observe `:dao.stream/ok`\n2605\t   on each. `:yin.k/carried` may then be empty. Retry of any failed append\n2606\t   reuses the occurrence (§7.7.4).\n2607\t5. Under `:exclusive`, append `:yin.k/offered` to the arbitration medium.\n2608\t   The export record stays for recovery; the emitter is now a candidate\n2609\t   like any other, and wakes only on custody (a grant to itself), never on\n2610\t   data.\n2611\t\n2612\t**Lower** (resumer side):\n2613\t\n2614\t1. Read the value; check `:yin.k/contract`, else `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`.\n2615\t2. Verify `:yin.k/id` by re-hashing the body, else `:yin.k/hash-mismatch`.\n2616\t3. Decode against the §7.5.1 grammar with full structural validation, else\n2617\t   `:yin.k/undecodable` — a correct hash is not structural validation, and\n2618\t   both checks always run.\n2619\t4. Evaluate `:yin.k/requires` (§7.6.5); fetch what may be fetched (segments\n2620\t   by address, verified by re-canonicalization); else\n2621\t   `:yin.k/unsatisfied` with the discovery status.\n2622\t5. Under `:exclusive`, attach to the arbitration medium (an attachment\n2623\t   failure here is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming it — the value declares its\n2624\t   authority, §7.7.3), propose, then observe the grant, else\n2625\t   `:yin.k/not-holder` with the lease state observed; while a proposal is\n2626\t   unanswered the driver's step answers `:yin.k/awaiting-grant` — a\n2627\t   grantor owes no answer and no bound applies (`dao.lease.md`,\n2628\t   *Authority*).\n2629\t6. Load every required segment by address into the per-stamp index,\n2630\t   verifying each (§7.3.4).\n2631\t7. **Restore into an isolated store** (§7.6.2): the slice, the remapped\n2632\t   cursor cells, the resumer's FFI pair. Attach to every stream descriptor in the\n2633\t   frame, pending waits, and cursor cells (§7.4.3); an attachment `not-found` is\n2634\t   `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream. **Any failure from this step\n2635\t   onward — after custody was acquired — releases first**: the resumer\n2636\t   appends `:dao.lease/released`, the occurrence returns to offered, and\n2637\t   the failure outcome is returned. A resumer that dies without releasing\n2638\t   is recovered by the lease's own lapse; nothing else is owed.\n2639\t8. `lower(frame, layout)` into engine registers; re-mint every pending wait\n2640\t   in the local wait set; seed `:id-counter` per §7.6.3; run — through a\n2641\t   fenced writer under `:exclusive` (§7.7.5).\n2642\t9. At the next safepoint or halt, produce the successor occurrence\n2643\t   (§7.7.6), record `:yin.k/resumed`, release.\n2644\t\n2645\tNeither sequence contains a callback. Each is a step function a\n2646\tcomposition-supplied driver repeats, as for every other interpreter in the\n2647\tsystem.\n2648\t\n2649\t## §7.9 Outcome algebra\n2650\t\n2651\tEvery UCF lift or lower operation returns one map dispatching on\n2652\t`:yin.k/status`; the set is closed and every non-`ok` outcome carries\n2653\tthe data needed to act on it. Admission outcomes (below) are the second\n2654\tfamily, disjoint from this one.\n2655\t\n2656\t| `:yin.k/status` | Raised by | Carries |\n2657\t|---|---|---|\n2658\t| `:yin.k/ok` | any | the value, or the lowered VM |\n2659\t| `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` | lift | `:yin.k/segment`, `:yin.k/pc` |\n2660\t| `:yin.k/not-quiescent` | lift | the ready-queue depth |\n2661\t| `:yin.k/non-portable` | lift | `:yin.k/path`, `:yin.k/kind`, `:yin.k/hint` — kinds at §7.5.4, cycles included as `:kind :cyclic` |\n2662\t| `:yin.k/refused` | lift | `:yin.k/reason`, the export or abort refusal |\n2663\t| `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` | lower | `:yin.k/contract` expected and found |\n2664\t| `:yin.k/hash-mismatch` | lower | the address claimed and the address computed |\n2665\t| `:yin.k/undecodable` | lower | `:yin.k/path`, the structural defect — malformed markers, missing or cyclic refs, malformed-but-correctly-hashed bodies |\n2666\t| `:yin.k/unsatisfied` | lower | `:yin.k/missing`, `:yin.k/discovery` — includes attachment failures, cursor profiles, and an unreachable arbitration medium |\n2667\t| `:yin.k/awaiting-grant` | lower | `:yin.k/occurrence`, the proposal — a step status while a proposal is unanswered, bounded by nothing (`dao.lease.md`) |\n2668\t| `:yin.k/not-holder` | lower | `:yin.k/occurrence`, the lease state observed |\n2669\t\n2670\tTerminal DaoStream outcomes met during lift or lower (`transport-error`,\n2671\t`closed`, `invalid-descriptor`, …) are surfaced unchanged inside the UCF\n2672\toutcome under their own key `:dao.stream/outcome`, exactly as the transport\n2673\tproduced them; UCF does not rename them, nest them under private keys, or\n2674\ttranslate between them. FFI requests and responses inside a value use the\n2675\treference machine's versioned envelope, `dao.stream.apply` —\n2676\t`:dao.stream.apply/id`, `/op`, `/args`, `/ok`, `/error` — never the\n2677\tdeleted v1 `dao.stream.apply` vocabulary. Every lower failure names its\n2678\tcleanup obligation: before custody, none; after custody, release\n2679\t(§7.8 step 7).\n2680\t\n2681\t**Amendment r5: version-1 refusals use the statuses above.** The\n2682\tamendment adds no `:yin.k/status` value; the set stays closed.\n2683\t\n2684\t- An unsupported, absent, or non-integer body version is\n2685\t  `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`, carrying `:yin.k/version` as found and\n2686\t  `:yin.k/supported`; it is raised before restoration and before\n2687\t  every other check but the bytes and the tag (7.2.1).\n2688\t- A malformed custody header, operation id, or install entry is\n2689\t  `:yin.k/undecodable` with its path (7.2.1, 7.4.3).\n2690\t- Lift refuses `:yin.k/non-portable` with kind `:incomplete-install`,\n2691\t  `:unprotected-pending`, or `:op-seq-exhausted` (7.4.3, 7.7.8).\n2692\t- A stream whose protection the resumer cannot match is\n2693\t  `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream (7.4.3).\n2694\t- A grant observed without a valid epoch binding is\n2695\t  `:yin.k/not-holder`, carrying the lease state observed; the\n2696\t  resumer releases the lease, as after any failure past custody.\n2697\t\n2698\t**Amendment r5: admission outcomes.** The outcome of an enrolled\n2699\tconsumer's admission (7.7.8) is data that the consumer appends to the\n2700\tcomposition stream the holder's driver reads. It is one map\n2701\tdispatching on a second key, `:yin.k/admission`. It is not a\n2702\t`:yin.k/status`, not a `:yin.k/kind`, and not a link refusal: it\n2703\treports what a consumer did with one effect, where a status reports\n2704\twhat a lift or a lower did with a value. The two families are\n2705\tdisjoint and their keys never appear in one map. A DaoStream map\n2706\tinside `:yin.k/effect-result` is a recorded historical result, so\n2707\tthe rule against nesting a stream outcome, which governs direct lift\n2708\tand lower failures, does not reach it. The set is closed, with\n2709\texactly these five values:\n2710\t\n2711\t| `:yin.k/admission` | Meaning | Also carries |\n2712\t|---|---|---|\n2713\t| `:committed` | committed now, once | `:yin.k/effect-result` |\n2714\t| `:replayed` | committed before; nothing new | `:yin.k/effect-result` |\n2715\t| `:stale` | tenure is not current | `:yin.k/observed-epoch` |\n2716\t| `:intent-conflict` | same id, other intent | both intents |\n2717\t| `:suspended` | authority cannot decide | `:yin.k/arbitration` |\n2718\t\n2719\t- Every outcome carries `:yin.k/op-id`, the id of the envelope it\n2720\t  answers, and echoes that envelope's `:yin.k/incarnation`. A driver\n2721\t  correlates on the pair, so an outcome answering another holder's\n2722\t  retry of the same id is not mistaken for its own.\n2723\t- Correlation alone is insufficient. An outcome counts only when it\n2724\t  is authenticated: attributed, by the composition's resolver, to\n2725\t  the enrolled consumer or admission authority for that target. An\n2726\t  outcome from any other author is a fact about that author; the\n2727\t  driver discharges nothing and ends nothing on it.\n2728\t- `:yin.k/effect-result` is the recorded result of 7.7.8, the same\n2729\t  data on the first answer and on every replay. A DaoStream outcome\n2730\t  inside it is the map the target produced, with no key added,\n2731\t  removed, or renamed.\n2732\t- `:stale` carries `:yin.k/observed-epoch`, the occurrence's current\n2733\t  epoch; `:yin.k/observed-lease`, its active lease, when one exists;\n2734\t  and `:yin.k/closed true` when the occurrence is closed.\n2735\t- `:intent-conflict` carries `:yin.k/recorded-intent` and\n2736\t  `:yin.k/observed-intent`, each an intent vector of 7.7.8. On\n2737\t  M-next C's ledger, `:yin.k/recorded-intent` and\n2738\t  `:yin.k/observed-intent` are the canonical intent digests -- the\n2739\t  BLAKE3 of the canonical bytes of `[:yin.k/append target value]`\n2740\t  -- because the durable dedup record stores the digest and a\n2741\t  closed-target record drops the payload. Digest equality is\n2742\t  canonical-byte equality; the conflict decision is unchanged.\n2743\t- `:suspended` carries `:yin.k/arbitration`, naming the authority\n2744\t  that could not be read or could not decide, as a body names it\n2745\t  (7.2.1). It also answers for an exhausted or quarantined\n2746\t  occurrence, where retry cannot succeed without governance. On\n2747\t  M-next C's authority, `:suspended`'s `:yin.k/arbitration` is\n2748\t  `{:dao.stream/identity arb}`, the authority's journal identity.\n2749\t- A defective envelope has no admission outcome; its structured\n2750\t  diagnostic is in 7.7.8.\n2751\t- Only `:committed` and `:replayed` discharge a wait, and the\n2752\t  effect's resume value comes from their recorded result as 7.4.1\n2753\t  states for the variant. `:suspended` leaves the wait undischarged\n2754\t  and the id retained; the driver retries the same envelope.\n2755\t  `:stale` and `:intent-conflict` leave the wait undischarged and\n2756\t  end the run: the driver emits nothing further through its fenced\n2757\t  writers and publishes no successor. After an intent conflict the\n2758\t  occurrence is quarantined (7.7.8).\n2759\t- A duplicate outcome for a wait already discharged is skipped, as\n2760\t  a duplicate link response is (7.4.3). Outcomes are compared as\n2761\t  data on every host, never as text.\n2762\t- (M-next C, slice C7.) **The outcome projection.** The authority\n2763\t  serves the `:committed` outcome of every fenced admission its\n2764\t  ledger records on one reader-only stream whose identity is\n2765\t  `\"<arb>/outcomes\"`, arb its journal identity: dense positions in\n2766\t  ledger order, stable across reopen, blocked at the tail, never\n2767\t  ended. Its records are the authority's because they are derived\n2768\t  from its ledger. A driver holding a kept cursor recovers a\n2769\t  committed result after a crash without a reply; the other\n2770\t  outcomes are direct replies, recovered by retrying.\n2771\t- (M-next C, slice C11.) A holder counts a record as\n2772\t  authority-authored only when its composition attributes the\n2773\t  stream it was read from to the arbitration identity: the reply\n2774\t  stream the composition serves under that identity, and the\n2775\t  outcome projection at the identity `\"<arb>/outcomes\"`.\n2776\t  Attribution is identity equality; a descriptor is never compared.\n2777\t  A front reply's `:yin.k/answer` is the landed answer unchanged\n2778\t  (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.2.4).\n2779\t\n2780\t(M-next D, as built.) The driver's own refusals and the exporting\n2781\tstate's holds ride the closed status set above, never a new dispatch\n2782\tkey. `:yin.k/refused` carries `:yin.k/reason`: the exporting state\n2783\traises it for a machine already exporting or ended (`:not-running`,\n2784\tD8), and the abort rule for `:restarted`, `:not-exporting`,\n2785\t`:tenure-ended`, `:tenure-unconfirmed` and\n2786\t`:offer-possibly-accepted` (D14, 7.7.4). The custody holds that\n2787\trefuse a lift are a second vocabulary under `:yin.k/hold`, surfaced\n2788\tas `:yin.k/non-portable` of kind `:reason-mismatch` naming the hold\n2789\tand the task path: `:observe`, `:held`, `:unminted-cursor`,\n2790\t`:pending-close` and `:link-cursor-not-installed` (7.7.4). An input\n2791\tconflict is a `:yin.k/status` refusal of the authority's input\n2792\tprotocol, never the admission outcome `:intent-conflict`: an input\n2793\tis evidence, not an effect, and an input conflict commits nothing\n2794\tand quarantines nothing (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.2.2). The two\n2795\tfamilies are disjoint and no path translates one into the other. A\n2796\tdriver's inbox retention (`:yin.k/journal :yin.k/inbox`) is one\n2797\trecord per selected observation; its journal position is the merge\n2798\torder, with no second persisted ordering counter, and control\n2799\trecords may pass earlier deferred program records while deferred\n2800\tprogram order and selected control order are preserved\n2801\tindependently (7.11).\n2802\t\n2803\t## §7.10 Invariant compliance\n2804\t\n2805\t| Invariant | How UCF honors it |\n2806\t|---|---|\n2807\t| No hidden global state | Every dependency is declared in `:yin.k/requires` — *as computed by the declared fixed point, with its completeness stated* (`:yin.k/discovery`); the store travels as an explicit slice restored into an isolated store; fresh-name state and parked records travel explicitly; custody is facts on a named, possessed medium. |\n2808\t| No implicit control flow | Resume happens only at a safepoint the machine's parking transitions define; the successor chain is explicit `:yin.k/origin` links; the exporting state is an observed data state, never a notification. |\n2809\t| No callbacks | Lift and lower are step functions; custody is observed, never notified; a lapsed holder stops at its own bound; an unanswered proposal is a step status, not a wait. |\n2810\t| No shared mutable state | A UCF value is content-addressed and immutable; occurrence identity separates content variants from the one leased computation; two resumers of one value under `:fork` diverge into distinct incarnations; the isolated execution store shares nothing with the resumer's tasks. |\n2811\t| No layer collapsing | Canonicalize, encode, discover, arbitrate, load, restore, execute are separate functions with data between them. The engine's hot loop is untouched: lifting is a pure function over the blocked machine's value, and the exporting state is that value with an emptied wait-set and a retained record — driver discipline, not engine machinery. |\n2812\t| No assumed graphs | Sharing is an explicit value table keyed by address; cursor aliasing is an explicit cursor-cell graph, not inferred from positions; code refs are pcs relative to an addressed vector; nothing is resolved by pointer; foreign engines reconstruct dynamic state from the frame, never from layouts they assume. |\n2813\t\n2814\tAnd the axioms — each claim conditioned on the obligation it depends on: the\n2815\tcontinuation is a stream value (1); its meaning is supplied by the\n2816\tresumer's loader and contract stamp, not carried in it (2); its code and its\n2817\tstate are datoms under `:yin.code/*` and `:yin.k/*` (3); and it pauses,\n2818\ttravels, and resumes anywhere that **holds what it declares, satisfies the\n2819\tdeclared cursor profiles, and obtains custody where the policy demands it**\n2820\t(4) — the unconditional form of that sentence was a claim the unresolved\n2821\tstate closure, custody enforcement, and effect admission had not earned.\n2822\t\n2823\t## §7.11 Acceptance blockers for the implementation phase\n2824\t\n2825\tThe warning at the top of this file is retained until each of these is\n2826\tclosed with a test in the parity or conformance suite. The first four are\n2827\tthe review's named architectural obligations; they are blockers to\n2828\t*accepting the design*, not merely work items.\n2829\t\n2830\t- **Authority grounding and wiring.** The arbitration `dao.space`, its\n2831\t  transactor as grantor, the occurrence ledger with epochs, and the\n2832\t  attribution resolver `dao.lease.md` requires, are composition duties; the\n2833\t  first composition to ship UCF is `yin.repl.core`'s handoff demo, and\n2834\t  its wiring becomes the worked example. A composition without a\n2835\t  transactable space offers `:yin.k/fork` only.\n2836\t- **Execution identity.** Occurrence minting, the `:yin.k/origin` chain,\n2837\t  retry idempotence (one occurrence per park), and successor completion as\n2838\t  a grantor ledger transition need conformance tests, including the crash\n2839\t  windows of §7.7.5.\n2840\t- **Pending-state completeness.** Every pending variant of §7.4.3 must\n2841\t  round-trip and resume *elsewhere*: retained writes, sent and retained FFI\n2842\t  calls with both endpoints and the kept response cursor, and cursor cells\n2843\t  through the aliasing scenarios of `check-wait-set`. This depends on\n2844\t  `dao.stream.remote.md` and its **portable cursor profile**\n2845\t  `:dao.stream.remote/v1`, which preserves the source stream's kept cursor\n2846\t  and `gap` outcomes. The M4 string-backed stream test below is the\n2847\t  required cross-host proof.\n2848\t- **Enforceable fencing.** Epoch-checked, atomic-with-commitment admission\n2849\t  and durable op-id plus intent dedup at enrolled consumers are design\n2850\t  items for `dao.space.transactor.md`. A consumer without a shared atomic\n2851\t  boundary declares at-least-once or fail-stop behavior; it cannot claim\n2852\t  exactly-once custody from the lease alone.\n2853\t- **Canonical byte encoding.** `:yin.code/hash` and `:yin.k/id` are exactly\n2854\t  as portable as `dao.jing/segment-key`. Canonical CBOR landed in\n2855\t  3ddaa21b (recorded in `yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md` section 5); the\n2856\t  cross-host address checks below still must pass.\n2857\t- **Contract revision publication.** `:yin.code/contract \"v3\"` needs a\n2858\t  published revision history naming the complete execution contract\n2859\t  (§7.3.3): the tuple grammar — mnemonic set, per-mnemonic arity and operand\n2860\t  kinds, saturation/defaults table — the opcode table and transitions, the\n2861\t  resolution and last-value-wins rules, the effect outcome map, and the\n2862\t  scheduler semantics. The index is in `yin.vm.semantic.md` 2.4 and the\n2863\t  history landed in dbae125b (`yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md`); runtime profile\n2864\t  checks below remain open.\n2865\t- **Primitive profile publication.** The standard `yin.vm/primitives`\n2866\t  map published with profiles (S7.5.2), including the `:effectful`\n2867\t  declaration for `require` (`yin/def` is syntax, not a primitive);\n2868\t  reverse-lookup uniqueness asserted at `create-vm`.\n2869\t- **Definition frames under Rule R.** The UCF frame encoding of a\n2870\t  pending definition (a define continuation awaiting its value), stamp\n2871\t  comparison at continuation lowering, module-store snapshot lowering\n2872\t  through `engine/store-put`, and the lift and lower round trip of a\n2873\t  parked definition are M4 obligations and do not pass today.\n2874\t- **Discovery completeness.** The fixed point of §7.6.1 implemented and\n2875\t  tested against the `:store-get`/`:store-put`, dynamic-callee, and\n2876\t  cross-activation-`E` cases; module manifests publishing store footprints\n2877\t  so `:yin.k/discovery :incomplete` disappears.\n2878\t- **Isolated-store lowering.** Conformance tests that the resumed task's\n2879\t  store contains exactly the slice and the resumer's pair, and that a\n2880\t  receiver-side binding of a name in the slice's name set cannot change\n2881\t  resolution.\n2882\t- **Safepoint conformance harness.** A test that runs every corpus segment\n2883\t  on the reference machine to each safepoint of the complete §7.4.1 table —\n2884\t  effect-producing calls included — lifts, lowers into a fresh VM, and\n2885\t  checks the parity suite's result; the same harness is what a foreign\n2886\t  engine runs to claim conformance, including `lift ∘ lower = id` on\n2887\t  canonical frames, the static `def`/`use`/`live` and\n2888\t  `lexically-required` checks, the frame validator on every frame, and\n2889\t  **cross-engine canonical equality**: one program at one safepoint lifts\n2890\t  to row sets with equal root ids after the harness's specified\n2891\t  re-emission (`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md` §5.5) from\n2892\t  the semantic-register kernel and from the stack and register kernels\n2893\t  through their layouts, the stack kernel with its tail-completion seam;\n2894\t  and the §7.3.4 image-equality assertion (the two AST projection lanes\n2895\t  and the direct vector load yield one image and address).\n2896\t- **Exporting-state integration.** The lift driver's exporting transition\n2897\t  and its failure/retry paths, tested specifically against the\n2898\t  poll-a-blocked-writer-appends hazard of §7.7.4.\n2899\t\n2900\t### Holder driver scheduling and retained inboxes (D15a)\n2901\t\n2902\tThe holder exposes four composition-local entries over the existing driver\n2903\tstate: `control-step`, `program-step`, `stop`, and `owed-control-write?`.\n2904\t`step` remains the normal-operation convenience entry, using the same machinery\n2905\twith one renewal and one program cycle per tick. These entries change no\n2906\thandoff body, authority fact, lease fact, or DaoStream outcome vocabulary."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"23e67cb0-f183-4c2e-b3d6-2e5b6c44b814","uuid":"cb55b259-a3f6-4e57-99cf-458c5e07e9a4","timestamp":"2026-10-13T08:31:46.038Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7plan/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md","content":"## §7.8 Lifecycle: lift on park, lower on resume\n\n**Lift** (emitter side; composition-driven, never automatic on every park):\n\n1. The machine is parked or blocked with a local record `r`, and its\n   ready-queue is empty, else `:yin.k/not-quiescent`.\n2. **Enter exporting** (§7.7.4): detach the wait set into the export\n   record, neutralize direct `:resume`. From here the source is fenced; no\n   poll, retry, or local resume can perform an effect.\n3. `lift(export-record)`: confirm the record is at a safepoint (§7.4.1),\n   else `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; canonicalize every referenced segment and\n   compute its address (§7.3.2), else the refusal; encode (§7.5), else the\n   refusal; compute `:yin.k/requires` and `:yin.k/store` by the fixed point\n   (§7.6), recording the discovery status; stamp the contract (§7.3.3); on\n   first export, mint the occurrence; mint `:yin.k/id`.\n4. Append the canonical instruction vectors of every required segment to a\n   `dao.jing` intake pool (the payloads *are* the vectors, §7.3.4), then\n   the id-less value body to the carrier medium; observe `:dao.stream/ok`\n   on each. `:yin.k/carried` may then be empty. Retry of any failed append\n   reuses the occurrence (§7.7.4).\n5. Under `:exclusive`, append `:yin.k/offered` to the arbitration medium.\n   The export record stays for recovery; the emitter is now a candidate\n   like any other, and wakes only on custody (a grant to itself), never on\n   data.\n\n**Lower** (resumer side):\n\n1. Read the value; check `:yin.k/contract`, else `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`.\n2. Verify `:yin.k/id` by re-hashing the body, else `:yin.k/hash-mismatch`.\n3. Decode against the §7.5.1 grammar with full structural validation, else\n   `:yin.k/undecodable` — a correct hash is not structural validation, and\n   both checks always run.\n4. Evaluate `:yin.k/requires` (§7.6.5); fetch what may be fetched (segments\n   by address, verified by re-canonicalization); else\n   `:yin.k/unsatisfied` with the discovery status.\n5. Under `:exclusive`, attach to the arbitration medium (an attachment\n   failure here is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming it — the value declares its\n   authority, §7.7.3), propose, then observe the grant, else\n   `:yin.k/not-holder` with the lease state observed; while a proposal is\n   unanswered the driver's step answers `:yin.k/awaiting-grant` — a\n   grantor owes no answer and no bound applies (`dao.lease.md`,\n   *Authority*).\n6. Load every required segment by address into the per-stamp index,\n   verifying each (§7.3.4).\n7. **Restore into an isolated store** (§7.6.2): the slice, the remapped\n   cursor cells, the resumer's FFI pair. Attach to every stream descriptor in the\n   frame, pending waits, and cursor cells (§7.4.3); an attachment `not-found` is\n   `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream. **Any failure from this step\n   onward — after custody was acquired — releases first**: the resumer\n   appends `:dao.lease/released`, the occurrence returns to offered, and\n   the failure outcome is returned. A resumer that dies without releasing\n   is recovered by the lease's own lapse; nothing else is owed.\n8. `lower(frame, layout)` into engine registers; re-mint every pending wait\n   in the local wait set; seed `:id-counter` per §7.6.3; run — through a\n   fenced writer under `:exclusive` (§7.7.5).\n9. At the next safepoint or halt, produce the successor occurrence\n   (§7.7.6), record `:yin.k/resumed`, release.\n\nNeither sequence contains a callback. Each is a step function a\ncomposition-supplied driver repeats, as for every other interpreter in the\nsystem.\n\n## §7.9 Outcome algebra\n\nEvery UCF lift or lower operation returns one map dispatching on\n`:yin.k/status`; the set is closed and every non-`ok` outcome carries\nthe data needed to act on it. Admission outcomes (below) are the second\nfamily, disjoint from this one.\n\n| `:yin.k/status` | Raised by | Carries |\n|---|---|---|\n| `:yin.k/ok` | any | the value, or the lowered VM |\n| `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` | lift | `:yin.k/segment`, `:yin.k/pc` |\n| `:yin.k/not-quiescent` | lift | the ready-queue depth |\n| `:yin.k/non-portable` | lift | `:yin.k/path`, `:yin.k/kind`, `:yin.k/hint` — kinds at §7.5.4, cycles included as `:kind :cyclic` |\n| `:yin.k/refused` | lift | `:yin.k/reason`, the export or abort refusal |\n| `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` | lower | `:yin.k/contract` expected and found |\n| `:yin.k/hash-mismatch` | lower | the address claimed and the address computed |\n| `:yin.k/undecodable` | lower | `:yin.k/path`, the structural defect — malformed markers, missing or cyclic refs, malformed-but-correctly-hashed bodies |\n| `:yin.k/unsatisfied` | lower | `:yin.k/missing`, `:yin.k/discovery` — includes attachment failures, cursor profiles, and an unreachable arbitration medium |\n| `:yin.k/awaiting-grant` | lower | `:yin.k/occurrence`, the proposal — a step status while a proposal is unanswered, bounded by nothing (`dao.lease.md`) |\n| `:yin.k/not-holder` | lower | `:yin.k/occurrence`, the lease state observed |\n\nTerminal DaoStream outcomes met during lift or lower (`transport-error`,\n`closed`, `invalid-descriptor`, …) are surfaced unchanged inside the UCF\noutcome under their own key `:dao.stream/outcome`, exactly as the transport\nproduced them; UCF does not rename them, nest them under private keys, or\ntranslate between them. FFI requests and responses inside a value use the\nreference machine's versioned envelope, `dao.stream.apply` —\n`:dao.stream.apply/id`, `/op`, `/args`, `/ok`, `/error` — never the\ndeleted v1 `dao.stream.apply` vocabulary. Every lower failure names its\ncleanup obligation: before custody, none; after custody, release\n(§7.8 step 7).\n\n**Amendment r5: version-1 refusals use the statuses above.** The\namendment adds no `:yin.k/status` value; the set stays closed.\n\n- An unsupported, absent, or non-integer body version is\n  `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`, carrying `:yin.k/version` as found and\n  `:yin.k/supported`; it is raised before restoration and before\n  every other check but the bytes and the tag (7.2.1).\n- A malformed custody header, operation id, or install entry is\n  `:yin.k/undecodable` with its path (7.2.1, 7.4.3).\n- Lift refuses `:yin.k/non-portable` with kind `:incomplete-install`,\n  `:unprotected-pending`, or `:op-seq-exhausted` (7.4.3, 7.7.8).\n- A stream whose protection the resumer cannot match is\n  `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream (7.4.3).\n- A grant observed without a valid epoch binding is\n  `:yin.k/not-holder`, carrying the lease state observed; the\n  resumer releases the lease, as after any failure past custody.\n\n**Amendment r5: admission outcomes.** The outcome of an enrolled\nconsumer's admission (7.7.8) is data that the consumer appends to the\ncomposition stream the holder's driver reads. It is one map\ndispatching on a second key, `:yin.k/admission`. It is not a\n`:yin.k/status`, not a `:yin.k/kind`, and not a link refusal: it\nreports what a consumer did with one effect, where a status reports\nwhat a lift or a lower did with a value. The two families are\ndisjoint and their keys never appear in one map. A DaoStream map\ninside `:yin.k/effect-result` is a recorded historical result, so\nthe rule against nesting a stream outcome, which governs direct lift\nand lower failures, does not reach it. The set is closed, with\nexactly these five values:\n\n| `:yin.k/admission` | Meaning | Also carries |\n|---|---|---|\n| `:committed` | committed now, once | `:yin.k/effect-result` |\n| `:replayed` | committed before; nothing new | `:yin.k/effect-result` |\n| `:stale` | tenure is not current | `:yin.k/observed-epoch` |\n| `:intent-conflict` | same id, other intent | both intents |\n| `:suspended` | authority cannot decide | `:yin.k/arbitration` |\n\n- Every outcome carries `:yin.k/op-id`, the id of the envelope it\n  answers, and echoes that envelope's `:yin.k/incarnation`. A driver\n  correlates on the pair, so an outcome answering another holder's\n  retry of the same id is not mistaken for its own.\n- Correlation alone is insufficient. An outcome counts only when it\n  is authenticated: attributed, by the composition's resolver, to\n  the enrolled consumer or admission authority for that target. An\n  outcome from any other author is a fact about that author; the\n  driver discharges nothing and ends nothing on it.\n- `:yin.k/effect-result` is the recorded result of 7.7.8, the same\n  data on the first answer and on every replay. A DaoStream outcome\n  inside it is the map the target produced, with no key added,\n  removed, or renamed.\n- `:stale` carries `:yin.k/observed-epoch`, the occurrence's current\n  epoch; `:yin.k/observed-lease`, its active lease, when one exists;\n  and `:yin.k/closed true` when the occurrence is closed.\n- `:intent-conflict` carries `:yin.k/recorded-intent` and\n  `:yin.k/observed-intent`, each an intent vector of 7.7.8. On\n  M-next C's ledger, `:yin.k/recorded-intent` and\n  `:yin.k/observed-intent` are the canonical intent digests -- the\n  BLAKE3 of the canonical bytes of `[:yin.k/append target value]`\n  -- because the durable dedup record stores the digest and a\n  closed-target record drops the payload. Digest equality is\n  canonical-byte equality; the conflict decision is unchanged.\n- `:suspended` carries `:yin.k/arbitration`, naming the authority\n  that could not be read or could not decide, as a body names it\n  (7.2.1). It also answers for an exhausted or quarantined\n  occurrence, where retry cannot succeed without governance. On\n  M-next C's authority, `:suspended`'s `:yin.k/arbitration` is\n  `{:dao.stream/identity arb}`, the authority's journal identity.\n- A defective envelope has no admission outcome; its structured\n  diagnostic is in 7.7.8.\n- Only `:committed` and `:replayed` discharge a wait, and the\n  effect's resume value comes from their recorded result as 7.4.1\n  states for the variant. `:suspended` leaves the wait undischarged\n  and the id retained; the driver retries the same envelope.\n  `:stale` and `:intent-conflict` leave the wait undischarged and\n  end the run: the driver emits nothing further through its fenced\n  writers and publishes no successor. After an intent conflict the\n  occurrence is quarantined (7.7.8).\n- A duplicate outcome for a wait already discharged is skipped, as\n  a duplicate link response is (7.4.3). Outcomes are compared as\n  data on every host, never as text.\n- (M-next C, slice C7.) **The outcome projection.** The authority\n  serves the `:committed` outcome of every fenced admission its\n  ledger records on one reader-only stream whose identity is\n  `\"<arb>/outcomes\"`, arb its journal identity: dense positions in\n  ledger order, stable across reopen, blocked at the tail, never\n  ended. Its records are the authority's because they are derived\n  from its ledger. A driver holding a kept cursor recovers a\n  committed result after a crash without a reply; the other\n  outcomes are direct replies, recovered by retrying.\n- (M-next C, slice C11.) A holder counts a record as\n  authority-authored only when its composition attributes the\n  stream it was read from to the arbitration identity: the reply\n  stream the composition serves under that identity, and the\n  outcome projection at the identity `\"<arb>/outcomes\"`.\n  Attribution is identity equality; a descriptor is never compared.\n  A front reply's `:yin.k/answer` is the landed answer unchanged\n  (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.2.4).\n\n(M-next D, as built.) The driver's own refusals and the exporting\nstate's holds ride the closed status set above, never a new dispatch\nkey. `:yin.k/refused` carries `:yin.k/reason`: the exporting state\nraises it for a machine already exporting or ended (`:not-running`,\nD8), and the abort rule for `:restarted`, `:not-exporting`,\n`:tenure-ended`, `:tenure-unconfirmed` and\n`:offer-possibly-accepted` (D14, 7.7.4). The custody holds that\nrefuse a lift are a second vocabulary under `:yin.k/hold`, surfaced\nas `:yin.k/non-portable` of kind `:reason-mismatch` naming the hold\nand the task path: `:observe`, `:held`, `:unminted-cursor`,\n`:pending-close` and `:link-cursor-not-installed` (7.7.4). An input\nconflict is a `:yin.k/status` refusal of the authority's input\nprotocol, never the admission outcome `:intent-conflict`: an input\nis evidence, not an effect, and an input conflict commits nothing\nand quarantines nothing (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.2.2). The two\nfamilies are disjoint and no path translates one into the other. A\ndriver's inbox retention (`:yin.k/journal :yin.k/inbox`) is one\nrecord per selected observation; its journal position is the merge\norder, with no second persisted ordering counter, and control\nrecords may pass earlier deferred program records while deferred\nprogram order and selected control order are preserved\nindependently (7.11).\n\n## §7.10 Invariant compliance\n\n| Invariant | How UCF honors it |\n|---|---|\n| No hidden global state | Every dependency is declared in `:yin.k/requires` — *as computed by the declared fixed point, with its completeness stated* (`:yin.k/discovery`); the store travels as an explicit slice restored into an isolated store; fresh-name state and parked records travel explicitly; custody is facts on a named, possessed medium. |\n| No implicit control flow | Resume happens only at a safepoint the machine's parking transitions define; the successor chain is explicit `:yin.k/origin` links; the exporting state is an observed data state, never a notification. |\n| No callbacks | Lift and lower are step functions; custody is observed, never notified; a lapsed holder stops at its own bound; an unanswered proposal is a step status, not a wait. |\n| No shared mutable state | A UCF value is content-addressed and immutable; occurrence identity separates content variants from the one leased computation; two resumers of one value under `:fork` diverge into distinct incarnations; the isolated execution store shares nothing with the resumer's tasks. |\n| No layer collapsing | Canonicalize, encode, discover, arbitrate, load, restore, execute are separate functions with data between them. The engine's hot loop is untouched: lifting is a pure function over the blocked machine's value, and the exporting state is that value with an emptied wait-set and a retained record — driver discipline, not engine machinery. |\n| No assumed graphs | Sharing is an explicit value table keyed by address; cursor aliasing is an explicit cursor-cell graph, not inferred from positions; code refs are pcs relative to an addressed vector; nothing is resolved by pointer; foreign engines reconstruct dynamic state from the frame, never from layouts they assume. |\n\nAnd the axioms — each claim conditioned on the obligation it depends on: the\ncontinuation is a stream value (1); its meaning is supplied by the\nresumer's loader and contract stamp, not carried in it (2); its code and its\nstate are datoms under `:yin.code/*` and `:yin.k/*` (3); and it pauses,\ntravels, and resumes anywhere that **holds what it declares, satisfies the\ndeclared cursor profiles, and obtains custody where the policy demands it**\n(4) — the unconditional form of that sentence was a claim the unresolved\nstate closure, custody enforcement, and effect admission had not earned.\n\n## §7.11 Acceptance blockers for the implementation phase\n\nThe warning at the top of this file is retained until each of these is\nclosed with a test in the parity or conformance suite. The first four are\nthe review's named architectural obligations; they are blockers to\n*accepting the design*, not merely work items.\n\n- **Authority grounding and wiring.** The arbitration `dao.space`, its\n  transactor as grantor, the occurrence ledger with epochs, and the\n  attribution resolver `dao.lease.md` requires, are composition duties; the\n  first composition to ship UCF is `yin.repl.core`'s handoff demo, and\n  its wiring becomes the worked example. A composition without a\n  transactable space offers `:yin.k/fork` only.\n- **Execution identity.** Occurrence minting, the `:yin.k/origin` chain,\n  retry idempotence (one occurrence per park), and successor completion as\n  a grantor ledger transition need conformance tests, including the crash\n  windows of §7.7.5.\n- **Pending-state completeness.** Every pending variant of §7.4.3 must\n  round-trip and resume *elsewhere*: retained writes, sent and retained FFI\n  calls with both endpoints and the kept response cursor, and cursor cells\n  through the aliasing scenarios of `check-wait-set`. This depends on\n  `dao.stream.remote.md` and its **portable cursor profile**\n  `:dao.stream.remote/v1`, which preserves the source stream's kept cursor\n  and `gap` outcomes. The M4 string-backed stream test below is the\n  required cross-host proof.\n- **Enforceable fencing.** Epoch-checked, atomic-with-commitment admission\n  and durable op-id plus intent dedup at enrolled consumers are design\n  items for `dao.space.transactor.md`. A consumer without a shared atomic\n  boundary declares at-least-once or fail-stop behavior; it cannot claim\n  exactly-once custody from the lease alone.\n- **Canonical byte encoding.** `:yin.code/hash` and `:yin.k/id` are exactly\n  as portable as `dao.jing/segment-key`. Canonical CBOR landed in\n  3ddaa21b (recorded in `yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md` section 5); the\n  cross-host address checks below still must pass.\n- **Contract revision publication.** `:yin.code/contract \"v3\"` needs a\n  published revision history naming the complete execution contract\n  (§7.3.3): the tuple grammar — mnemonic set, per-mnemonic arity and operand\n  kinds, saturation/defaults table — the opcode table and transitions, the\n  resolution and last-value-wins rules, the effect outcome map, and the\n  scheduler semantics. The index is in `yin.vm.semantic.md` 2.4 and the\n  history landed in dbae125b (`yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md`); runtime profile\n  checks below remain open.\n- **Primitive profile publication.** The standard `yin.vm/primitives`\n  map published with profiles (S7.5.2), including the `:effectful`\n  declaration for `require` (`yin/def` is syntax, not a primitive);\n  reverse-lookup uniqueness asserted at `create-vm`.\n- **Definition frames under Rule R.** The UCF frame encoding of a\n  pending definition (a define continuation awaiting its value), stamp\n  comparison at continuation lowering, module-store snapshot lowering\n  through `engine/store-put`, and the lift and lower round trip of a\n  parked definition are M4 obligations and do not pass today.\n- **Discovery completeness.** The fixed point of §7.6.1 implemented and\n  tested against the `:store-get`/`:store-put`, dynamic-callee, and\n  cross-activation-`E` cases; module manifests publishing store footprints\n  so `:yin.k/discovery :incomplete` disappears.\n- **Isolated-store lowering.** Conformance tests that the resumed task's\n  store contains exactly the slice and the resumer's pair, and that a\n  receiver-side binding of a name in the slice's name set cannot change\n  resolution.\n- **Safepoint conformance harness.** A test that runs every corpus segment\n  on the reference machine to each safepoint of the complete §7.4.1 table —\n  effect-producing calls included — lifts, lowers into a fresh VM, and\n  checks the parity suite's result; the same harness is what a foreign\n  engine runs to claim conformance, including `lift ∘ lower = id` on\n  canonical frames, the static `def`/`use`/`live` and\n  `lexically-required` checks, the frame validator on every frame, and\n  **cross-engine canonical equality**: one program at one safepoint lifts\n  to row sets with equal root ids after the harness's specified\n  re-emission (`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md` §5.5) from\n  the semantic-register kernel and from the stack and register kernels\n  through their layouts, the stack kernel with its tail-completion seam;\n  and the §7.3.4 image-equality assertion (the two AST projection lanes\n  and the direct vector load yield one image and address).\n- **Exporting-state integration.** The lift driver's exporting transition\n  and its failure/retry paths, tested specifically against the\n  poll-a-blocked-writer-appends hazard of §7.7.4.\n\n### Holder driver scheduling and retained inboxes (D15a)\n\nThe holder exposes four composition-local entries over the existing driver\nstate: `control-step`, `program-step`, `stop`, and `owed-control-write?`.\n`step` remains the normal-operation convenience entry, using the same machinery\nwith one renewal and one program cycle per tick. These entries change no\nhandoff body, authority fact, lease fact, or DaoStream outcome vocabulary.","numLines":320,"startLine":2587,"totalLines":3333}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_011wHTNQVyWVUnV5G1rT79DA","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
