{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013chZ9uFNK7BVVyRijk3EpU","type":"tool_result","content":"2900\t### Holder driver scheduling and retained inboxes (D15a)\n2901\t\n2902\tThe holder exposes four composition-local entries over the existing driver\n2903\tstate: `control-step`, `program-step`, `stop`, and `owed-control-write?`.\n2904\t`step` remains the normal-operation convenience entry, using the same machinery\n2905\twith one renewal and one program cycle per tick. These entries change no\n2906\thandoff body, authority fact, lease fact, or DaoStream outcome vocabulary.\n2907\t\n2908\t`control-step` retains incoming observations and handles authenticated custody\n2909\tanswers, renewals, retries, reports, releases, and closure evidence. It never\n2910\tlowers a newly granted checkpoint, attaches program resources, applies program\n2911\tresults, executes guest code, replays inputs, emits program effects, observes\n2912\tprogram streams, or prepares an export. A newly accepted grant remains in\n2913\t`:activating` with its original lease observation basis and no machine.\n2914\t\n2915\t`program-step` independently checks complete authenticated evidence, the same\n2916\tlive occurrence/lease/epoch, and fresh `lease/holding?` before activation,\n2917\texecution, and retained-result application. Unavailable evidence suspends;\n2918\tcontradiction or expiry uses the existing diagnostic/cleanup run end. The\n2919\tper-effect tenure guards remain in force, including across computation and\n2920\tbefore replay. Export preparation and capture belong here; established\n2921\toffer/report/release/closure brackets belong to control progress. Activation\n2922\tuses fresh evidence without refreshing the original tenure basis; authenticated\n2923\trenewals and the maximum cap still determine the current bound.\n2924\t\n2925\tAssembly requires both `:reply-inbox` and `:outcome-inbox`, each:\n2926\t\n2927\t```clojure\n2928\t{:version 1 :identity portable-stable-identity\n2929\t :read-at! (fn [position] result)}\n2930\t```\n2931\t\n2932\tThe operation is non-destructive over dense exact portable positions. Its\n2933\tclosed result union is `{:status :record :position n :author a :record r}`,\n2934\t`{:status :empty}` at the currently available tail, or\n2935\t`{:status :unavailable}` for missing, gapped, or inaccessible history. Empty\n2936\tdoes not advance. Positions cannot wrap or advance beyond the journal bound.\n2937\tThis version requires complete retention throughout recovery; it has no\n2938\ttruncation or acknowledgment protocol. Legacy destructive readers are rejected,\n2939\tnot automatically spooled after consumption.\n2940\t\n2941\tEither lane's unavailable answer suspends program progress and new activation,\n2942\tnot valid custody control or cleanup. Complete authenticated ledger evidence\n2943\tand the same live binding remain necessary to schedule renewal. An unavailable\n2944\treply position ends that tick's reply-first scan without consuming the outcome\n2945\tlane; due renewals and pending requests still send or retry unchanged. Inbound\n2946\tacceptance credits no renewal: the request identity and original pre-send\n2947\treading remain pending until authenticated carriage passes the before-and-after\n2948\ttenure checks. A delayed reply cannot revive a run past its local bound.\n2949\tExpiry still gates the machine ended and emits one run-end diagnostic, with a\n2950\tdurable release intent before sending and unchanged cleanup retries until\n2951\tauthenticated carriage. Availability obstruction remains observable until a\n2952\tsuccessful scan; malformed/conflicting evidence and journal uncertainty are\n2953\tnot this permissive case. Reopening a journaled holder may recover cleanup\n2954\twhile receipt-source reconciliation is unavailable, retaining the receipts\n2955\twithout dispatch until reconciliation succeeds. It never restores execution.\n2956\t\n2957\tSelection is reply-first. Before dispatch or source advancement the driver\n2958\tdurably appends `:yin.k/journal :yin.k/inbox`, carrying `:yin.k/lane`,\n2959\t`:yin.k/identity`, `:yin.k/position`, `:yin.k/author`, `:yin.k/record`, and,\n2960\twhen present, `:yin.k/binding` naming the local occurrence, lease, and epoch.\n2961\tThe journal position is the merge order; there is no second persisted ordering\n2962\tcounter. Control records may pass earlier deferred program records. Deferred\n2963\tprogram order and selected control order are preserved independently.\n2964\t\n2965\tAn uncertain append stalls both step entries until reopen reconciles it.\n2966\tReceipt reconciliation deduplicates identical lane/identity/position records\n2967\tand rejects conflicting canonical contents, changed sources, and gaps. Source\n2968\tpositions and queues are derived from those records; a surviving machine\n2969\tconsumes each queued observation once. There is no persisted VM-applied bit.\n2970\tAfter process restart the existing checkpoint/regrant/replay rules still govern;\n2971\told-lease receipts never authorize a fresh run. Persisted clock readings do not\n2972\tre-establish tenure.\n2973\t\n2974\t`stop` is an irreversible, idempotent local latch, not a machine gate or an\n2975\texport abort. Program steps thereafter do nothing. Control ticks authenticate\n2976\tlate grants and release them without lower, suppress fresh/replacement/recovery\n2977\tcandidacy, and drain already established control obligations. An unfinished\n2978\tprogram/export cannot invent a completion to keep its lease alive. Cleanup\n2979\tattempts retain their request identity and intent-before-send journal brackets.\n2980\tThe composition owns the bounded drain budget, judge/front progress, and\n2981\treapplying the local latch when reopening during shutdown.\n2982\t\n2983\t`owed-control-write?` is pure and total: proposals, pending renewals, releases,\n2984\teligible offers/reports, and required diagnostic/cleanup remain owed until the\n2985\tappropriate authenticated evidence discharges them. Inbound acceptance alone\n2986\tdoes not discharge a bracket. Future renewal deadlines, passive ledger waits,\n2987\tand uncertain-journal stalls are not executable writes.\n2988\tPending eligible renewal or release remains owed during inbox unavailability;\n2989\tunavailable history alone is not an owed write. D15 owns production\n2990\tpositional adapters and REPL integration; memory-backed test reconstruction is\n2991\tsimulated process recovery, not proof of durable media.\n2992\t\n2993\t### 7.11.1 Blocker-closure acceptance matrix\n2994\t\n2995\tThese are observable test contracts, not a declaration that the blockers\n2996\tare closed. M3 and M4 tests run on JVM, CLJS (Node), and CLJD. A named\n2997\trefusal must be a data outcome before a code image is loaded, a machine is\n2998\trestored, or an effect is committed, as applicable. The M4 lift driver\n2999\tmust consume the UCF table amendments in linker section 11 item 12 before\n3000\tits round-trip tests run. Full UCF acceptance waits for every row,\n3001\tincluding the post-M5 gates; passing a linker milestone alone is not a\n3002\tclaim that all five blockers have closed.\n3003\t\n3004\t**Code identity (7.3; blocker 5).**\n3005\t\n3006\t- Invariant: A resumer executes the instruction stream named by the\n3007\t  continuation's address and contract stamp, or refuses before loading it.\n3008\t- Setup: On each host, make equal resolved vectors from batches with\n3009\t  different entity ids, order, provenance, and omitted defaults; make\n3010\t  unequal vectors by reversing repeated single-valued operands. Include\n3011\t  a `:gensym` prefix of `false` beside absent and explicit `\"id\"` prefixes.\n3012\t  Verify the published `v2` decision on the direct vector load and on\n3013\t  the vector each of the two AST lanes (row lane, datom lane) produces;\n3014\t  there is no projected vector load (§7.3.4).\n3015\t  Include an index hit, carried code, a `dao.jing` miss/hit path, a valid\n3016\t  vector under a wrong index entry, a correctly addressed malformed\n3017\t  vector, a missing address, an old stamp, and two valid images claiming\n3018\t  one live local id.\n3019\t- Action and expected outcome: M3 `step`/`fetch` transfers and verifies\n3020\t  each format over ring buffers; the two equal vectors address equally,\n3021\t  the unequal vectors do not, and a `:sha256` address still verifies under\n3022\t  its named algorithm. M3 also passes linker section 9's full refusal\n3023\t  matrix, staged-response, invalid-request, and traffic-equivalence\n3024\t  tests without a function or handle on the link stream. M4 lower selects\n3025\t  by stamp then address, verifies hash and grammar, and gives B0-equal\n3026\t  execution on all three hosts.\n3027\t  Wrong index content is linker `:hash-mismatch` at M3 or\n3028\t  `:yin.k/hash-mismatch` at UCF lower; malformed code is\n3029\t  `:yin.k/undecodable` naming the pc; a miss is `:yin.k/unsatisfied`;\n3030\t  an incompatible stamp is `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`. No check may\n3031\t  redirect an existing local id or bypass the common validator. The\n3032\t  falsy-prefix case either normalizes identically on the direct load and\n3033\t  on both AST lanes or is refused as inadmissible before an address is\n3034\t  minted.\n3035\t- Landing and order: M3 lands the stream and format-identity gate;\n3036\t  M4 closes UCF identity after M3 and the published normalization\n3037\t  decision, before any lifted image is run.\n3038\t  The already landed `v2` history (dbae125b), canonical CBOR\n3039\t  (3ddaa21b), and Phase 1 vector/stamp (f51077f2) supply the names and\n3040\t  bytes, not the cross-host lowering proof. Landed linker design\n3041\t  (1f7990d5, sections 5 and 9) specifies M2's four format records and\n3042\t  refusal tests; the records must pass those tests before M3 starts.\n3043\t\n3044\t**Safepoint reconstruction (7.4; blocker 1).**\n3045\t\n3046\t- Invariant: At every declared parking transition, lifting and lowering\n3047\t  preserve the next observable step and its wait, while other pcs refuse.\n3048\t- Setup: Run the corpus to every 7.4.1 row: explicit park, blocked\n3049\t  stream read, blocked write, sent FFI, retained FFI, ordinary and tail\n3050\t  effectful calls, and halt; also exercise M4's `:link-request`,\n3051\t  `:link-response`, and `:install` pending variants (linker section 11\n3052\t  item 12). Exercise distinct activation depths and captured environments\n3053\t  at one pc, a nonempty ready queue, a nonsafepoint pc, and a kept response\n3054\t  cursor shared by two waiters. Omit the portable cursor profile for the\n3055\t  refusal case. Separately prepare the three engines intended to\n3056\t  reconstruct canonical frames (the semantic-register kernel; the\n3057\t  register kernel; the stack kernel after its tail-completion seam) with\n3058\t  a nontrivial call stack, a captured closure, and a blocked effect at\n3059\t  the handoff point.\n3060\t- Action and expected outcome: On each host, lift a parked task and lower\n3061\t  into a fresh VM, then compare its result and effect trace with the\n3062\t  reference machine. Before the full corpus, lift that handoff from one\n3063\t  engine and lower it in the other; the canonical row set after\n3064\t  re-emission, the pending wait, next result and effect trace must agree\n3065\t  across all three and in every lowering direction. Static\n3066\t  `:yin.safepoint/kinds`, `def`/`use`/`live`, and lexical reads agree with\n3067\t  the reference trace. The observed parked record, wait entry, and effect\n3068\t  outcome determine `:yin.k/reason` and `:yin.k/pending`, never the\n3069\t  static kind alone.\n3070\t  Test both FFI states and each call effect. `lift(lower(frame))` must\n3071\t  reproduce the canonical frame. An undeclared pc gives\n3072\t  `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; queued work gives `:yin.k/not-quiescent`.\n3073\t  Insufficient pending evidence refuses before publication, as does a\n3074\t  missing portable cursor profile. Halt yields a result, not a frame.\n3075\t- Reflection acceptance (`dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md`, slice 8): Park a\n3076\t  continuation holding a string-backed local `dao.stream` with a kept\n3077\t  cursor, then migrate to another network node. The exporter\n3078\t  mechanically serves that stream by entering it in its table; the cursor cell\n3079\t  descriptor is a remote descriptor and the cursor cell carries the kept\n3080\t  cursor, and the receiver holds a reflection on the original logical\n3081\t  stream. Reads resume at the kept position. Force retention loss and\n3082\t  verify the reflection returns `:dao.stream/gap` with the source's\n3083\t  successor cursor, without replay or silent skip. Repeat with a source\n3084\t  that cannot honor the profile: lift refuses `:yin.k/unsatisfied`; a\n3085\t  reflection marked gone on lower is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the\n3086\t  stream. The exporter's entry and any relay pair the route depends on\n3087\t  are lease-governed, and a resumer that arrives after reclaim observes\n3088\t  `not-found`.\n3089\t- Landing and order: M4 covers the early two-engine gate, the table,\n3090\t  reference-machine parity, and the cross-host reflection proof after code\n3091\t  identity; the kept-cursor proof no longer waits for post-M5 hardening.\n3092\t  M4 must land the mirror step and reflection of `dao.stream.remote.md`,\n3093\t  not just reuse a native network stream.\n3094\t  Before export, specify the no-wait explicit-park representation and any\n3095\t  `:call-effect` pending shape; test them or remove that reason from the\n3096\t  wire contract. The `:reasons` Option B ruling in\n3097\t  `yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md` section 8 is landed design, not a runtime\n3098\t  fix: M4 replaces it with static `:kinds` and tests dynamic reasons.\n3099\t  A foreign engine claiming conformance runs the same frame parity harness.\n3100\t\n3101\t**Recursive portable encoding (7.5; blocker 2).**\n3102\t\n3103\t- Invariant: Every reachable portable value returns with its meaning and\n3104\t  aliasing intact, and every unsupported or malformed value refuses as a\n3105\t  whole with a precise path before execution.\n3106\t- Setup: Park frames with nested literal maps that look like UCF tags,\n3107\t  map keys containing values, closures, reified and parked continuations,\n3108\t  profiled primitives, shared subvalues, stream refs, and two cursor refs\n3109\t  that share a cursor cell beside two independent cursor cells at the same position.\n3110\t  Add a host object, unnamed or ambiguous function, host-state primitive,\n3111\t  cyclic value, forged resource ref, missing table ref, cyclic ref data,\n3112\t  extra table entry, and a correctly hashed malformed marker.\n3113\t- Action and expected outcome: Lift, transfer, and lower on each host;\n3114\t  literals remain literals, repeated refs share one value, independent\n3115\t  cursor cells advance independently, and shared cursor cells advance in wait\n3116\t  order. The lift refuses unsupported leaves as `:yin.k/non-portable`\n3117\t  with path and kind and publishes no partial value; decode refuses\n3118\t  malformed markers and refs as `:yin.k/undecodable` with path before\n3119\t  restoring state. Forged resource refs refuse before lift, while valid\n3120\t  refs re-seal on lower into the receiver's private resource table. A\n3121\t  same-named primitive with a different profile is `:yin.k/unsatisfied`,\n3122\t  never substituted by name alone.\n3123\t- Landing and order: M4 supplies the recursive codec, published standard\n3124\t  primitive profiles (including effectful `yin/def` and `require`), and\n3125\t  linker section 11 item 12's `:yin.k/binding`, `:yin.k/store-of`,\n3126\t  private-resource, and sealed-reference variants before lift uses them.\n3127\t  M4 also proves the string-backed stream reflection and kept-cursor case in\n3128\t  the safepoint row before cross-host values are accepted. Phase 1\n3129\t  canonicalization and M2 format records do not encode frames or values.\n3130\t\n3131\t**Ownership arbitration (7.7; blocker 4).**\n3132\t\n3133\t- Invariant: One occurrence has at most one admitted holder, and each\n3134\t  enrolled protected effect commits at most once across re-grants.\n3135\t- Setup: Copy one checkpoint, including two valid encodings with one\n3136\t  occurrence id, to two readers and leave the source's blocked writer\n3137\t  wakeable. Use a transactable arbitration space, a grantor ledger,\n3138\t  enrolled transactional consumer, and durable op-id intent and result\n3139\t  records. Inject publication failure, retry, stale lease, consumer\n3140\t  partition, and crashes after the successor append, resumed report, and\n3141\t  release. After a crash, evict a kept-cursor value so the re-granted run\n3142\t  observes `gap` and attempts a different effect at the same sequence.\n3143\t- Action and expected outcome: M4's exporting transition detaches waits\n3144\t  before publication; polling or direct resume of the exporting source\n3145\t  cannot append, and retry preserves the occurrence. With arbitration,\n3146\t  only a granted holder runs; competitors get `:yin.k/not-holder` or\n3147\t  `:yin.k/awaiting-grant`. Reclaim advances the epoch; stale effects fail\n3148\t  at commitment. At the enrolled consumer, epoch check, operation-intent\n3149\t  check, dedup record, and side-effect commitment share one atomic\n3150\t  boundary: equal id and intent return the stored result without a second\n3151\t  commit; equal id with divergent intent refuses and commits nothing.\n3152\t  A re-grant either replays durable inputs or fails closed on divergence.\n3153\t  The grantor's ledger alone completes the occurrence. A partitioned\n3154\t  protected consumer suspends admission. Without enrollment, the stream\n3155\t  declares at-least-once or fail-stop behavior; without a durable\n3156\t  transactable authority the composition offers `:yin.k/fork` only.\n3157\t- Landing and order: M4 must land and test exporting before any lift\n3158\t  publishes. Exclusive custody, epoch admission, crash recovery, and the\n3159\t  `yin.repl.core` handoff composition close in post-M5 hardening, after\n3160\t  code, state, and pending waits can round-trip. Until then an unfenced\n3161\t  stream declares at-least-once delivery, not exclusive custody.\n3162\t\n3163\t**Dependency closure (7.6; blocker 3).**\n3164\t\n3165\t- Invariant: A resumer runs only when every reachable dependency is\n3166\t  declared and satisfied, using an isolated store that cannot inherit a\n3167\t  receiver task's bindings.\n3168\t- Setup: Make code that reaches names through `:store-get` and\n3169\t  `:store-put`, a dynamically called closure, two activations of one\n3170\t  address with different captured environments, a transitive module\n3171\t  closure, and a parked-id reference. Give the receiver a conflicting\n3172\t  store binding and a different FFI pair. Omit in turn a segment, module\n3173\t  footprint, primitive profile, stream attachment, and parked record.\n3174\t- Action and expected outcome: Lift computes the fixed point over\n3175\t  address-context pairs and the reachable store slice; lowering checks\n3176\t  every requirement before restoring. The resumed task reads the carried\n3177\t  binding, uses the receiver's pair only for future calls, routes pending\n3178\t  calls to their carried endpoints, and shares no task-local module store.\n3179\t  A missing segment reports `:blocked`; an undeclared footprint reports\n3180\t  `:incomplete`; missing requirements yield `:yin.k/unsatisfied` with\n3181\t  the computed discovery state and missing set. A missing active-value\n3182\t  parked ref refuses lift as `:yin.k/non-portable` with\n3183\t  `:yin.k/kind :foreign-parked-ref`. No partial lower occurs.\n3184\t- Landing and order: M4 must test linker obligations, manifest store\n3185\t  footprints, transitive child-install slices, and isolated lowering\n3186\t  after encoding and code identity; linker section 11 criteria 15 and 23\n3187\t  are the minimum module cases. The full UCF fixed point, scheduler\n3188\t  parked records and fresh-name state, and all missing-dependency modes\n3189\t  close in post-M5 hardening. M2 format records verify link payloads;\n3190\t  they do not prove the task's transitive runtime closure.\n3191\t\n3192\t**Fenced custody, version 1 (7.2.1, 7.4.3, 7.7.8, 7.9; amendment r5).**\n3193\t\n3194\tThis block adds obligations; it changes no row above. The safepoint\n3195\tand portable-encoding rows keep their M4 kept-cursor and reflection\n3196\tgate with its meaning and its evidence as written. The stage-1\n3197\thandoff tests that landed with M-next A (80b59233,\n3198\t`test/yin/vm/ucf/handoff_test.cljc`) are recorded by\n3199\t`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.1.3 and by `yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md` I-5;\n3200\tthey are not moved here and nothing below relies on them as custody\n3201\tevidence. The ownership row keeps its own contract; the rows below\n3202\tare the wire-level clauses that row's tests must also exercise.\n3203\t\n3204\t- Invariant: A version-1 body, envelope, binding, and admission\n3205\t  outcome mean the same on JVM, Node, and Dart, and every breach of\n3206\t  their grammar is a data outcome before a machine is restored or an\n3207\t  effect is committed.\n3208\t- What M-next B claims: the grammar is published. It lands no code,\n3209\t  passes no test, and closes no row. `handoff-version` is still 0.\n3210\t- How each clause is tested on three hosts: by canonical byte\n3211\t  fixtures decoded on each host, so a rule about integer kind or key\n3212\t  presence is checked on the bytes and not on a host number type;\n3213\t  and by the ordered host pairs of `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.1.1\n3214\t  where a value crosses. Outcomes are compared as data.\n3215\t\n3216\tClauses, with the stage that owes the evidence\n3217\t(`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.3):\n3218\t\n3219\t1. Version gate (7.2.1). Fixtures: version 2, absent, a float `1.0`,\n3220\t   a version-0 child in a version-1 root. Assert\n3221\t   `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` with the found and supported versions,\n3222\t   or `:yin.k/undecodable` for the mixed tree, with zero attach\n3223\t   calls, zero proposals, and no machine: recursive validation has\n3224\t   no side effect. The integral float is refused on Node as on the\n3225\t   others. A version-0-only reader refuses a version-1 body. A\n3226\t   version-0 body still lowers as a fork on a reader that speaks\n3227\t   both; a composition requiring exclusive refuses it. Stage: D.\n3228\t2. Custody header (7.2.1). Fixtures omit each required key, give a\n3229\t   fork policy, a nil occurrence, an origin equal to the body's\n3230\t   occurrence, and header keys other than the origin on a halted\n3231\t   root. Root and embedded child are distinguished: any header key\n3232\t   on a child, a halted child with an origin included, is refused,\n3233\t   and a halted child without one validates. Assert\n3234\t   `:yin.k/undecodable` with the path. Stage: D.\n3235\t3. Sequence state (7.7.8). Assign-before-append, one increment,\n3236\t   retention across `full`, unknown effect, and `:suspended`; the\n3237\t   counter restored exactly; children drawing from the root.\n3238\t   Stage: D for assignment and restoration; C for durable input\n3239\t   replay; E for crash and regrant through the composition.\n3240\t4. Carried ids (7.4.3, 7.7.8). An id crosses park, bytes, and lower\n3241\t   unchanged on `:put`, `:ffi-request`, and `:link-request`, and the\n3242\t   retry is fenced. Fixtures: an id on `:next`, a sequence at or\n3243\t   above the counter, a duplicate id, an id in a body without an\n3244\t   origin, an id naming the body's own occurrence. Assert\n3245\t   `:yin.k/undecodable`. Protection mismatch in each direction is\n3246\t   `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream; a first exclusive export\n3247\t   over an attempted write is `:unprotected-pending`. Stage: D for\n3248\t   these structural checks. Stage: C for authoritative membership\n3249\t   and ancestry: an ancestor id the granted checkpoint never\n3250\t   carried is refused, a carried one is admitted, a child's carried\n3251\t   id counts, and an orphan successor gives no ancestry.\n3252\t5. Explicit park and install completeness (7.4.3), both versions.\n3253\t   A `:park` or `:call-effect` reason and an `:install` pending\n3254\t   without its entry refuse at decode; a `:parked` body's frames\n3255\t   restore in order; a response that fails verification and a phase\n3256\t   outside `:running` and `:parked` refuse; each of the two phases\n3257\t   crosses with each child kind `:blocked`, `:parked` and `:halted`,\n3258\t   a `:running` phase over a blocked child and an explicitly parked\n3259\t   child included, and lower preserves both fields; a runnable\n3260\t   child refuses export; a restored child continues from its saved\n3261\t   state without rerunning initialization or replaying the link\n3262\t   request. Version 1 adds: phase and parent required, and a\n3263\t   child's carried ids checked in the root's context. The two\n3264\t   version-0 defects of `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.3 are fixed with\n3265\t   version-0 tests. Stage: D.\n3266\t6. Envelope (7.7.8). Fixtures drop each key, change the dispatch\n3267\t   value, give a wrong author, an unbound lease, and a foreign id,\n3268\t   and nest an envelope-shaped program value. Assert each defective\n3269\t   form commits nothing, records nothing, and yields the structured\n3270\t   diagnostic with its defect and provenance and no admission\n3271\t   outcome; absent attribution is `:wrong-author` and an intent\n3272\t   that cannot be canonically encoded is `:malformed`; a failed or\n3273\t   backpressured diagnostic append admits nothing and is itself a\n3274\t   driver outcome; the nested value arrives as payload; no envelope key\n3275\t   appears in any body. Stage: C for the consumer, D for the writer.\n3276\t7. Epoch binding (7.7.8). First grant binds 0; each reclaim raises\n3277\t   the epoch by one in the lapse's transaction; a successor starts\n3278\t   at 0. A binding by another author, without authenticated\n3279\t   common-transaction evidence, duplicated for one lease, or with a\n3280\t   float epoch establishes nothing. Reopen the ledger and assert\n3281\t   the epoch is recovered and raised, never reset. Stage: C for the\n3282\t   authority facts and their atomic provenance, including through a\n3283\t   remote reflection; D for the holder, which answers\n3284\t   `:yin.k/not-holder` and releases on an invalid binding.\n3285\t8. Admission order and outcomes (7.7.8, 7.9). One fixture per check\n3286\t   in order: unreadable authority, wrong author, stale epoch, stale\n3287\t   lease, closed occurrence, a foreign id, an inherited id from a\n3288\t   closed ancestor, equal intent, different intent, fresh commit.\n3289\t   Add a cross-target conflict: one id sent to a second enrolled\n3290\t   target answers `:intent-conflict` from the shared namespace and\n3291\t   quarantines the occurrence. Add forged outcomes: a `:committed`\n3292\t   and an `:intent-conflict` from an unattributed author discharge\n3293\t   nothing and quarantine nothing. Add an unknown-effect transport\n3294\t   error, cut both before and after the remote commit: the id is\n3295\t   retained, the retry goes through the fenced boundary, and it\n3296\t   commits once or replays the result already held. Add an\n3297\t   unreadable accepted checkpoint: an inherited id answers\n3298\t   `:suspended` with tenure, quarantine, and dedup state unchanged,\n3299\t   and a stale holder still answers `:stale`. Add a snapshot\n3300\t   variant that keeps an id and changes its intent or the root\n3301\t   counter: the authority refuses it. Assert the exact\n3302\t   outcome map of 7.9 for each and that stale wins over an existing\n3303\t   record. Stage: C for the consumer, D for the driver.\n3304\t9. Bounds (7.7.8). A counter at 2^52-1 assigns nothing and refuses\n3305\t   export. An epoch at 2^52-1 is tested twice: while valid, its\n3306\t   grant activates and its effects commit; after the reclaim that\n3307\t   would increment it, the lapse is recorded, nothing is granted,\n3308\t   no successor is eligible, and admission answers `:suspended`\n3309\t   before tenure. The values 2^52 and -1 are refused on the bytes.\n3310\t   Stage: C for the epoch, D for the sequence.\n3311\t10. Composition (linker 14.2.4). The crash, partition, reclaim, and\n3312\t    successor-completion contracts run through the wired handoff\n3313\t    composition on both host matrices, with crash cuts around\n3314\t    completion and around result delivery. Stage: E.\n3315\t\n3316\tCanonical fixtures prove grammar parity. They do not prove\n3317\tatomicity, attribution, or recovery; those need the durable\n3318\ttransactional seam of `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.2.4.\n3319\t\n3320\t(M-next C, slice C12.) The stage-C gate proves the substrate: every\n3321\tauthority transition under every journal crash cut reopens to its\n3322\tpre-state or its post-state with only whole transactions and a\n3323\tconverging retry, and one ledger's frames are byte-identical across\n3324\thosts. It does not close clause 10: crash, partition, and\n3325\tsuccessor-completion through the wired composition, with real kills,\n3326\tremain stage E.\n3327\t\n3328\tNot claimed by this amendment, and still owed by the ownership row:\n3329\texclusive custody itself, atomic admission, crash recovery, input\n3330\treplay, and the `yin.repl.core` handoff composition. Governance\n3331\trecovery, after quarantine, exhaustion, or lost authority state,\n3332\tstays outside the automatic protocol.\n3333\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"23e67cb0-f183-4c2e-b3d6-2e5b6c44b814","uuid":"040b4cdb-e6c2-4f63-a90c-5822b55a72b3","timestamp":"2026-10-13T08:31:48.175Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7plan/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md","content":"### Holder driver scheduling and retained inboxes (D15a)\n\nThe holder exposes four composition-local entries over the existing driver\nstate: `control-step`, `program-step`, `stop`, and `owed-control-write?`.\n`step` remains the normal-operation convenience entry, using the same machinery\nwith one renewal and one program cycle per tick. These entries change no\nhandoff body, authority fact, lease fact, or DaoStream outcome vocabulary.\n\n`control-step` retains incoming observations and handles authenticated custody\nanswers, renewals, retries, reports, releases, and closure evidence. It never\nlowers a newly granted checkpoint, attaches program resources, applies program\nresults, executes guest code, replays inputs, emits program effects, observes\nprogram streams, or prepares an export. A newly accepted grant remains in\n`:activating` with its original lease observation basis and no machine.\n\n`program-step` independently checks complete authenticated evidence, the same\nlive occurrence/lease/epoch, and fresh `lease/holding?` before activation,\nexecution, and retained-result application. Unavailable evidence suspends;\ncontradiction or expiry uses the existing diagnostic/cleanup run end. The\nper-effect tenure guards remain in force, including across computation and\nbefore replay. Export preparation and capture belong here; established\noffer/report/release/closure brackets belong to control progress. Activation\nuses fresh evidence without refreshing the original tenure basis; authenticated\nrenewals and the maximum cap still determine the current bound.\n\nAssembly requires both `:reply-inbox` and `:outcome-inbox`, each:\n\n```clojure\n{:version 1 :identity portable-stable-identity\n :read-at! (fn [position] result)}\n```\n\nThe operation is non-destructive over dense exact portable positions. Its\nclosed result union is `{:status :record :position n :author a :record r}`,\n`{:status :empty}` at the currently available tail, or\n`{:status :unavailable}` for missing, gapped, or inaccessible history. Empty\ndoes not advance. Positions cannot wrap or advance beyond the journal bound.\nThis version requires complete retention throughout recovery; it has no\ntruncation or acknowledgment protocol. Legacy destructive readers are rejected,\nnot automatically spooled after consumption.\n\nEither lane's unavailable answer suspends program progress and new activation,\nnot valid custody control or cleanup. Complete authenticated ledger evidence\nand the same live binding remain necessary to schedule renewal. An unavailable\nreply position ends that tick's reply-first scan without consuming the outcome\nlane; due renewals and pending requests still send or retry unchanged. Inbound\nacceptance credits no renewal: the request identity and original pre-send\nreading remain pending until authenticated carriage passes the before-and-after\ntenure checks. A delayed reply cannot revive a run past its local bound.\nExpiry still gates the machine ended and emits one run-end diagnostic, with a\ndurable release intent before sending and unchanged cleanup retries until\nauthenticated carriage. Availability obstruction remains observable until a\nsuccessful scan; malformed/conflicting evidence and journal uncertainty are\nnot this permissive case. Reopening a journaled holder may recover cleanup\nwhile receipt-source reconciliation is unavailable, retaining the receipts\nwithout dispatch until reconciliation succeeds. It never restores execution.\n\nSelection is reply-first. Before dispatch or source advancement the driver\ndurably appends `:yin.k/journal :yin.k/inbox`, carrying `:yin.k/lane`,\n`:yin.k/identity`, `:yin.k/position`, `:yin.k/author`, `:yin.k/record`, and,\nwhen present, `:yin.k/binding` naming the local occurrence, lease, and epoch.\nThe journal position is the merge order; there is no second persisted ordering\ncounter. Control records may pass earlier deferred program records. Deferred\nprogram order and selected control order are preserved independently.\n\nAn uncertain append stalls both step entries until reopen reconciles it.\nReceipt reconciliation deduplicates identical lane/identity/position records\nand rejects conflicting canonical contents, changed sources, and gaps. Source\npositions and queues are derived from those records; a surviving machine\nconsumes each queued observation once. There is no persisted VM-applied bit.\nAfter process restart the existing checkpoint/regrant/replay rules still govern;\nold-lease receipts never authorize a fresh run. Persisted clock readings do not\nre-establish tenure.\n\n`stop` is an irreversible, idempotent local latch, not a machine gate or an\nexport abort. Program steps thereafter do nothing. Control ticks authenticate\nlate grants and release them without lower, suppress fresh/replacement/recovery\ncandidacy, and drain already established control obligations. An unfinished\nprogram/export cannot invent a completion to keep its lease alive. Cleanup\nattempts retain their request identity and intent-before-send journal brackets.\nThe composition owns the bounded drain budget, judge/front progress, and\nreapplying the local latch when reopening during shutdown.\n\n`owed-control-write?` is pure and total: proposals, pending renewals, releases,\neligible offers/reports, and required diagnostic/cleanup remain owed until the\nappropriate authenticated evidence discharges them. Inbound acceptance alone\ndoes not discharge a bracket. Future renewal deadlines, passive ledger waits,\nand uncertain-journal stalls are not executable writes.\nPending eligible renewal or release remains owed during inbox unavailability;\nunavailable history alone is not an owed write. D15 owns production\npositional adapters and REPL integration; memory-backed test reconstruction is\nsimulated process recovery, not proof of durable media.\n\n### 7.11.1 Blocker-closure acceptance matrix\n\nThese are observable test contracts, not a declaration that the blockers\nare closed. M3 and M4 tests run on JVM, CLJS (Node), and CLJD. A named\nrefusal must be a data outcome before a code image is loaded, a machine is\nrestored, or an effect is committed, as applicable. The M4 lift driver\nmust consume the UCF table amendments in linker section 11 item 12 before\nits round-trip tests run. Full UCF acceptance waits for every row,\nincluding the post-M5 gates; passing a linker milestone alone is not a\nclaim that all five blockers have closed.\n\n**Code identity (7.3; blocker 5).**\n\n- Invariant: A resumer executes the instruction stream named by the\n  continuation's address and contract stamp, or refuses before loading it.\n- Setup: On each host, make equal resolved vectors from batches with\n  different entity ids, order, provenance, and omitted defaults; make\n  unequal vectors by reversing repeated single-valued operands. Include\n  a `:gensym` prefix of `false` beside absent and explicit `\"id\"` prefixes.\n  Verify the published `v2` decision on the direct vector load and on\n  the vector each of the two AST lanes (row lane, datom lane) produces;\n  there is no projected vector load (§7.3.4).\n  Include an index hit, carried code, a `dao.jing` miss/hit path, a valid\n  vector under a wrong index entry, a correctly addressed malformed\n  vector, a missing address, an old stamp, and two valid images claiming\n  one live local id.\n- Action and expected outcome: M3 `step`/`fetch` transfers and verifies\n  each format over ring buffers; the two equal vectors address equally,\n  the unequal vectors do not, and a `:sha256` address still verifies under\n  its named algorithm. M3 also passes linker section 9's full refusal\n  matrix, staged-response, invalid-request, and traffic-equivalence\n  tests without a function or handle on the link stream. M4 lower selects\n  by stamp then address, verifies hash and grammar, and gives B0-equal\n  execution on all three hosts.\n  Wrong index content is linker `:hash-mismatch` at M3 or\n  `:yin.k/hash-mismatch` at UCF lower; malformed code is\n  `:yin.k/undecodable` naming the pc; a miss is `:yin.k/unsatisfied`;\n  an incompatible stamp is `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`. No check may\n  redirect an existing local id or bypass the common validator. The\n  falsy-prefix case either normalizes identically on the direct load and\n  on both AST lanes or is refused as inadmissible before an address is\n  minted.\n- Landing and order: M3 lands the stream and format-identity gate;\n  M4 closes UCF identity after M3 and the published normalization\n  decision, before any lifted image is run.\n  The already landed `v2` history (dbae125b), canonical CBOR\n  (3ddaa21b), and Phase 1 vector/stamp (f51077f2) supply the names and\n  bytes, not the cross-host lowering proof. Landed linker design\n  (1f7990d5, sections 5 and 9) specifies M2's four format records and\n  refusal tests; the records must pass those tests before M3 starts.\n\n**Safepoint reconstruction (7.4; blocker 1).**\n\n- Invariant: At every declared parking transition, lifting and lowering\n  preserve the next observable step and its wait, while other pcs refuse.\n- Setup: Run the corpus to every 7.4.1 row: explicit park, blocked\n  stream read, blocked write, sent FFI, retained FFI, ordinary and tail\n  effectful calls, and halt; also exercise M4's `:link-request`,\n  `:link-response`, and `:install` pending variants (linker section 11\n  item 12). Exercise distinct activation depths and captured environments\n  at one pc, a nonempty ready queue, a nonsafepoint pc, and a kept response\n  cursor shared by two waiters. Omit the portable cursor profile for the\n  refusal case. Separately prepare the three engines intended to\n  reconstruct canonical frames (the semantic-register kernel; the\n  register kernel; the stack kernel after its tail-completion seam) with\n  a nontrivial call stack, a captured closure, and a blocked effect at\n  the handoff point.\n- Action and expected outcome: On each host, lift a parked task and lower\n  into a fresh VM, then compare its result and effect trace with the\n  reference machine. Before the full corpus, lift that handoff from one\n  engine and lower it in the other; the canonical row set after\n  re-emission, the pending wait, next result and effect trace must agree\n  across all three and in every lowering direction. Static\n  `:yin.safepoint/kinds`, `def`/`use`/`live`, and lexical reads agree with\n  the reference trace. The observed parked record, wait entry, and effect\n  outcome determine `:yin.k/reason` and `:yin.k/pending`, never the\n  static kind alone.\n  Test both FFI states and each call effect. `lift(lower(frame))` must\n  reproduce the canonical frame. An undeclared pc gives\n  `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; queued work gives `:yin.k/not-quiescent`.\n  Insufficient pending evidence refuses before publication, as does a\n  missing portable cursor profile. Halt yields a result, not a frame.\n- Reflection acceptance (`dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md`, slice 8): Park a\n  continuation holding a string-backed local `dao.stream` with a kept\n  cursor, then migrate to another network node. The exporter\n  mechanically serves that stream by entering it in its table; the cursor cell\n  descriptor is a remote descriptor and the cursor cell carries the kept\n  cursor, and the receiver holds a reflection on the original logical\n  stream. Reads resume at the kept position. Force retention loss and\n  verify the reflection returns `:dao.stream/gap` with the source's\n  successor cursor, without replay or silent skip. Repeat with a source\n  that cannot honor the profile: lift refuses `:yin.k/unsatisfied`; a\n  reflection marked gone on lower is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the\n  stream. The exporter's entry and any relay pair the route depends on\n  are lease-governed, and a resumer that arrives after reclaim observes\n  `not-found`.\n- Landing and order: M4 covers the early two-engine gate, the table,\n  reference-machine parity, and the cross-host reflection proof after code\n  identity; the kept-cursor proof no longer waits for post-M5 hardening.\n  M4 must land the mirror step and reflection of `dao.stream.remote.md`,\n  not just reuse a native network stream.\n  Before export, specify the no-wait explicit-park representation and any\n  `:call-effect` pending shape; test them or remove that reason from the\n  wire contract. The `:reasons` Option B ruling in\n  `yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md` section 8 is landed design, not a runtime\n  fix: M4 replaces it with static `:kinds` and tests dynamic reasons.\n  A foreign engine claiming conformance runs the same frame parity harness.\n\n**Recursive portable encoding (7.5; blocker 2).**\n\n- Invariant: Every reachable portable value returns with its meaning and\n  aliasing intact, and every unsupported or malformed value refuses as a\n  whole with a precise path before execution.\n- Setup: Park frames with nested literal maps that look like UCF tags,\n  map keys containing values, closures, reified and parked continuations,\n  profiled primitives, shared subvalues, stream refs, and two cursor refs\n  that share a cursor cell beside two independent cursor cells at the same position.\n  Add a host object, unnamed or ambiguous function, host-state primitive,\n  cyclic value, forged resource ref, missing table ref, cyclic ref data,\n  extra table entry, and a correctly hashed malformed marker.\n- Action and expected outcome: Lift, transfer, and lower on each host;\n  literals remain literals, repeated refs share one value, independent\n  cursor cells advance independently, and shared cursor cells advance in wait\n  order. The lift refuses unsupported leaves as `:yin.k/non-portable`\n  with path and kind and publishes no partial value; decode refuses\n  malformed markers and refs as `:yin.k/undecodable` with path before\n  restoring state. Forged resource refs refuse before lift, while valid\n  refs re-seal on lower into the receiver's private resource table. A\n  same-named primitive with a different profile is `:yin.k/unsatisfied`,\n  never substituted by name alone.\n- Landing and order: M4 supplies the recursive codec, published standard\n  primitive profiles (including effectful `yin/def` and `require`), and\n  linker section 11 item 12's `:yin.k/binding`, `:yin.k/store-of`,\n  private-resource, and sealed-reference variants before lift uses them.\n  M4 also proves the string-backed stream reflection and kept-cursor case in\n  the safepoint row before cross-host values are accepted. Phase 1\n  canonicalization and M2 format records do not encode frames or values.\n\n**Ownership arbitration (7.7; blocker 4).**\n\n- Invariant: One occurrence has at most one admitted holder, and each\n  enrolled protected effect commits at most once across re-grants.\n- Setup: Copy one checkpoint, including two valid encodings with one\n  occurrence id, to two readers and leave the source's blocked writer\n  wakeable. Use a transactable arbitration space, a grantor ledger,\n  enrolled transactional consumer, and durable op-id intent and result\n  records. Inject publication failure, retry, stale lease, consumer\n  partition, and crashes after the successor append, resumed report, and\n  release. After a crash, evict a kept-cursor value so the re-granted run\n  observes `gap` and attempts a different effect at the same sequence.\n- Action and expected outcome: M4's exporting transition detaches waits\n  before publication; polling or direct resume of the exporting source\n  cannot append, and retry preserves the occurrence. With arbitration,\n  only a granted holder runs; competitors get `:yin.k/not-holder` or\n  `:yin.k/awaiting-grant`. Reclaim advances the epoch; stale effects fail\n  at commitment. At the enrolled consumer, epoch check, operation-intent\n  check, dedup record, and side-effect commitment share one atomic\n  boundary: equal id and intent return the stored result without a second\n  commit; equal id with divergent intent refuses and commits nothing.\n  A re-grant either replays durable inputs or fails closed on divergence.\n  The grantor's ledger alone completes the occurrence. A partitioned\n  protected consumer suspends admission. Without enrollment, the stream\n  declares at-least-once or fail-stop behavior; without a durable\n  transactable authority the composition offers `:yin.k/fork` only.\n- Landing and order: M4 must land and test exporting before any lift\n  publishes. Exclusive custody, epoch admission, crash recovery, and the\n  `yin.repl.core` handoff composition close in post-M5 hardening, after\n  code, state, and pending waits can round-trip. Until then an unfenced\n  stream declares at-least-once delivery, not exclusive custody.\n\n**Dependency closure (7.6; blocker 3).**\n\n- Invariant: A resumer runs only when every reachable dependency is\n  declared and satisfied, using an isolated store that cannot inherit a\n  receiver task's bindings.\n- Setup: Make code that reaches names through `:store-get` and\n  `:store-put`, a dynamically called closure, two activations of one\n  address with different captured environments, a transitive module\n  closure, and a parked-id reference. Give the receiver a conflicting\n  store binding and a different FFI pair. Omit in turn a segment, module\n  footprint, primitive profile, stream attachment, and parked record.\n- Action and expected outcome: Lift computes the fixed point over\n  address-context pairs and the reachable store slice; lowering checks\n  every requirement before restoring. The resumed task reads the carried\n  binding, uses the receiver's pair only for future calls, routes pending\n  calls to their carried endpoints, and shares no task-local module store.\n  A missing segment reports `:blocked`; an undeclared footprint reports\n  `:incomplete`; missing requirements yield `:yin.k/unsatisfied` with\n  the computed discovery state and missing set. A missing active-value\n  parked ref refuses lift as `:yin.k/non-portable` with\n  `:yin.k/kind :foreign-parked-ref`. No partial lower occurs.\n- Landing and order: M4 must test linker obligations, manifest store\n  footprints, transitive child-install slices, and isolated lowering\n  after encoding and code identity; linker section 11 criteria 15 and 23\n  are the minimum module cases. The full UCF fixed point, scheduler\n  parked records and fresh-name state, and all missing-dependency modes\n  close in post-M5 hardening. M2 format records verify link payloads;\n  they do not prove the task's transitive runtime closure.\n\n**Fenced custody, version 1 (7.2.1, 7.4.3, 7.7.8, 7.9; amendment r5).**\n\nThis block adds obligations; it changes no row above. The safepoint\nand portable-encoding rows keep their M4 kept-cursor and reflection\ngate with its meaning and its evidence as written. The stage-1\nhandoff tests that landed with M-next A (80b59233,\n`test/yin/vm/ucf/handoff_test.cljc`) are recorded by\n`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.1.3 and by `yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md` I-5;\nthey are not moved here and nothing below relies on them as custody\nevidence. The ownership row keeps its own contract; the rows below\nare the wire-level clauses that row's tests must also exercise.\n\n- Invariant: A version-1 body, envelope, binding, and admission\n  outcome mean the same on JVM, Node, and Dart, and every breach of\n  their grammar is a data outcome before a machine is restored or an\n  effect is committed.\n- What M-next B claims: the grammar is published. It lands no code,\n  passes no test, and closes no row. `handoff-version` is still 0.\n- How each clause is tested on three hosts: by canonical byte\n  fixtures decoded on each host, so a rule about integer kind or key\n  presence is checked on the bytes and not on a host number type;\n  and by the ordered host pairs of `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.1.1\n  where a value crosses. Outcomes are compared as data.\n\nClauses, with the stage that owes the evidence\n(`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.3):\n\n1. Version gate (7.2.1). Fixtures: version 2, absent, a float `1.0`,\n   a version-0 child in a version-1 root. Assert\n   `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` with the found and supported versions,\n   or `:yin.k/undecodable` for the mixed tree, with zero attach\n   calls, zero proposals, and no machine: recursive validation has\n   no side effect. The integral float is refused on Node as on the\n   others. A version-0-only reader refuses a version-1 body. A\n   version-0 body still lowers as a fork on a reader that speaks\n   both; a composition requiring exclusive refuses it. Stage: D.\n2. Custody header (7.2.1). Fixtures omit each required key, give a\n   fork policy, a nil occurrence, an origin equal to the body's\n   occurrence, and header keys other than the origin on a halted\n   root. Root and embedded child are distinguished: any header key\n   on a child, a halted child with an origin included, is refused,\n   and a halted child without one validates. Assert\n   `:yin.k/undecodable` with the path. Stage: D.\n3. Sequence state (7.7.8). Assign-before-append, one increment,\n   retention across `full`, unknown effect, and `:suspended`; the\n   counter restored exactly; children drawing from the root.\n   Stage: D for assignment and restoration; C for durable input\n   replay; E for crash and regrant through the composition.\n4. Carried ids (7.4.3, 7.7.8). An id crosses park, bytes, and lower\n   unchanged on `:put`, `:ffi-request`, and `:link-request`, and the\n   retry is fenced. Fixtures: an id on `:next`, a sequence at or\n   above the counter, a duplicate id, an id in a body without an\n   origin, an id naming the body's own occurrence. Assert\n   `:yin.k/undecodable`. Protection mismatch in each direction is\n   `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream; a first exclusive export\n   over an attempted write is `:unprotected-pending`. Stage: D for\n   these structural checks. Stage: C for authoritative membership\n   and ancestry: an ancestor id the granted checkpoint never\n   carried is refused, a carried one is admitted, a child's carried\n   id counts, and an orphan successor gives no ancestry.\n5. Explicit park and install completeness (7.4.3), both versions.\n   A `:park` or `:call-effect` reason and an `:install` pending\n   without its entry refuse at decode; a `:parked` body's frames\n   restore in order; a response that fails verification and a phase\n   outside `:running` and `:parked` refuse; each of the two phases\n   crosses with each child kind `:blocked`, `:parked` and `:halted`,\n   a `:running` phase over a blocked child and an explicitly parked\n   child included, and lower preserves both fields; a runnable\n   child refuses export; a restored child continues from its saved\n   state without rerunning initialization or replaying the link\n   request. Version 1 adds: phase and parent required, and a\n   child's carried ids checked in the root's context. The two\n   version-0 defects of `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.3 are fixed with\n   version-0 tests. Stage: D.\n6. Envelope (7.7.8). Fixtures drop each key, change the dispatch\n   value, give a wrong author, an unbound lease, and a foreign id,\n   and nest an envelope-shaped program value. Assert each defective\n   form commits nothing, records nothing, and yields the structured\n   diagnostic with its defect and provenance and no admission\n   outcome; absent attribution is `:wrong-author` and an intent\n   that cannot be canonically encoded is `:malformed`; a failed or\n   backpressured diagnostic append admits nothing and is itself a\n   driver outcome; the nested value arrives as payload; no envelope key\n   appears in any body. Stage: C for the consumer, D for the writer.\n7. Epoch binding (7.7.8). First grant binds 0; each reclaim raises\n   the epoch by one in the lapse's transaction; a successor starts\n   at 0. A binding by another author, without authenticated\n   common-transaction evidence, duplicated for one lease, or with a\n   float epoch establishes nothing. Reopen the ledger and assert\n   the epoch is recovered and raised, never reset. Stage: C for the\n   authority facts and their atomic provenance, including through a\n   remote reflection; D for the holder, which answers\n   `:yin.k/not-holder` and releases on an invalid binding.\n8. Admission order and outcomes (7.7.8, 7.9). One fixture per check\n   in order: unreadable authority, wrong author, stale epoch, stale\n   lease, closed occurrence, a foreign id, an inherited id from a\n   closed ancestor, equal intent, different intent, fresh commit.\n   Add a cross-target conflict: one id sent to a second enrolled\n   target answers `:intent-conflict` from the shared namespace and\n   quarantines the occurrence. Add forged outcomes: a `:committed`\n   and an `:intent-conflict` from an unattributed author discharge\n   nothing and quarantine nothing. Add an unknown-effect transport\n   error, cut both before and after the remote commit: the id is\n   retained, the retry goes through the fenced boundary, and it\n   commits once or replays the result already held. Add an\n   unreadable accepted checkpoint: an inherited id answers\n   `:suspended` with tenure, quarantine, and dedup state unchanged,\n   and a stale holder still answers `:stale`. Add a snapshot\n   variant that keeps an id and changes its intent or the root\n   counter: the authority refuses it. Assert the exact\n   outcome map of 7.9 for each and that stale wins over an existing\n   record. Stage: C for the consumer, D for the driver.\n9. Bounds (7.7.8). A counter at 2^52-1 assigns nothing and refuses\n   export. An epoch at 2^52-1 is tested twice: while valid, its\n   grant activates and its effects commit; after the reclaim that\n   would increment it, the lapse is recorded, nothing is granted,\n   no successor is eligible, and admission answers `:suspended`\n   before tenure. The values 2^52 and -1 are refused on the bytes.\n   Stage: C for the epoch, D for the sequence.\n10. Composition (linker 14.2.4). The crash, partition, reclaim, and\n    successor-completion contracts run through the wired handoff\n    composition on both host matrices, with crash cuts around\n    completion and around result delivery. Stage: E.\n\nCanonical fixtures prove grammar parity. They do not prove\natomicity, attribution, or recovery; those need the durable\ntransactional seam of `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.2.4.\n\n(M-next C, slice C12.) The stage-C gate proves the substrate: every\nauthority transition under every journal crash cut reopens to its\npre-state or its post-state with only whole transactions and a\nconverging retry, and one ledger's frames are byte-identical across\nhosts. It does not close clause 10: crash, partition, and\nsuccessor-completion through the wired composition, with real kills,\nremain stage E.\n\nNot claimed by this amendment, and still owed by the ownership row:\nexclusive custody itself, atomic admission, crash recovery, input\nreplay, and the `yin.repl.core` handoff composition. Governance\nrecovery, after quarantine, exhaustion, or lost authority state,\nstays outside the automatic protocol.\n","numLines":434,"startLine":2900,"totalLines":3333}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_013chZ9uFNK7BVVyRijk3EpU","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011Cfyvc12xx9JuxkFtDRGqk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NdrQkNskVS26GhH9kGv6uR","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7plan/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md","offset":282,"limit":95},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":18705,"cache_read_input_tokens":56271,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":18705},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"23e67cb0-f183-4c2e-b3d6-2e5b6c44b814","uuid":"67f96c67-4f18-4dce-b051-99df103846f8","timestamp":"2026-10-13T08:31:48.922Z","request_id":"req_011CfyvbzkbqiVjAFmkGJrMN","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01NdrQkNskVS26GhH9kGv6uR":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7plan/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md","offset":282,"limit":95}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NdrQkNskVS26GhH9kGv6uR","type":"tool_result","content":"282\t### 2.5 Captures, parked records, module contexts, the parked table\n283\t\n284\t**DECIDED** (frozen §2.4; evaluator §1.6; draft §5.1, §5.4).\n285\t\n286\t- A **reified continuation** value is `[:row B]`, `B` a `:reified\n287\t  [act-cref]` row; the act has no pending, its `pc−1` is a\n288\t  `:current-continuation`, `rd` is that instruction's destination, the\n289\t  window is `saved(pc, rd)`.\n290\t- The **parked table** is `[:parked-table P₁ state-cref₁ P₂ state-cref₂ …]`\n291\t  sorted by `P`; a state is an `:act` whose `pc−1` is `:park` (an\n292\t  explicit park), **or** FFI bookkeeping under a call id `P` that a live\n293\t  `:ffi`/`:ffi-request` pending of the same task carries: an `:act` whose\n294\t  `pc−1` is `:ffi-call`, or a `:tail` whose site is the parking tail\n295\t  `:call`. An active `parked-id` never selects a bookkeeping record (v2\n296\t  amendment §7.3, kept).\n297\t- A **module context** is `E`'s `store-of`; the module store travels as\n298\t  `[:module-store M store-cref]`; every `store-of` resolves to a carried\n299\t  module store (draft §8 step 3).\n300\t- **Install children** are whole `:task` rows in the child role (draft\n301\t  §5.4, §5.5), unchanged.\n302\t\n303\t**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13), what travels and what does not**\n304\t(engineer deviations 4, 6, 7; codex round 1 accepted all three):\n305\t\n306\t- **The store slice is the whole root store.** `lift-task` walks every\n307\t  entry of `(:store m)` (`store-node`), and every parked record travels\n308\t  (`parked-table` over all of `(:parked m)`), each judged by P4. No\n309\t  reachability pruning of the store is performed. *Why:* the kernel\n310\t  holds no liveness information for store entries; the conservative\n311\t  slice is correct and the draft's \"exactly the reachable records\" was\n312\t  never implemented.\n313\t- **Module stores travel by reachability.** A `:module-store` row is\n314\t  emitted for every `store-of` an `E` or closure names, transitively\n315\t  through the values of stores already discovered (`module-store-nodes`);\n316\t  a named store the kernel lacks is `:yin.k/non-portable\n317\t  :missing-module-store`.\n318\t- **Linked registry entries do not travel.** The row table has no slot\n319\t  for the receiver's module registry; only module stores do. A name a\n320\t  carried closure resolves through the source's registry is a\n321\t  **composition prerequisite** of the receiver: row 17's receiver is\n322\t  given the source's registry value, and without it the relift refuses\n323\t  `:yin.k/unsatisfied {:obligations #{host.mod/f}}`. The receiver's\n324\t  registry is kept as given; there is no isolated-receiver pruning.\n325\t- **Custody is nil at the root in phase 6.** The lift emits `custody nil`\n326\t  (fork), and the lower refuses any non-nil custody as `:yin.k/unsupported\n327\t  :custody`; the exclusive and holder path is phase 7 (§8.4).\n328\t\n329\t### 2.6 The root (replacing draft §5.5 in two slots)\n330\t\n331\t**PROPOSED**, with the reviewer's correction of the free environment:\n332\t\n333\t```clojure\n334\t[T :task\n335\t   version          ; 3\n336\t   contract         ; {:yin.code/contract \"v4\" :yin.k/version 1}\n337\t   kind             ; :blocked | :parked | :halted\n338\t   role             ; :root | :child\n339\t   id-counter       ; N\n340\t   free-env-cref    ; NEW: a :bindings row, the task's initial free environment (§6.3)\n341\t   store-cref\n342\t   module-stores    ; crefs sorted by M\n343\t   parked-cref      ; :parked-table\n344\t   cursor-cells-cref       ; :cursor-cells census\n345\t   requires         ; plain: UCF §7.6.1's map plus :yin.k/trees (§6.1)\n346\t   code             ; plain: {A canonical-vector …}, \"v4\" vectors verified to hash to their key\n347\t   installs         ; crefs to :install rows, sorted by module name\n348\t   frames           ; crefs to :wait rows, in wait order     (:blocked, :parked)\n349\t   parked-id        ; P or nil                                (:parked only)\n350\t   result           ; V or nil                                (:halted only)\n351\t   custody]         ; plain, draft §5.5's table\n352\t```\n353\t\n354\tNo `layout` slot and no native image: those were the positional\n355\tprofiles' (v2 amendment §2, §4.1). The custody table of the draft §5.5\n356\tstands, with its version-0 comparison reworded (§10.3 R10).\n357\t**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13):** of that table only the fork rows are\n358\treachable in phase 6; the lift writes `custody nil` and the lower's step\n359\t10 (`check-custody!`) refuses any non-nil value `:yin.k/unsupported\n360\t:custody` (§2.5, §7.2). *Why:* the exclusive and holder path is phase\n361\t7's; carrying a custody arm no reader admits would be an untested slot.\n362\t\n363\t### 2.7 The bijection with the keyed frame\n364\t\n365\t**PROPOSED.** `rows->frame` and `frame->rows` (`yin.vm.ucf.rows`, §8.1)\n366\tare inverse on valid row sets and canonical keyed frames: `:act` ↔\n367\t`{:yin.k/state :act …}` with the deliver record synthesised from the\n368\tkind and `rd`; `:tail` ↔ `{:yin.k/state :tail …}`; `:window` ↔\n369\t`:yin.k/window` as a map (`[:lit nil]` ↔ a nil-valued key); `:k` ↔\n370\t`:yin.k/k` in order. `rows->frame` reshapes and decides nothing the\n371\tvalidator decides.\n372\t\n373\t---\n374\t\n375\t## 3. The frame validator\n376\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"23e67cb0-f183-4c2e-b3d6-2e5b6c44b814","uuid":"a6ccfd18-bac7-4112-9cbe-bfeb9441bdfb","timestamp":"2026-10-13T08:31:48.949Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7plan/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md","content":"### 2.5 Captures, parked records, module contexts, the parked table\n\n**DECIDED** (frozen §2.4; evaluator §1.6; draft §5.1, §5.4).\n\n- A **reified continuation** value is `[:row B]`, `B` a `:reified\n  [act-cref]` row; the act has no pending, its `pc−1` is a\n  `:current-continuation`, `rd` is that instruction's destination, the\n  window is `saved(pc, rd)`.\n- The **parked table** is `[:parked-table P₁ state-cref₁ P₂ state-cref₂ …]`\n  sorted by `P`; a state is an `:act` whose `pc−1` is `:park` (an\n  explicit park), **or** FFI bookkeeping under a call id `P` that a live\n  `:ffi`/`:ffi-request` pending of the same task carries: an `:act` whose\n  `pc−1` is `:ffi-call`, or a `:tail` whose site is the parking tail\n  `:call`. An active `parked-id` never selects a bookkeeping record (v2\n  amendment §7.3, kept).\n- A **module context** is `E`'s `store-of`; the module store travels as\n  `[:module-store M store-cref]`; every `store-of` resolves to a carried\n  module store (draft §8 step 3).\n- **Install children** are whole `:task` rows in the child role (draft\n  §5.4, §5.5), unchanged.\n\n**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13), what travels and what does not**\n(engineer deviations 4, 6, 7; codex round 1 accepted all three):\n\n- **The store slice is the whole root store.** `lift-task` walks every\n  entry of `(:store m)` (`store-node`), and every parked record travels\n  (`parked-table` over all of `(:parked m)`), each judged by P4. No\n  reachability pruning of the store is performed. *Why:* the kernel\n  holds no liveness information for store entries; the conservative\n  slice is correct and the draft's \"exactly the reachable records\" was\n  never implemented.\n- **Module stores travel by reachability.** A `:module-store` row is\n  emitted for every `store-of` an `E` or closure names, transitively\n  through the values of stores already discovered (`module-store-nodes`);\n  a named store the kernel lacks is `:yin.k/non-portable\n  :missing-module-store`.\n- **Linked registry entries do not travel.** The row table has no slot\n  for the receiver's module registry; only module stores do. A name a\n  carried closure resolves through the source's registry is a\n  **composition prerequisite** of the receiver: row 17's receiver is\n  given the source's registry value, and without it the relift refuses\n  `:yin.k/unsatisfied {:obligations #{host.mod/f}}`. The receiver's\n  registry is kept as given; there is no isolated-receiver pruning.\n- **Custody is nil at the root in phase 6.** The lift emits `custody nil`\n  (fork), and the lower refuses any non-nil custody as `:yin.k/unsupported\n  :custody`; the exclusive and holder path is phase 7 (§8.4).\n\n### 2.6 The root (replacing draft §5.5 in two slots)\n\n**PROPOSED**, with the reviewer's correction of the free environment:\n\n```clojure\n[T :task\n   version          ; 3\n   contract         ; {:yin.code/contract \"v4\" :yin.k/version 1}\n   kind             ; :blocked | :parked | :halted\n   role             ; :root | :child\n   id-counter       ; N\n   free-env-cref    ; NEW: a :bindings row, the task's initial free environment (§6.3)\n   store-cref\n   module-stores    ; crefs sorted by M\n   parked-cref      ; :parked-table\n   cursor-cells-cref       ; :cursor-cells census\n   requires         ; plain: UCF §7.6.1's map plus :yin.k/trees (§6.1)\n   code             ; plain: {A canonical-vector …}, \"v4\" vectors verified to hash to their key\n   installs         ; crefs to :install rows, sorted by module name\n   frames           ; crefs to :wait rows, in wait order     (:blocked, :parked)\n   parked-id        ; P or nil                                (:parked only)\n   result           ; V or nil                                (:halted only)\n   custody]         ; plain, draft §5.5's table\n```\n\nNo `layout` slot and no native image: those were the positional\nprofiles' (v2 amendment §2, §4.1). The custody table of the draft §5.5\nstands, with its version-0 comparison reworded (§10.3 R10).\n**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13):** of that table only the fork rows are\nreachable in phase 6; the lift writes `custody nil` and the lower's step\n10 (`check-custody!`) refuses any non-nil value `:yin.k/unsupported\n:custody` (§2.5, §7.2). *Why:* the exclusive and holder path is phase\n7's; carrying a custody arm no reader admits would be an untested slot.\n\n### 2.7 The bijection with the keyed frame\n\n**PROPOSED.** `rows->frame` and `frame->rows` (`yin.vm.ucf.rows`, §8.1)\nare inverse on valid row sets and canonical keyed frames: `:act` ↔\n`{:yin.k/state :act …}` with the deliver record synthesised from the\nkind and `rd`; `:tail` ↔ `{:yin.k/state :tail …}`; `:window` ↔\n`:yin.k/window` as a map (`[:lit nil]` ↔ a nil-valued key); `:k` ↔\n`:yin.k/k` in order. `rows->frame` reshapes and decides nothing the\nvalidator decides.\n\n---\n\n## 3. The frame validator\n","numLines":95,"startLine":282,"totalLines":2118}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01NdrQkNskVS26GhH9kGv6uR","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
