{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PBH8tzNaaou8ARhuxkKxMR","type":"tool_result","content":"1158\t## 7. Lift and lower\n1159\t\n1160\t### 7.1 Lift\n1161\t\n1162\t**DECIDED** (draft §9, UCF §7.8): refuse unless quiescent; refuse the\n1163\tholds (`unliftable-hold`, `handoff.cljc:588`) as `:yin.k/non-portable\n1164\t:reason-mismatch`; classify kind; run the **dependency closure over the\n1165\t`\"v4\"` vectors**, which needs the phase-5 scanners\n1166\t(`scan/requirements`, `scan/free-names`, `scan/free-occurrences` over the\n1167\t§2.3 table: the current `completion/code-facts` reads stack-table\n1168\tpositions and cannot be used), and refuse `:yin.k/unsatisfied` unless\n1169\t`:complete`, `:yin.k/trees` included; number cursor cells (draft §6); walk\n1170\twaits, parked records, the store slice, module stores, the free\n1171\tenvironment (from the kernel's `:free-env` field or the lift's explicit\n1172\t`:free-env` argument, §6.3, never from a current `E`), the result,\n1173\tinstall children; run `frame/defect` over every\n1174\tframe; emit rows and the census. A de Bruijn source walks the frames\n1175\t`layout/lift-act`/`lift-tail` reconstruct.\n1176\t\n1177\t**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13): the order as `lift-task*` runs it (B4),\n1178\tand the discharge rule.** The paragraph above put the dependency closure\n1179\tbefore the numbering and the walk, but the carried segments, contexts\n1180\tand module stores are known only once the walk has found them, so the\n1181\tclosure cannot run first. The merged order is:\n1182\t\n1183\t1. **Quiescence and safepoint.** Refuse `:yin.k/not-quiescent` on a\n1184\t   non-empty ready queue; refuse the holds (`unliftable-hold`: `:observe`,\n1185\t   `:held`, `:unminted-cursor`, `:pending-close`,\n1186\t   `:link-cursor-not-installed`) as `:yin.k/non-portable :reason-mismatch`;\n1187\t   refuse `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` when `:control` is set.\n1188\t2. **Kind.** `:parked` when the value is a parked continuation the parked\n1189\t   table holds, else `:blocked` on a non-empty wait set, else `:halted`\n1190\t   with an empty queue; anything else is `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`.\n1191\t3. **Free environment** (§6.3): the option, else the kernel field, else\n1192\t   `:missing-free-env`.\n1193\t4. **The walk.** Waits (state and pending), the parked table, the whole\n1194\t   store, the result (halted only), install children (each a recursive\n1195\t   `lift-task*` in the child role with its own accumulator), then the\n1196\t   module stores by reachability. Every value is classified `[:lit x]`,\n1197\t   `[:row n]` or `[:cursor-cell source]`; references are authenticated\n1198\t   against the kernel's resource table before encoding\n1199\t   (`:forged-resource-reference`), a foreign-owned value is\n1200\t   `:foreign-value`, a heap cell `:cell`, a host object `:host-object`.\n1201\t   The walk records every **segment** reached and every **context**: a\n1202\t   closure's `(A, entry, E)`, an `act`'s `(A, pc, E)`, a return frame's\n1203\t   `(A, pc, E)`. A `tail` state contributes its `K` frames' contexts and\n1204\t   none for its own dead activation.\n1205\t5. **Code.** `{A vector}` for the segments reached, from the kernel's\n1206\t   code table through its aliases; an unaddressed segment is\n1207\t   `:yin.k/non-portable :unaddressed-segment`, an address the kernel\n1208\t   cannot produce a vector for is `:yin.k/unsatisfied`.\n1209\t6. **Dependency closure** (`discharge!`), **per context, over live\n1210\t   code.** For each recorded context `(A, at, E)`, the live pcs are\n1211\t   `(live-pcs v at)`: the closure of `at` under `analysis/successors`\n1212\t   plus, for every reachable `:closure` instruction, that closure's body\n1213\t   from its entry (reachable code can still construct it). Every free\n1214\t   occurrence (`scan/free-occurrences`) at a live pc must be **answered\n1215\t   in that context**, in `resolve-var`'s order: a reserved name; the\n1216\t   context's own `E`; the **one** store `engine/active-store` selects for\n1217\t   that `E` (the root store when `E` has no `store-of`, else the module\n1218\t   store it names, both of which travel); a profiled primitive (a\n1219\t   `:host`-class profile refuses `:yin.k/non-portable\n1220\t   :host-state-primitive` at that point, so a host primitive shadowed by\n1221\t   `E` or the store lifts); an installing module's export (`m/x` built\n1222\t   with `(symbol (name m) (name x))`); a module the registry resolves for\n1223\t   a namespaced name. Any unanswered name is `:yin.k/unsatisfied`,\n1224\t   `:yin.k/discovery :incomplete`, `:yin.k/missing {:obligations #{…}}`.\n1225\t   *Why per context:* codex F3, the union of all module-store keys\n1226\t   answered names the running closure could not resolve. *Why live\n1227\t   code:* codex F6, a numeric or whole-body scan refused valid\n1228\t   continuations whose unreachable branches named a missing symbol.\n1229\t   The parser admits no backward jump (§3.1 of the frozen design), so no\n1230\t   carried vector exercises the backward edge; `live-pcs` is public and\n1231\t   is tested directly on a hand-built vector with a `:jump` to an earlier\n1232\t   pc. Discharge runs at lift only; the lower does not re-discharge free\n1233\t   names against the receiver (§7.2).\n1234\t7. **Trees** (§6.1): every carried segment has a non-empty entry in the\n1235\t   `:source-index` option, else `:yin.k/unsatisfied` with `:yin.k/trees`.\n1236\t8. **The task.** `requires` is `{:yin.k/cursor-profiles … :yin.k/segments\n1237\t   … :yin.k/trees …}`; the root is assembled with `custody nil` (§2.5).\n1238\t9. **Frames.** `validate-frames` over the assembled abstract task (X1,\n1239\t   every wait, parked state and reified value, children recursively); a\n1240\t   defect is `:yin.k/non-portable :inconsistent-state` carrying it. The\n1241\t   lift does not decode its own output a second time.\n1242\t10. **Emit** (`emit`): canonical cursor-cell numbering and rows.\n1243\t\n1244\t**Refusals are thrown.** Every refusal of lift and lower is an `ex-info`\n1245\twhose ex-data carries `:yin.k/status`, a `:yin.k/kind` where one is\n1246\tnamed, and the defect's data, the same convention as `decode` and `emit`;\n1247\tnothing returns a `{:status …}` map (engineer deviation 2, accepted).\n1248\t\n1249\t### 7.2 Lower\n1250\t\n1251\t**DECIDED** (draft §8 in order; §3.3): gate, structure, graph, hashes,\n1252\tcanonical form, canonical numbering, **frames**, pending and operations,\n1253\tdependency closure (`requires`/`code` agree with the census;\n1254\t`:yin.k/trees` complete per §6.1), custody, composition, restore. Restore\n1255\tbuilds the receiving kernel's state: semantic-register: `code` through\n1256\t`load-vector`/`attach-image` under `code/contract`, the free environment\n1257\tas base `:env`, each `:wait` to an engine entry (payload plus the\n1258\tpending's engine fields, `lower-frame!`'s shapes, `handoff.cljc:3182-3246`,\n1259\t`:deliver` carried), parked records into `:parked`, the result into\n1260\t`:value`; de Bruijn: derive and attach the image for each A, set\n1261\t`:free-env`, and `layout/lower-act`/`lower-tail` each frame (the stack\n1262\tlane's `tail` needs 6c-pre). Restoration delivers nothing, runs nothing,\n1263\tmints nothing.\n1264\t\n1265\t**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13): the lower's staged checks as\n1266\t`lower-task` runs them, and its refusal kinds.** Steps 1-7 are `decode`\n1267\t(gate, structure, graph, hashes, canonical form, numbering, frames,\n1268\t§3.3). Steps 8-10 then run as four stages, **each over the whole task\n1269\ttree (root and every install child, pre-order) before the next**, and\n1270\t**every one of them before the first `attach!`**; a defect in a child's\n1271\tlater stage therefore cannot be masked by the root's earlier one, and\n1272\tzero streams are attached on any refusal (codex F1, F2, F4, F7; mutants\n1273\tthat attach early are killed by the attach-count law):\n1274\t\n1275\t| Stage | Function | Checks, in order | Refusal |\n1276\t|---|---|---|---|\n1277\t| stores | `check-stores!` | no key of the root store or of any module store is a reserved name (`vm/reserved-name?`), refused here rather than by restore's `store-put` | `:yin.k/undecodable :reserved-name` (`:yin.k/role :store-key`) |\n1278\t| pendings and install records | `check-pendings!` | the task's role slot equals its position (`:root`/`:child`); every `:install` pending names a carried install row; kind consistency (`:halted` has no waits, `:blocked` has waits, `:parked` names a key of its parked table); then every install record by `check-install!` (§5.2: phase ∈ `#{:running :parked}`, the retained response and image contract, the child `:blocked`) | `:role`, `:incomplete-install`, `:kind`, `:parked-id`, `:install-phase`, `:install-response`, `:install-child`, all `:yin.k/undecodable` |\n1279\t| dependencies | `check-dependencies!` | `requires` is a map whose `:yin.k/segments` equals the key set of `code` (`:requires`); every segment has a non-empty `:yin.k/trees` entry; every `store-of` of every `:closure` and `:env` row of **this task** (children excluded, each checked in its own stage pass) names one of this task's `:module-store` rows (§2.6), so a parent's or sibling's store of the same key cannot answer (codex F7, F8); every vector attaches in the receiving kernel under its A (`attaches?`: `module/attach-module` into a throwaway copy, the result discarded) (`:segment`); every `:closure` row names a `:closure` tuple of its vector at its entry (`:closure-origin`) with the same binders (`:closure-binders`); every `:prim` names a primitive the receiver holds | `:yin.k/undecodable :requires`, `:yin.k/unsatisfied` with `:yin.k/trees` or **`:yin.k/stores`** (`:yin.k/discovery :incomplete`), `:yin.k/undecodable :segment`, `:closure-origin`, `:closure-binders`, `:yin.k/unsatisfied {:yin.k/name p}` |\n1280\t| custody | `check-custody!` | the root's custody slot is nil (phase 6 carries fork only) | `:yin.k/unsupported :custody` |\n1281\t\n1282\tThen **attachment** (step 11, `attach-streams!`): one `attach!` per\n1283\tdistinct stream identity of the whole tree, `:yin.k/unsatisfied\n1284\t{:dao.stream/identity …}` when the composition declines. Then **restore**\n1285\t(step 12, `restore-task`): the carried vectors attached by A into a\n1286\treceiver emptied of code, so frame and closure addresses map through the\n1287\treceiver's own aliases and no source-local segment number travels; fresh\n1288\tresource keys per stream identity and per cursor cell; values re-sealed\n1289\tunder the receiver's owner; waits in `lower-frame!`'s entry shapes, a\n1290\t`tail` with `{:deliver :return}` and no window, env or pc; parked records\n1291\twith a cycle guard; the store through `engine/store-put`; module stores;\n1292\tinstall children spawned by the engine's spawn discipline and then\n1293\trestored recursively (the spawn's initial control is replaced, no\n1294\t`vm/run`); the free environment as both `:env` and `:free-env`; the id\n1295\tcounter `max(local, carried)`. Restore meets no carrier defect, by\n1296\tconstruction of the stages above. The lower does **not** re-discharge\n1297\tfree names against the receiver: a name a closure resolves through the\n1298\tregistry is the composition prerequisite of §2.5, and the receiver's\n1299\tregistry is kept as given. The kinds `:role :custody :incomplete-install\n1300\t:kind :parked-id :requires :segment :install-phase :install-response\n1301\t:install-child :closure-origin :closure-binders` and the `:yin.k/stores`\n1302\tcensus are the engineer's and the fix rounds' naming; the earlier text\n1303\tnamed none of them (deviation 9, accepted with the requirement that every\n1304\tdefect be a named transport outcome before attachment).\n1305\t\n1306\t---\n1307\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"23e67cb0-f183-4c2e-b3d6-2e5b6c44b814","uuid":"bc7e3660-598e-4789-9a1c-fdc274211bc8","timestamp":"2026-10-13T08:31:50.349Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7plan/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md","content":"## 7. Lift and lower\n\n### 7.1 Lift\n\n**DECIDED** (draft §9, UCF §7.8): refuse unless quiescent; refuse the\nholds (`unliftable-hold`, `handoff.cljc:588`) as `:yin.k/non-portable\n:reason-mismatch`; classify kind; run the **dependency closure over the\n`\"v4\"` vectors**, which needs the phase-5 scanners\n(`scan/requirements`, `scan/free-names`, `scan/free-occurrences` over the\n§2.3 table: the current `completion/code-facts` reads stack-table\npositions and cannot be used), and refuse `:yin.k/unsatisfied` unless\n`:complete`, `:yin.k/trees` included; number cursor cells (draft §6); walk\nwaits, parked records, the store slice, module stores, the free\nenvironment (from the kernel's `:free-env` field or the lift's explicit\n`:free-env` argument, §6.3, never from a current `E`), the result,\ninstall children; run `frame/defect` over every\nframe; emit rows and the census. A de Bruijn source walks the frames\n`layout/lift-act`/`lift-tail` reconstruct.\n\n**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13): the order as `lift-task*` runs it (B4),\nand the discharge rule.** The paragraph above put the dependency closure\nbefore the numbering and the walk, but the carried segments, contexts\nand module stores are known only once the walk has found them, so the\nclosure cannot run first. The merged order is:\n\n1. **Quiescence and safepoint.** Refuse `:yin.k/not-quiescent` on a\n   non-empty ready queue; refuse the holds (`unliftable-hold`: `:observe`,\n   `:held`, `:unminted-cursor`, `:pending-close`,\n   `:link-cursor-not-installed`) as `:yin.k/non-portable :reason-mismatch`;\n   refuse `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` when `:control` is set.\n2. **Kind.** `:parked` when the value is a parked continuation the parked\n   table holds, else `:blocked` on a non-empty wait set, else `:halted`\n   with an empty queue; anything else is `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`.\n3. **Free environment** (§6.3): the option, else the kernel field, else\n   `:missing-free-env`.\n4. **The walk.** Waits (state and pending), the parked table, the whole\n   store, the result (halted only), install children (each a recursive\n   `lift-task*` in the child role with its own accumulator), then the\n   module stores by reachability. Every value is classified `[:lit x]`,\n   `[:row n]` or `[:cursor-cell source]`; references are authenticated\n   against the kernel's resource table before encoding\n   (`:forged-resource-reference`), a foreign-owned value is\n   `:foreign-value`, a heap cell `:cell`, a host object `:host-object`.\n   The walk records every **segment** reached and every **context**: a\n   closure's `(A, entry, E)`, an `act`'s `(A, pc, E)`, a return frame's\n   `(A, pc, E)`. A `tail` state contributes its `K` frames' contexts and\n   none for its own dead activation.\n5. **Code.** `{A vector}` for the segments reached, from the kernel's\n   code table through its aliases; an unaddressed segment is\n   `:yin.k/non-portable :unaddressed-segment`, an address the kernel\n   cannot produce a vector for is `:yin.k/unsatisfied`.\n6. **Dependency closure** (`discharge!`), **per context, over live\n   code.** For each recorded context `(A, at, E)`, the live pcs are\n   `(live-pcs v at)`: the closure of `at` under `analysis/successors`\n   plus, for every reachable `:closure` instruction, that closure's body\n   from its entry (reachable code can still construct it). Every free\n   occurrence (`scan/free-occurrences`) at a live pc must be **answered\n   in that context**, in `resolve-var`'s order: a reserved name; the\n   context's own `E`; the **one** store `engine/active-store` selects for\n   that `E` (the root store when `E` has no `store-of`, else the module\n   store it names, both of which travel); a profiled primitive (a\n   `:host`-class profile refuses `:yin.k/non-portable\n   :host-state-primitive` at that point, so a host primitive shadowed by\n   `E` or the store lifts); an installing module's export (`m/x` built\n   with `(symbol (name m) (name x))`); a module the registry resolves for\n   a namespaced name. Any unanswered name is `:yin.k/unsatisfied`,\n   `:yin.k/discovery :incomplete`, `:yin.k/missing {:obligations #{…}}`.\n   *Why per context:* codex F3, the union of all module-store keys\n   answered names the running closure could not resolve. *Why live\n   code:* codex F6, a numeric or whole-body scan refused valid\n   continuations whose unreachable branches named a missing symbol.\n   The parser admits no backward jump (§3.1 of the frozen design), so no\n   carried vector exercises the backward edge; `live-pcs` is public and\n   is tested directly on a hand-built vector with a `:jump` to an earlier\n   pc. Discharge runs at lift only; the lower does not re-discharge free\n   names against the receiver (§7.2).\n7. **Trees** (§6.1): every carried segment has a non-empty entry in the\n   `:source-index` option, else `:yin.k/unsatisfied` with `:yin.k/trees`.\n8. **The task.** `requires` is `{:yin.k/cursor-profiles … :yin.k/segments\n   … :yin.k/trees …}`; the root is assembled with `custody nil` (§2.5).\n9. **Frames.** `validate-frames` over the assembled abstract task (X1,\n   every wait, parked state and reified value, children recursively); a\n   defect is `:yin.k/non-portable :inconsistent-state` carrying it. The\n   lift does not decode its own output a second time.\n10. **Emit** (`emit`): canonical cursor-cell numbering and rows.\n\n**Refusals are thrown.** Every refusal of lift and lower is an `ex-info`\nwhose ex-data carries `:yin.k/status`, a `:yin.k/kind` where one is\nnamed, and the defect's data, the same convention as `decode` and `emit`;\nnothing returns a `{:status …}` map (engineer deviation 2, accepted).\n\n### 7.2 Lower\n\n**DECIDED** (draft §8 in order; §3.3): gate, structure, graph, hashes,\ncanonical form, canonical numbering, **frames**, pending and operations,\ndependency closure (`requires`/`code` agree with the census;\n`:yin.k/trees` complete per §6.1), custody, composition, restore. Restore\nbuilds the receiving kernel's state: semantic-register: `code` through\n`load-vector`/`attach-image` under `code/contract`, the free environment\nas base `:env`, each `:wait` to an engine entry (payload plus the\npending's engine fields, `lower-frame!`'s shapes, `handoff.cljc:3182-3246`,\n`:deliver` carried), parked records into `:parked`, the result into\n`:value`; de Bruijn: derive and attach the image for each A, set\n`:free-env`, and `layout/lower-act`/`lower-tail` each frame (the stack\nlane's `tail` needs 6c-pre). Restoration delivers nothing, runs nothing,\nmints nothing.\n\n**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13): the lower's staged checks as\n`lower-task` runs them, and its refusal kinds.** Steps 1-7 are `decode`\n(gate, structure, graph, hashes, canonical form, numbering, frames,\n§3.3). Steps 8-10 then run as four stages, **each over the whole task\ntree (root and every install child, pre-order) before the next**, and\n**every one of them before the first `attach!`**; a defect in a child's\nlater stage therefore cannot be masked by the root's earlier one, and\nzero streams are attached on any refusal (codex F1, F2, F4, F7; mutants\nthat attach early are killed by the attach-count law):\n\n| Stage | Function | Checks, in order | Refusal |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| stores | `check-stores!` | no key of the root store or of any module store is a reserved name (`vm/reserved-name?`), refused here rather than by restore's `store-put` | `:yin.k/undecodable :reserved-name` (`:yin.k/role :store-key`) |\n| pendings and install records | `check-pendings!` | the task's role slot equals its position (`:root`/`:child`); every `:install` pending names a carried install row; kind consistency (`:halted` has no waits, `:blocked` has waits, `:parked` names a key of its parked table); then every install record by `check-install!` (§5.2: phase ∈ `#{:running :parked}`, the retained response and image contract, the child `:blocked`) | `:role`, `:incomplete-install`, `:kind`, `:parked-id`, `:install-phase`, `:install-response`, `:install-child`, all `:yin.k/undecodable` |\n| dependencies | `check-dependencies!` | `requires` is a map whose `:yin.k/segments` equals the key set of `code` (`:requires`); every segment has a non-empty `:yin.k/trees` entry; every `store-of` of every `:closure` and `:env` row of **this task** (children excluded, each checked in its own stage pass) names one of this task's `:module-store` rows (§2.6), so a parent's or sibling's store of the same key cannot answer (codex F7, F8); every vector attaches in the receiving kernel under its A (`attaches?`: `module/attach-module` into a throwaway copy, the result discarded) (`:segment`); every `:closure` row names a `:closure` tuple of its vector at its entry (`:closure-origin`) with the same binders (`:closure-binders`); every `:prim` names a primitive the receiver holds | `:yin.k/undecodable :requires`, `:yin.k/unsatisfied` with `:yin.k/trees` or **`:yin.k/stores`** (`:yin.k/discovery :incomplete`), `:yin.k/undecodable :segment`, `:closure-origin`, `:closure-binders`, `:yin.k/unsatisfied {:yin.k/name p}` |\n| custody | `check-custody!` | the root's custody slot is nil (phase 6 carries fork only) | `:yin.k/unsupported :custody` |\n\nThen **attachment** (step 11, `attach-streams!`): one `attach!` per\ndistinct stream identity of the whole tree, `:yin.k/unsatisfied\n{:dao.stream/identity …}` when the composition declines. Then **restore**\n(step 12, `restore-task`): the carried vectors attached by A into a\nreceiver emptied of code, so frame and closure addresses map through the\nreceiver's own aliases and no source-local segment number travels; fresh\nresource keys per stream identity and per cursor cell; values re-sealed\nunder the receiver's owner; waits in `lower-frame!`'s entry shapes, a\n`tail` with `{:deliver :return}` and no window, env or pc; parked records\nwith a cycle guard; the store through `engine/store-put`; module stores;\ninstall children spawned by the engine's spawn discipline and then\nrestored recursively (the spawn's initial control is replaced, no\n`vm/run`); the free environment as both `:env` and `:free-env`; the id\ncounter `max(local, carried)`. Restore meets no carrier defect, by\nconstruction of the stages above. The lower does **not** re-discharge\nfree names against the receiver: a name a closure resolves through the\nregistry is the composition prerequisite of §2.5, and the receiver's\nregistry is kept as given. The kinds `:role :custody :incomplete-install\n:kind :parked-id :requires :segment :install-phase :install-response\n:install-child :closure-origin :closure-binders` and the `:yin.k/stores`\ncensus are the engineer's and the fix rounds' naming; the earlier text\nnamed none of them (deviation 9, accepted with the requirement that every\ndefect be a named transport outcome before attachment).\n\n---\n","numLines":150,"startLine":1158,"totalLines":2118}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01PBH8tzNaaou8ARhuxkKxMR","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
