{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TTn2bRdeRVASRQZFG5dPhz","type":"tool_result","content":"825\t## 5. The safepoint harness\n826\t\n827\t### 5.1 What it proves\n828\t\n829\t**DECIDED** (frozen §10 phase 6 gate; UCF §7.11). For every row of the\n830\texpanded matrix, both delivery modes, nested and reified captures,\n831\tmodule contexts and every refusal: lift at the safepoint, lower into a\n832\tfresh machine, continue, and result and effect trace equal the\n833\tuninterrupted reference run; `lift(lower(frame)) = frame`; the frame's\n834\treason is the observed wait's; on the three lanes: `:semantic-register`,\n835\t`:stack-through-layout`, `:register-through-layout`.\n836\t\n837\t### 5.2 The matrix\n838\t\n839\t**Expected results are stated for the bare expression.** Each row runs\n840\tunder the four segment wrappers of the old harness\n841\t(`safepoint_harness_test.cljc:92-106`), whose expected results follow\n842\tmechanically from the bare result `r`: `:bare` → `r`; `:operand` →\n843\t`[:operand r]` (`(cat [:operand] r)`); `:depth-3` → `[3 [2 [1 r]]]`\n844\t(`nest` wraps `(cat [d] ((fn [x] …) d))` three times, as the old test's\n845\tpinned `[2 [1 [:two 2 \"C\"]]]` shows for depth 2, `:574`); `:captured` →\n846\t`[:captured :arg r]` (`(cat (cat (cat [] k) y) r)` with `k = :captured`,\n847\t`y = :arg`). **Amended after 6b (2026-10-13), one exception to the\n848\tmechanical rule (B2):** `nest`'s own lambda parameter is `x`, so under\n849\t`:depth-3` any row whose program captures the free name `x` captures the\n850\twrapper's `x` instead. Row 21 is the one such row: its `(fn [] x)` is\n851\tcreated inside the innermost `nest` application where `x = 1`, so the\n852\treference run itself answers `[3 [2 [1 1]]]`, not `[3 [2 [1 99]]]`, and\n853\tthe harness pins `1` for that one case (`safepoint_harness_test.cljc`,\n854\tthe `(and (= 21 n) (= :depth-3 label))` branch). The bare, `:operand`\n855\tand `:captured` results of row 21 keep `99`. The wrapper rule is\n856\totherwise unchanged; the exception is stated rather than the wrapper\n857\trenamed, because the old harness's segments are shared and the lowered\n858\trun matches the reference either way (law 1 holds on the row). **Frames\n859\tthe wrappers add.** The harness marks `:tail?\n860\ttrue` only where a row says so (`:228`, `:256`; `ast->semantic-bytecode`\n861\tdefaults it to false, `vm.cljc:1089`; `mark-tail` is the expander's and\n862\tis not run here), so every `let1` and every `nest` application is a\n863\tnon-tail call that pushes a return frame. `with-cat` (`:69-76`,\n864\toutermost in every segment, `:96`) adds **one** frame below everything\n865\tthe row builds, with `stack-base 0` and an empty slice on the stack lane\n866\t(nothing is pushed before its application). On top of it `:bare` and\n867\t`:operand` add none of their own (the row is the operand), `:depth-3`\n868\tadds three (`nest`'s applications, each with two values, `cat` and\n869\t`[d]`, pushed under it), and `:captured` adds three (`let1 k`, `let1 f`,\n870\t`(f :arg)`). A row's own `let1`s add theirs. Row 20 alone runs outside\n871\tthe wrappers.\n872\t\n873\tPrograms use `v2_support`'s AST helpers (`v2_support.cljc:56-104`:\n874\t`lit v lam app def! then cursor-of next-of let1`) and the four segments\n875\tof the old harness (`safepoint_harness_test.cljc:92-106`: `:bare`,\n876\t`:operand`, `:depth-3`, `:captured`); `cat` is the closure `(fn [p q]\n877\t(conj p q))` bound around every body (`:69-81`). \"Expected\" is the\n878\treference run's normalised result; where a row says \"executable\" it runs\n879\ton all three lanes; \"synthetic\" means the frame is built by hand and\n880\tjudged by `frame/defect` only, because no program reaches it on the\n881\tlanded or stage-2 evaluator.\n882\t\n883\t| # | Row | Program (inside each segment) | How parked / satisfied | State, pending | Expected after satisfy | Executable? |\n884\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n885\t| 1 | blocked read | `(let1 s (stream/make 4) (let1 c (cursor-of s) (let1 a (next-of c) (cat (cat [] a) (next-of c)))))`, `\"A\"` appended before the run | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next` | `[\"A\" \"B\"]` | yes |\n886\t| 2 | blocked write | `(stream-put w \"v\")` on a one-slot stream warmed with `:warmed` | take one | act, `:put` with retained `\"v\"` | `\"v\"` landed | yes |\n887\t| 3 | FFI sent | `(dao.stream.apply/call :op/echo \"hello\")` | append the success response | act, `:ffi` | `\"echo!\"` | 4b stage 2 |\n888\t| 4 | FFI retained | as 3 with a one-slot, warmed call-in | free the slot, then answer | act, `:ffi-request` | `\"echo!\"` | 4b stage 2 |\n889\t| 5 | effectful call, non-tail, stream | `(cat [:ordinary] (stream/next! c))` | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next` | `[:ordinary \"B\"]` | yes (stage 1 has streams; `stream/next!` is the stream module's) |\n890\t| 6 | **effectful call, tail, stream** | `((fn [u] (stream/next! c)) 0)` with the inner call `:tail? true` | append `\"B\"` | **tail**, `:next` | `\"B\"` delivered through `K` | yes |\n891\t| 7 | link request phase | `(require 'host.mod)` parked **before** the request append succeeds (one-slot, warmed request stream) | free the slot, then answer `:ok` | act, `:link-request` | `'host.mod` | 4b stage 2 |\n892\t| 8 | link response phase | `(require 'host.mod)` polling (request appended) | append the `:ok` response | act, `:link-response` | `'host.mod` | 4b stage 2 |\n893\t| 9 | **link, tail** | `((fn [u] (require 'host.mod)) 0)` tail | as 8 | **tail**, `:link-response` | `'host.mod` through `K` | 4b stage 2 |\n894\t| 10 | install, blocked child (**amended after 6b**) | `(require 'host.mod)` where `host.mod`'s body reads a stream the test has not fed, so the child is `:blocked` and the parent's wait is `:install` | feed the child's stream | act, `:install` + install entry, child kind `:blocked`, **phase `:parked`** | `'host.mod` | yes |\n895\t| 11 | install, `:running` wire phase (**amended after 6b, synthetic phase**) | row 10's machine with the install entry's phase set to `:running` **by hand** before the lift | as 10 | act, `:install`, child kind `:blocked`, phase `:running` | `'host.mod` | the program is row 10's; the phase is not engine-reachable at a safepoint (see below) |\n896\t| 12 | install, tail | `((fn [u] (require 'host.mod)) 0)` tail with 10's child | as 10 | **tail**, `:install` | `'host.mod` through `K` | 4b stage 2 |\n897\t| 13 | explicit park | `(vm/park)` | `(vm/resume pid \"back\")` beside | parked record, root `:parked` | `\"back\"` | yes |\n898\t| 14 | reified capture live in a window | `(let1 k (vm/current-continuation) (cat (cat [] k) (next-of c)))` | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next`; `k` is a `:reified` value in `W` | `[:continuation \"B\"]` normalised | yes |\n899\t| 15 | capture invoked after lower | `(let1 k (vm/current-continuation) (if (= k :second) :done (let1 r (next-of c) (k :second))))`: on the first pass `k` is a continuation, so the test fails and the read runs and parks; after satisfaction `(k :second)` delivers `:second` into the capture's `rd` (the `let1` operand) and re-enters at the capture's successor pc, so the body is entered again with `k = :second` and the test succeeds **without repeating the read** (the invocation is abortive on every lane: `:continuation` operator kind, `srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:212-217` stage 2, `stack.cljc:760-763`). `if` is `{:type :if :test :consequent :alternate}` (`vm.cljc:1069`), a helper the new harness file adds; `=` is the primitive (`vm.cljc:387`), consumed before the read | append `\"x\"` after lower | act, `:next`; `k` is a `:reified` value in `E` (the closure's) | `:done` | yes |\n900\t| 16 | nested capture | `(let1 k1 (vm/current-continuation) (let1 k2 (vm/current-continuation) (cat (cat (cat [] k1) k2) (next-of c))))` | append `\"B\"` | act; `k2`'s act window holds `k1` (a `:reified` inside a `:reified`'s window) | `[:continuation :continuation \"B\"]` | yes |\n901\t| 17 | module context, blocked inside a module closure | `host.mod` exports `f = (fn [c] (stream/next! c))`; root: `(f c)` after `(require 'host.mod)` | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next`, `E` carries `store-of host.mod` | `\"B\"` | yes; **amended after 6b:** the receiver composition is given the source's registry entry for `host.mod`, a composition prerequisite (§2.5): linked registry entries do not travel |\n902\t| 18 | module-store snapshot | `parked-module-store-cursors` (`lift_support.cljc:310-333`): an explicit park whose module store holds two cursors named by a module closure | resume | parked record; `:module-store` row | the two cursor cells | yes; **amended after 6b:** the fixture's module store is hand-built and keyed by the **symbol** `host.mod`, as `frame_test` does, while the kernel keys installed stores by module address; row 17 exercises a real installation |\n903\t| 19 | halt | `(lit 42)` | — | result | `42` | yes |\n904\t| 20 | **tail-effect halt** | run **outside the wrappers**, in two batches on one machine: a setup batch `(then (def! 's (stream/make 4)) (def! 'c (cursor-of s)))` run to halt (store writes persist across batches, as `lift_support.cljc:320-322` relies on), then the batch `(stream/next! c)` **marked `:tail? true` at the root**, `c` resolving through the store; no application precedes the call, so `K` is empty (any `let1` or wrapper would push a frame, §5.2 above) | append `\"B\"` | tail, `:next` | halts with `\"B\"` (4b: a tail effect with no frame to pop halts, `srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:205-211` stage 2; stack: the seam's empty-`K` case, §4.3), env free of `store-of` | yes |\n905\t| 21 | **free-env shadowing** (the review's row) | initial free env `{x 99}`; `(let1 f (fn [] x) ((fn [x] (f (next-of c))) 1))` | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next`; `E` at the read binds `x` to 1; root `free-env` carries `x 99` | the lowered `f` returns `99` (not nil, not 1) on every lane; **the one row the earlier design would have failed** | yes |\n906\t| 22 | nil-valued live register | `(let1 pair (fn [p q] (cat (cat [] p) q)) (pair nil (next-of c)))`: the `nil` operand is evaluated **before** the read and is live across it (`(cat nil (next-of c))` would give `(conj nil \"B\")`, the list `(\"B\")`, so a vector-building `pair` is used; there is no `vector` primitive, `vm.cljc:380-410`, and `pair` builds through the segment's `cat`) | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next`; `W` has a present key with `[:lit nil]` | `[nil \"B\"]` | yes |\n907\t| 23 | tail FFI bookkeeping | a `tail` parked record under a call id `P ∈ :ffi-call-ids` | — | parked, context `:parked`, bookkeeping clause of P4 | validator accepts; the **same valid tail under a key not in `:ffi-call-ids`** is `:parked-site` (P4); moving `site` to a non-tail call is `:site` (T1, which runs first) | **synthetic**: no primitive returns an FFI effect through a tail `:call` today (evaluator §1.6) |\n908\t| 24 | `act` FFI bookkeeping | 3's parked bookkeeping record under its call id | — | parked, P4 bookkeeping | validator accepts | 4b stage 2 |\n909\t\n910\t**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13), rows 10-12 and the install contract\n911\t(B1).** The earlier text promised a child \"`:blocked`, phase `:running`\"\n912\t(row 10) and a child \"executing `:park`, kind `:parked`\" (row 11).\n913\tNeither is an engine state at a quiescent safepoint. `advance-install`\n914\t(`engine.cljc:1421-1454`) runs the child inside the sweep and then\n915\trecords it: a **blocked** child is retained at phase **`:parked`**\n916\t(`:1444-1446`); a **halted** child goes straight to `link-install` in the\n917\tsame sweep (`:1447-1452`), so it is never retained; and a child that\n918\texecutes `:park` is `:halted?` with a parked value, so it too goes to\n919\t`link-install`, which completes the install if the exports are already\n920\tdefined and otherwise refuses it `:export-missing`. Phase `:running` is\n921\tassigned only in the `:else` arm, within one sweep, and is overwritten\n922\tbefore any safepoint. So: row 10 is the engine's real state (child\n923\t`:blocked`, phase `:parked`); row 11 exercises the `:running` **wire\n924\tphase**, which the codec accepts (`install-node` admits `#{:running\n925\t:parked}` on lift and `check-install!` on lower) and the harness\n926\tconstructs by hand on row 10's machine (`installing % false :running`,\n927\t`safepoint_harness_test.cljc:342`); row 12 is row 10's child under tail\n928\tdelivery. No row promises an executable kind-`:parked` install child on\n929\tthe current engine (owner question §11 Q1). **The install validation\n930\tthe lower enforces** (`check-install!`, before any attachment, §7.2):\n931\tthe phase is one of `#{:running :parked}` (`:install-phase`); the\n932\tretained response is a map with `:status :ok`, a `:manifest` map whose\n933\t`:yin.module/name` equals the install's module name and whose\n934\t`:yin.module/exports` is a collection of symbols, and an `:image` map\n935\twhose `:value` the receiving kernel's loader admits (`:install-response`);\n936\tand the carried child task's kind is **`:blocked`** (`:install-child`),\n937\tbecause a halted or parked child would have been linked in the sweep it\n938\thalted in. On lift, `install-node` refuses a phase outside the set as\n939\t`:yin.k/non-portable :incomplete-install` and a response CBOR cannot\n940\tencode as `:yin.k/non-portable :install-response`.\n941\t\n942\tRefusal rows: `:yin.k/not-quiescent` (`a-nonempty-ready-queue-is-not-quiescent`,\n943\t`:591-603`); `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` (a register kernel stepped one\n944\tinstruction past a `:const`, asked to lift); `:yin.k/non-portable\n945\t:reason-mismatch` (the `:observe` hold, `:675-692`); and the validator's\n946\t**membership tampers**, each a row-set edit of a valid body before\n947\tlower: a window with an extra live key → `:window-extra`; a live key\n948\tremoved → `:window-missing`; `rd` added → `:window-self`; `rd` replaced\n949\tby another register → `:destination`; a return frame's `pc` moved after\n950\ta tail call → `:return-site`; a reason the site does not admit →\n951\t`:reason-mismatch`; a `:tail` row given a fourth slot → **`:row-shape`\n952\tat the codec's structure step (2), not `:frame-shape`** (amended after\n953\t6b, B3: the row table fixes `:tail` at three slots, so the wire never\n954\treaches rule S1 with a fourth slot; S1 still governs an abstract frame\n955\thanded to `frame/defect` directly; and because `emit`/`emit-numbered`\n956\tsilently drop extra slots from an abstract node, mapping over the\n957\tshorter descriptor list, this tamper must be a **raw row edit with a\n958\trehash**, never an abstract-task edit);\n959\tthe `[:lit nil]` of row 22 dropped → `:window-missing`; a `:reified`\n960\tact whose `pc−1` is not a capture → `:captured-site`; a parked act whose\n961\t`pc−1` is not `:park` → `:parked-site`; for E2, with row 21's initial\n962\tfree environment extended to `{x 99 y 7}`, the **unshadowed** free\n963\tbinding `y` in the act's `E` changed to `8` → `:free-env-mismatch`\n964\t(changing the lexical `x`, which is `1` at the read, trips nothing: E2\n965\tcompares off the lexical names), and the lexical `x` **removed** from\n966\t`E` → `:free-env-mismatch` (presence clause (a)); the root `free-env`\n967\trow given a reserved key → `:reserved-name` (X1). **A changed live value**\n968\t(e.g. `\"A\"` → `\"Z\"` in row 1's window) is not a membership defect and is\n969\tnot expected to be refused: it is tested by **execution parity** (the\n970\tlowered run's result differs from the reference), as the review ruled.\n971\t\n972\t### 5.3 Lanes\n973\t\n974\tThe `:semantic-register` lane's wait entries are canonical frames plus\n975\tengine fields. The de Bruijn lanes load `(:image (derive/stack-image v))`\n976\tand `(:image (derive/register-image v))` of `v = (:vector (project P))`\n977\tthrough `dvm/create-vm`/`rvm/create-vm`, run to the safepoint and lift\n978\tthrough `layout/lift-act`/`lift-tail`; the stack lane's tail rows (6,\n979\t9, 12, 20) require the seam of §4.3 and are gated on 6c-pre.\n980\t\n981\t### 5.4 Laws\n982\t\n983\t1. **Reference parity**: result and effect trace of the lowered,\n984\t   satisfied machine equal the reference run (`reference`/`handed-off`,\n985\t   `:368-401`).\n986\t2. **Round trip**: `lift(lower(S)) = S` by root id under the\n987\t   fresh-receiver context of the draft's §9, after the re-emission of\n988\t   §5.5.\n989\t3. **Observed reason**: the pending's reason is the wait's, never the\n990\t   static kind (ucf-revisions §8).\n991\t4. **Cross-engine canonical equality**: one program at one safepoint\n992\t   lifts to row sets whose root ids are **equal after the re-emission of\n993\t   §5.5** on all three lanes.\n994\t5. **Cross-engine lowering**: a row set lifted on one lane lowers on\n995\t   each of the other two (six ordered pairs) and continues to the\n996\t   reference result.\n997\t6. **Validator agreement**: every lifted frame passes `frame/defect` in\n998\t   its context; every tamper of §5.2 is refused with its kind; the\n999\t   verdict is lane-independent.\n1000\t\n1001\t### 5.5 Re-emission for equality (replacing \"stream-blinding\")\n1002\t\n1003\t**PROPOSED** (reviewer: `stream-blind` rewrites decoded maps and does\n1004\tnot recompute Merkle ids, so it cannot establish root-id equality). The\n1005\tharness first **fixes identities where it can**: all three lanes serve\n1006\ttheir streams through one toy channel (`v2_support/toy`,\n1007\t`served-peer`), so stream identities and descriptors are equal across\n1008\tlanes by construction where the same logical stream is lifted. Where a\n1009\tlane mints its own (fresh cursor cells on relift, the receiver's own call\n1010\tpair), the comparison uses `rows/canonical-under`, a **test-only**\n1011\ttransformation whose placeholder assignment is **never derived from\n1012\t*host* traversal order** (the canonical walk of draft §6 is a function\n1013\tof the state and is what the procedure reads; the review's objection: map and set traversal, and\n1014\t\"cursor cells by body\", can depend on the very identities being erased). It\n1015\ttakes an **explicit logical-stream correspondence** from the fixture:\n1016\tthe harness created every stream the row reaches, so it supplies\n1017\t`{lane-identity → logical-name}` (e.g. `{id-of-s :s, id-of-call-out\n1018\t:call-out}`) for each lane; `canonical-under` decodes the row set to the\n1019\tabstract task (draft §6's input), replaces each stream marker's identity\n1020\tand descriptor by the logical name the map gives it (a marker the map\n1021\tdoes not cover is a test failure, never a guess), runs the canonical\n1022\tcursor-cell numbering (draft §6), re-emits every row and recomputes every id,\n1023\tand compares root ids. Draft §6 is invariant under renaming of the\n1024\t**cursor cells** (its labels never encode an incoming cursor-cell id), and the only\n1025\tother identity it consumes is the stream marker in each cursor cell's body\n1026\tlabel (`[:body [:stream identity descriptor] position]`, draft §6 as\n1027\tamended, spelled by rank inside keys) and in cursor-cell bodies; after the substitution those are the fixture's\n1028\tlogical names, so the procedure's inputs no longer depend on any lane's\n1029\tidentities. Guest sets and maps are entered in the bytewise order of\n1030\ttheir members' keys, and `:store`, `:bindings` and the parked table in\n1031\tthe byte order of their structural keys (draft §6 W1), never in host\n1032\tenumeration order, so\n1033\ta permutation of the erased identities cannot change the result once\n1034\tthe markers are substituted. It is never used outside the harness.\n1035\t\n1036\t### 5.6 Static checks the harness runs once per corpus segment\n1037\t\n1038\t1. `frame/defect` on every frame the reference lane mints: nil.\n1039\t2. For every **reachable** parking pc `p` of `v`: `(analysis/saved a\n1040\t   (inc p) rd)` has no member `≥ k(body(p))` and does not contain `rd`.\n1041\t3. Register layout: at every reachable boundary pc `p`, `virt(p, s)` is\n1042\t   single-valued on the physical image of `live-out*(p)`; the `live`\n1043\t   operand of `R[p]` equals `(sort (map phys (disj live-out*(p) rd)))`,\n1044\t   the destination removed as a **virtual** id before mapping, which is\n1045\t   `[]` for a tail call (phase-5 §2.5). Worked example, inner call at\n1046\t   `p = 3`: `L(4) = {1 2}`, minus `rd 2` → `{1}` → `[0]`, the pinned\n1047\t   `live` of phase-5 §6.3 row 2.\n1048\t4. Stack layout: the k-th non-push instruction of `H` has the mnemonic\n1049\t   family of `v[k]` for every `k`; counts agree.\n1050\t5. Stack layout: `(set (stack-layout p)) = (analysis/saved a (inc p)\n1051\t   rd)` for every reachable **non-tail** parking pc `p`.\n1052\t6. `E` reconstruction: at every safepoint reached, the layout's `E`\n1053\t   equals the reference lane's `E` (values normalised), including rows\n1054\t   21 and 22 and the `:duplicate-param`, `:nested-closure`,\n1055\t   `:nested-lambdas` programs.\n1056\t7. Closure reconstruction: canonical ↔ positional closures round-trip on\n1057\t   the `\"closure value\"` and `\"higher order\"` B0 rows and\n1058\t   `:duplicate-param`.\n1059\t8. Tail lowering: the `:frames`/`:store-of` chosen for a lowered `tail`\n1060\t   entry are unobservable: lowering with `[]`/`nil` and with arbitrary\n1061\t   values gives equal observables on rows 6, 9, 12, 20.\n1062\t9. Slices: for every non-tail safepoint with `m` return frames, the\n1063\t   `m+1` slices of §4.3 partition the stack kernel's operand stack\n1064\t   exactly, and each has the length of its `stack-layout`.\n1065\t\n1066\t---\n1067\t\n1068\t## 6. Code and task context in the carrier\n1069\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"23e67cb0-f183-4c2e-b3d6-2e5b6c44b814","uuid":"45919fff-1191-4ffa-801e-897e3908c5f2","timestamp":"2026-10-13T08:33:51.508Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7plan/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md","content":"## 5. The safepoint harness\n\n### 5.1 What it proves\n\n**DECIDED** (frozen §10 phase 6 gate; UCF §7.11). For every row of the\nexpanded matrix, both delivery modes, nested and reified captures,\nmodule contexts and every refusal: lift at the safepoint, lower into a\nfresh machine, continue, and result and effect trace equal the\nuninterrupted reference run; `lift(lower(frame)) = frame`; the frame's\nreason is the observed wait's; on the three lanes: `:semantic-register`,\n`:stack-through-layout`, `:register-through-layout`.\n\n### 5.2 The matrix\n\n**Expected results are stated for the bare expression.** Each row runs\nunder the four segment wrappers of the old harness\n(`safepoint_harness_test.cljc:92-106`), whose expected results follow\nmechanically from the bare result `r`: `:bare` → `r`; `:operand` →\n`[:operand r]` (`(cat [:operand] r)`); `:depth-3` → `[3 [2 [1 r]]]`\n(`nest` wraps `(cat [d] ((fn [x] …) d))` three times, as the old test's\npinned `[2 [1 [:two 2 \"C\"]]]` shows for depth 2, `:574`); `:captured` →\n`[:captured :arg r]` (`(cat (cat (cat [] k) y) r)` with `k = :captured`,\n`y = :arg`). **Amended after 6b (2026-10-13), one exception to the\nmechanical rule (B2):** `nest`'s own lambda parameter is `x`, so under\n`:depth-3` any row whose program captures the free name `x` captures the\nwrapper's `x` instead. Row 21 is the one such row: its `(fn [] x)` is\ncreated inside the innermost `nest` application where `x = 1`, so the\nreference run itself answers `[3 [2 [1 1]]]`, not `[3 [2 [1 99]]]`, and\nthe harness pins `1` for that one case (`safepoint_harness_test.cljc`,\nthe `(and (= 21 n) (= :depth-3 label))` branch). The bare, `:operand`\nand `:captured` results of row 21 keep `99`. The wrapper rule is\notherwise unchanged; the exception is stated rather than the wrapper\nrenamed, because the old harness's segments are shared and the lowered\nrun matches the reference either way (law 1 holds on the row). **Frames\nthe wrappers add.** The harness marks `:tail?\ntrue` only where a row says so (`:228`, `:256`; `ast->semantic-bytecode`\ndefaults it to false, `vm.cljc:1089`; `mark-tail` is the expander's and\nis not run here), so every `let1` and every `nest` application is a\nnon-tail call that pushes a return frame. `with-cat` (`:69-76`,\noutermost in every segment, `:96`) adds **one** frame below everything\nthe row builds, with `stack-base 0` and an empty slice on the stack lane\n(nothing is pushed before its application). On top of it `:bare` and\n`:operand` add none of their own (the row is the operand), `:depth-3`\nadds three (`nest`'s applications, each with two values, `cat` and\n`[d]`, pushed under it), and `:captured` adds three (`let1 k`, `let1 f`,\n`(f :arg)`). A row's own `let1`s add theirs. Row 20 alone runs outside\nthe wrappers.\n\nPrograms use `v2_support`'s AST helpers (`v2_support.cljc:56-104`:\n`lit v lam app def! then cursor-of next-of let1`) and the four segments\nof the old harness (`safepoint_harness_test.cljc:92-106`: `:bare`,\n`:operand`, `:depth-3`, `:captured`); `cat` is the closure `(fn [p q]\n(conj p q))` bound around every body (`:69-81`). \"Expected\" is the\nreference run's normalised result; where a row says \"executable\" it runs\non all three lanes; \"synthetic\" means the frame is built by hand and\njudged by `frame/defect` only, because no program reaches it on the\nlanded or stage-2 evaluator.\n\n| # | Row | Program (inside each segment) | How parked / satisfied | State, pending | Expected after satisfy | Executable? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | blocked read | `(let1 s (stream/make 4) (let1 c (cursor-of s) (let1 a (next-of c) (cat (cat [] a) (next-of c)))))`, `\"A\"` appended before the run | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next` | `[\"A\" \"B\"]` | yes |\n| 2 | blocked write | `(stream-put w \"v\")` on a one-slot stream warmed with `:warmed` | take one | act, `:put` with retained `\"v\"` | `\"v\"` landed | yes |\n| 3 | FFI sent | `(dao.stream.apply/call :op/echo \"hello\")` | append the success response | act, `:ffi` | `\"echo!\"` | 4b stage 2 |\n| 4 | FFI retained | as 3 with a one-slot, warmed call-in | free the slot, then answer | act, `:ffi-request` | `\"echo!\"` | 4b stage 2 |\n| 5 | effectful call, non-tail, stream | `(cat [:ordinary] (stream/next! c))` | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next` | `[:ordinary \"B\"]` | yes (stage 1 has streams; `stream/next!` is the stream module's) |\n| 6 | **effectful call, tail, stream** | `((fn [u] (stream/next! c)) 0)` with the inner call `:tail? true` | append `\"B\"` | **tail**, `:next` | `\"B\"` delivered through `K` | yes |\n| 7 | link request phase | `(require 'host.mod)` parked **before** the request append succeeds (one-slot, warmed request stream) | free the slot, then answer `:ok` | act, `:link-request` | `'host.mod` | 4b stage 2 |\n| 8 | link response phase | `(require 'host.mod)` polling (request appended) | append the `:ok` response | act, `:link-response` | `'host.mod` | 4b stage 2 |\n| 9 | **link, tail** | `((fn [u] (require 'host.mod)) 0)` tail | as 8 | **tail**, `:link-response` | `'host.mod` through `K` | 4b stage 2 |\n| 10 | install, blocked child (**amended after 6b**) | `(require 'host.mod)` where `host.mod`'s body reads a stream the test has not fed, so the child is `:blocked` and the parent's wait is `:install` | feed the child's stream | act, `:install` + install entry, child kind `:blocked`, **phase `:parked`** | `'host.mod` | yes |\n| 11 | install, `:running` wire phase (**amended after 6b, synthetic phase**) | row 10's machine with the install entry's phase set to `:running` **by hand** before the lift | as 10 | act, `:install`, child kind `:blocked`, phase `:running` | `'host.mod` | the program is row 10's; the phase is not engine-reachable at a safepoint (see below) |\n| 12 | install, tail | `((fn [u] (require 'host.mod)) 0)` tail with 10's child | as 10 | **tail**, `:install` | `'host.mod` through `K` | 4b stage 2 |\n| 13 | explicit park | `(vm/park)` | `(vm/resume pid \"back\")` beside | parked record, root `:parked` | `\"back\"` | yes |\n| 14 | reified capture live in a window | `(let1 k (vm/current-continuation) (cat (cat [] k) (next-of c)))` | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next`; `k` is a `:reified` value in `W` | `[:continuation \"B\"]` normalised | yes |\n| 15 | capture invoked after lower | `(let1 k (vm/current-continuation) (if (= k :second) :done (let1 r (next-of c) (k :second))))`: on the first pass `k` is a continuation, so the test fails and the read runs and parks; after satisfaction `(k :second)` delivers `:second` into the capture's `rd` (the `let1` operand) and re-enters at the capture's successor pc, so the body is entered again with `k = :second` and the test succeeds **without repeating the read** (the invocation is abortive on every lane: `:continuation` operator kind, `srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:212-217` stage 2, `stack.cljc:760-763`). `if` is `{:type :if :test :consequent :alternate}` (`vm.cljc:1069`), a helper the new harness file adds; `=` is the primitive (`vm.cljc:387`), consumed before the read | append `\"x\"` after lower | act, `:next`; `k` is a `:reified` value in `E` (the closure's) | `:done` | yes |\n| 16 | nested capture | `(let1 k1 (vm/current-continuation) (let1 k2 (vm/current-continuation) (cat (cat (cat [] k1) k2) (next-of c))))` | append `\"B\"` | act; `k2`'s act window holds `k1` (a `:reified` inside a `:reified`'s window) | `[:continuation :continuation \"B\"]` | yes |\n| 17 | module context, blocked inside a module closure | `host.mod` exports `f = (fn [c] (stream/next! c))`; root: `(f c)` after `(require 'host.mod)` | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next`, `E` carries `store-of host.mod` | `\"B\"` | yes; **amended after 6b:** the receiver composition is given the source's registry entry for `host.mod`, a composition prerequisite (§2.5): linked registry entries do not travel |\n| 18 | module-store snapshot | `parked-module-store-cursors` (`lift_support.cljc:310-333`): an explicit park whose module store holds two cursors named by a module closure | resume | parked record; `:module-store` row | the two cursor cells | yes; **amended after 6b:** the fixture's module store is hand-built and keyed by the **symbol** `host.mod`, as `frame_test` does, while the kernel keys installed stores by module address; row 17 exercises a real installation |\n| 19 | halt | `(lit 42)` | — | result | `42` | yes |\n| 20 | **tail-effect halt** | run **outside the wrappers**, in two batches on one machine: a setup batch `(then (def! 's (stream/make 4)) (def! 'c (cursor-of s)))` run to halt (store writes persist across batches, as `lift_support.cljc:320-322` relies on), then the batch `(stream/next! c)` **marked `:tail? true` at the root**, `c` resolving through the store; no application precedes the call, so `K` is empty (any `let1` or wrapper would push a frame, §5.2 above) | append `\"B\"` | tail, `:next` | halts with `\"B\"` (4b: a tail effect with no frame to pop halts, `srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:205-211` stage 2; stack: the seam's empty-`K` case, §4.3), env free of `store-of` | yes |\n| 21 | **free-env shadowing** (the review's row) | initial free env `{x 99}`; `(let1 f (fn [] x) ((fn [x] (f (next-of c))) 1))` | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next`; `E` at the read binds `x` to 1; root `free-env` carries `x 99` | the lowered `f` returns `99` (not nil, not 1) on every lane; **the one row the earlier design would have failed** | yes |\n| 22 | nil-valued live register | `(let1 pair (fn [p q] (cat (cat [] p) q)) (pair nil (next-of c)))`: the `nil` operand is evaluated **before** the read and is live across it (`(cat nil (next-of c))` would give `(conj nil \"B\")`, the list `(\"B\")`, so a vector-building `pair` is used; there is no `vector` primitive, `vm.cljc:380-410`, and `pair` builds through the segment's `cat`) | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next`; `W` has a present key with `[:lit nil]` | `[nil \"B\"]` | yes |\n| 23 | tail FFI bookkeeping | a `tail` parked record under a call id `P ∈ :ffi-call-ids` | — | parked, context `:parked`, bookkeeping clause of P4 | validator accepts; the **same valid tail under a key not in `:ffi-call-ids`** is `:parked-site` (P4); moving `site` to a non-tail call is `:site` (T1, which runs first) | **synthetic**: no primitive returns an FFI effect through a tail `:call` today (evaluator §1.6) |\n| 24 | `act` FFI bookkeeping | 3's parked bookkeeping record under its call id | — | parked, P4 bookkeeping | validator accepts | 4b stage 2 |\n\n**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13), rows 10-12 and the install contract\n(B1).** The earlier text promised a child \"`:blocked`, phase `:running`\"\n(row 10) and a child \"executing `:park`, kind `:parked`\" (row 11).\nNeither is an engine state at a quiescent safepoint. `advance-install`\n(`engine.cljc:1421-1454`) runs the child inside the sweep and then\nrecords it: a **blocked** child is retained at phase **`:parked`**\n(`:1444-1446`); a **halted** child goes straight to `link-install` in the\nsame sweep (`:1447-1452`), so it is never retained; and a child that\nexecutes `:park` is `:halted?` with a parked value, so it too goes to\n`link-install`, which completes the install if the exports are already\ndefined and otherwise refuses it `:export-missing`. Phase `:running` is\nassigned only in the `:else` arm, within one sweep, and is overwritten\nbefore any safepoint. So: row 10 is the engine's real state (child\n`:blocked`, phase `:parked`); row 11 exercises the `:running` **wire\nphase**, which the codec accepts (`install-node` admits `#{:running\n:parked}` on lift and `check-install!` on lower) and the harness\nconstructs by hand on row 10's machine (`installing % false :running`,\n`safepoint_harness_test.cljc:342`); row 12 is row 10's child under tail\ndelivery. No row promises an executable kind-`:parked` install child on\nthe current engine (owner question §11 Q1). **The install validation\nthe lower enforces** (`check-install!`, before any attachment, §7.2):\nthe phase is one of `#{:running :parked}` (`:install-phase`); the\nretained response is a map with `:status :ok`, a `:manifest` map whose\n`:yin.module/name` equals the install's module name and whose\n`:yin.module/exports` is a collection of symbols, and an `:image` map\nwhose `:value` the receiving kernel's loader admits (`:install-response`);\nand the carried child task's kind is **`:blocked`** (`:install-child`),\nbecause a halted or parked child would have been linked in the sweep it\nhalted in. On lift, `install-node` refuses a phase outside the set as\n`:yin.k/non-portable :incomplete-install` and a response CBOR cannot\nencode as `:yin.k/non-portable :install-response`.\n\nRefusal rows: `:yin.k/not-quiescent` (`a-nonempty-ready-queue-is-not-quiescent`,\n`:591-603`); `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` (a register kernel stepped one\ninstruction past a `:const`, asked to lift); `:yin.k/non-portable\n:reason-mismatch` (the `:observe` hold, `:675-692`); and the validator's\n**membership tampers**, each a row-set edit of a valid body before\nlower: a window with an extra live key → `:window-extra`; a live key\nremoved → `:window-missing`; `rd` added → `:window-self`; `rd` replaced\nby another register → `:destination`; a return frame's `pc` moved after\na tail call → `:return-site`; a reason the site does not admit →\n`:reason-mismatch`; a `:tail` row given a fourth slot → **`:row-shape`\nat the codec's structure step (2), not `:frame-shape`** (amended after\n6b, B3: the row table fixes `:tail` at three slots, so the wire never\nreaches rule S1 with a fourth slot; S1 still governs an abstract frame\nhanded to `frame/defect` directly; and because `emit`/`emit-numbered`\nsilently drop extra slots from an abstract node, mapping over the\nshorter descriptor list, this tamper must be a **raw row edit with a\nrehash**, never an abstract-task edit);\nthe `[:lit nil]` of row 22 dropped → `:window-missing`; a `:reified`\nact whose `pc−1` is not a capture → `:captured-site`; a parked act whose\n`pc−1` is not `:park` → `:parked-site`; for E2, with row 21's initial\nfree environment extended to `{x 99 y 7}`, the **unshadowed** free\nbinding `y` in the act's `E` changed to `8` → `:free-env-mismatch`\n(changing the lexical `x`, which is `1` at the read, trips nothing: E2\ncompares off the lexical names), and the lexical `x` **removed** from\n`E` → `:free-env-mismatch` (presence clause (a)); the root `free-env`\nrow given a reserved key → `:reserved-name` (X1). **A changed live value**\n(e.g. `\"A\"` → `\"Z\"` in row 1's window) is not a membership defect and is\nnot expected to be refused: it is tested by **execution parity** (the\nlowered run's result differs from the reference), as the review ruled.\n\n### 5.3 Lanes\n\nThe `:semantic-register` lane's wait entries are canonical frames plus\nengine fields. The de Bruijn lanes load `(:image (derive/stack-image v))`\nand `(:image (derive/register-image v))` of `v = (:vector (project P))`\nthrough `dvm/create-vm`/`rvm/create-vm`, run to the safepoint and lift\nthrough `layout/lift-act`/`lift-tail`; the stack lane's tail rows (6,\n9, 12, 20) require the seam of §4.3 and are gated on 6c-pre.\n\n### 5.4 Laws\n\n1. **Reference parity**: result and effect trace of the lowered,\n   satisfied machine equal the reference run (`reference`/`handed-off`,\n   `:368-401`).\n2. **Round trip**: `lift(lower(S)) = S` by root id under the\n   fresh-receiver context of the draft's §9, after the re-emission of\n   §5.5.\n3. **Observed reason**: the pending's reason is the wait's, never the\n   static kind (ucf-revisions §8).\n4. **Cross-engine canonical equality**: one program at one safepoint\n   lifts to row sets whose root ids are **equal after the re-emission of\n   §5.5** on all three lanes.\n5. **Cross-engine lowering**: a row set lifted on one lane lowers on\n   each of the other two (six ordered pairs) and continues to the\n   reference result.\n6. **Validator agreement**: every lifted frame passes `frame/defect` in\n   its context; every tamper of §5.2 is refused with its kind; the\n   verdict is lane-independent.\n\n### 5.5 Re-emission for equality (replacing \"stream-blinding\")\n\n**PROPOSED** (reviewer: `stream-blind` rewrites decoded maps and does\nnot recompute Merkle ids, so it cannot establish root-id equality). The\nharness first **fixes identities where it can**: all three lanes serve\ntheir streams through one toy channel (`v2_support/toy`,\n`served-peer`), so stream identities and descriptors are equal across\nlanes by construction where the same logical stream is lifted. Where a\nlane mints its own (fresh cursor cells on relift, the receiver's own call\npair), the comparison uses `rows/canonical-under`, a **test-only**\ntransformation whose placeholder assignment is **never derived from\n*host* traversal order** (the canonical walk of draft §6 is a function\nof the state and is what the procedure reads; the review's objection: map and set traversal, and\n\"cursor cells by body\", can depend on the very identities being erased). It\ntakes an **explicit logical-stream correspondence** from the fixture:\nthe harness created every stream the row reaches, so it supplies\n`{lane-identity → logical-name}` (e.g. `{id-of-s :s, id-of-call-out\n:call-out}`) for each lane; `canonical-under` decodes the row set to the\nabstract task (draft §6's input), replaces each stream marker's identity\nand descriptor by the logical name the map gives it (a marker the map\ndoes not cover is a test failure, never a guess), runs the canonical\ncursor-cell numbering (draft §6), re-emits every row and recomputes every id,\nand compares root ids. Draft §6 is invariant under renaming of the\n**cursor cells** (its labels never encode an incoming cursor-cell id), and the only\nother identity it consumes is the stream marker in each cursor cell's body\nlabel (`[:body [:stream identity descriptor] position]`, draft §6 as\namended, spelled by rank inside keys) and in cursor-cell bodies; after the substitution those are the fixture's\nlogical names, so the procedure's inputs no longer depend on any lane's\nidentities. Guest sets and maps are entered in the bytewise order of\ntheir members' keys, and `:store`, `:bindings` and the parked table in\nthe byte order of their structural keys (draft §6 W1), never in host\nenumeration order, so\na permutation of the erased identities cannot change the result once\nthe markers are substituted. It is never used outside the harness.\n\n### 5.6 Static checks the harness runs once per corpus segment\n\n1. `frame/defect` on every frame the reference lane mints: nil.\n2. For every **reachable** parking pc `p` of `v`: `(analysis/saved a\n   (inc p) rd)` has no member `≥ k(body(p))` and does not contain `rd`.\n3. Register layout: at every reachable boundary pc `p`, `virt(p, s)` is\n   single-valued on the physical image of `live-out*(p)`; the `live`\n   operand of `R[p]` equals `(sort (map phys (disj live-out*(p) rd)))`,\n   the destination removed as a **virtual** id before mapping, which is\n   `[]` for a tail call (phase-5 §2.5). Worked example, inner call at\n   `p = 3`: `L(4) = {1 2}`, minus `rd 2` → `{1}` → `[0]`, the pinned\n   `live` of phase-5 §6.3 row 2.\n4. Stack layout: the k-th non-push instruction of `H` has the mnemonic\n   family of `v[k]` for every `k`; counts agree.\n5. Stack layout: `(set (stack-layout p)) = (analysis/saved a (inc p)\n   rd)` for every reachable **non-tail** parking pc `p`.\n6. `E` reconstruction: at every safepoint reached, the layout's `E`\n   equals the reference lane's `E` (values normalised), including rows\n   21 and 22 and the `:duplicate-param`, `:nested-closure`,\n   `:nested-lambdas` programs.\n7. Closure reconstruction: canonical ↔ positional closures round-trip on\n   the `\"closure value\"` and `\"higher order\"` B0 rows and\n   `:duplicate-param`.\n8. Tail lowering: the `:frames`/`:store-of` chosen for a lowered `tail`\n   entry are unobservable: lowering with `[]`/`nil` and with arbitrary\n   values gives equal observables on rows 6, 9, 12, 20.\n9. Slices: for every non-tail safepoint with `m` return frames, the\n   `m+1` slices of §4.3 partition the stack kernel's operand stack\n   exactly, and each has the length of its `stack-layout`.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Code and task context in the carrier\n","numLines":245,"startLine":825,"totalLines":2118}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01TTn2bRdeRVASRQZFG5dPhz","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CfyvjpgLio3WkfzohXe9A","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01R67pvpjRKp6KtW2UiBjwo1","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7plan/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md","offset":185,"limit":181},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":45022,"cache_read_input_tokens":346542,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":45022},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"23e67cb0-f183-4c2e-b3d6-2e5b6c44b814","uuid":"4c3687f0-65c8-431f-8515-79d4549b18a5","timestamp":"2026-10-13T08:33:52.805Z","request_id":"req_011CfyvjpKmKznoA1oYPXdog","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01R67pvpjRKp6KtW2UiBjwo1":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7plan/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md","offset":185,"limit":181}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01R67pvpjRKp6KtW2UiBjwo1","type":"tool_result","content":"185\t### 7.2.1 The handoff body: the task wire grammar, versions 0 and 1\n186\t\n187\t**Scope (2026-10-11).** Body versions 0, 1 and 2 and every clause of\n188\tthis document that admits, reads, recovers or interoperates with them\n189\tare **historical**: they describe the stack-shaped machine's handoff,\n190\twhich leaves at `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md` §10 phase 8. The row\n191\tcarrier of body version 3 (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md` as amended by\n192\t`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md`) reads no other version and\n193\ttakes **precedence** over any sentence below that implies otherwise; a\n194\tconflict is resolved in its favour without further amendment. **Not\n195\thistorical**: the custody, fencing and operation-id semantics (§7.7.8,\n196\t§7.9, §7.4.3's operation ids) travel in version 3 as the root's\n197\t`custody` slot (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md` §5.5) and stay\n198\toperative; where those sections say \"a version-1 body\" read, for version\n199\t3, \"a root whose custody policy is `:yin.k/exclusive`\", and where they\n200\tsay \"a version-0 body\" or \"fork\", \"a root whose policy is `:yin.k/fork`\"\n201\t(§5.5's table). The code stamp `{:yin.code/contract \"v3\" :yin.k/version\n202\t0}` wherever this document shows it (§7.2's envelope example, the body\n203\texample below, §7.3.3) is the stack-shaped machine's; the\n204\tregister-shaped machine's contract is `\"v4\"`\n205\t(`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md` §6), the one the version-3 profile map\n206\tnames, and §7.2's envelope example is superseded for version 3 by the\n207\trow carrier's wire unit (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md` §3, ids under\n208\tJing's default digest).\n209\t\n210\t(Amendment r5, 2026-10-04; `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.3 item 2.) The\n211\tenvelope above is the design shape. The value a driver publishes is the\n212\t**handoff body**: one task, the whole blocked machine of 7.6.3, as one\n213\tmap in canonical CBOR (`dao.jing.cbor.md`) under its content address.\n214\tWhere the envelope sketch and this grammar differ, this grammar is the\n215\twire contract. A task has an ordered wait set, so the body carries\n216\tordered `:yin.k/frames`, never one `:yin.k/frame`. The scheduler slice of\n217\t7.6.3 is flattened into top-level keys. Code travels as an address-keyed\n218\tmap. The body carries no `:yin.k/id`: its address is the hash of the body\n219\t(7.3.4). A halt is a body of kind `:halted`, the `:yin.k/result` sibling.\n220\t\n221\t```clojure\n222\t{:yin.k/handoff       true      ; the tag\n223\t :yin.k/version       0         ; the BODY's version: 0 or 1\n224\t :yin.k/kind          :blocked  ; :blocked | :parked | :halted\n225\t :yin.k/contract      {:yin.code/contract \"v3\" :yin.k/version 0}\n226\t :yin.k/id-counter    n         ; 7.6.3 fresh-name state\n227\t :yin.k/parked        {pid registers}      ; 7.6.3 parked records\n228\t :yin.k/store         {encoded encoded}    ; 7.6.2 isolated slice\n229\t :yin.k/module-stores {module {encoded encoded}}\n230\t :yin.k/cells         {cursor-cell {:yin.k/stream marker\n231\t                             :yin.k/position p}}   ; 7.5.3\n232\t :yin.k/requires      {:yin.k/cursor-profiles #{profile}\n233\t                       :yin.k/segments #{address}}\n234\t :yin.k/code          {address vector}     ; 7.3.4, each verified\n235\t ;; by kind\n236\t :yin.k/frames        [{:yin.k/registers registers\n237\t                        :yin.k/pending pending}]   ; in wait order\n238\t :yin.k/parked-id     pid       ; :parked only: the active record\n239\t :yin.k/result        encoded   ; :halted only\n240\t ;; when the task holds live install children (7.4.3)\n241\t :yin.k/installs      {module install}}\n242\t```\n243\t\n244\tThe spellings `:yin.k/cells`, `:yin.k/cell`, `:yin.k/response-cell` and `:yin.k/c-<n>` are the body-version 0/1/2 wire spellings, as the version-2 code (`ucf/handoff.cljc`, `ucf/remote.cljc`) uses them; version 3, the row carrier of `yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`, spells the same cursor cells `:yin.k/cursor-cells`, `:yin.k/cursor-cell`, `:yin.k/response-cursor-cell` and `:yin.k/cc-<n>`.\n245\t\n246\t`registers` is `{:yin.k/segment address :yin.k/pc n :yin.k/env {name\n247\tencoded} :yin.k/stack [encoded] :yin.k/k [frame]}`, every key required,\n248\tthe segment a key of `:yin.k/code` and the pc inside that vector.\n249\t`pending` is one variant of 7.4.3. Every value is in the 7.5 grammar.\n250\t\n251\tShape rules, each a `:yin.k/undecodable` refusal naming its path:\n252\t\n253\t- `:blocked` requires nonempty frames. `:halted` requires\n254\t  `:yin.k/result` and forbids frames. `:parked` requires\n255\t  `:yin.k/parked-id` naming a key of `:yin.k/parked`. A `:yin.k/result`\n256\t  on any other kind is refused.\n257\t- Frames restore in their carried order, for `:blocked` and for\n258\t  `:parked` alike; a resumer never drops a carried frame (7.4.3).\n259\t- A frame's resume pc is a static safepoint whose kinds admit the\n260\t  pending's reason; a parked record's resume pc is an `:explicit-park`\n261\t  safepoint (`yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md` section 8).\n262\t- The cursor cells the body carries are exactly the cursor cells its roots name:\n263\t  none missing, none extra. A body with cursor cells claims\n264\t  `:dao.stream.remote/v1`, else `:yin.k/unsatisfied`.\n265\t- Each code vector hashes to its key (`:yin.k/hash-mismatch`) and is\n266\t  well formed under the stamp.\n267\t\n268\tLift adds these kinds to the `:yin.k/non-portable` set of 7.5.4:\n269\t`:incomplete-install`, `:install-response`, `:incomplete-write`,\n270\t`:reason-mismatch`, `:inconsistent-halt`, `:unaddressed-segment`,\n271\t`:address-mismatch`, and, under version 1, `:unprotected-pending` and\n272\t`:op-seq-exhausted` (7.4.3).\n273\t\n274\t**Two version keys.** `:yin.k/version` at the top of the body is the\n275\tbody's own version and is what this amendment raises. The key of the\n276\tsame name inside `:yin.k/contract` belongs to the code stamp of 7.3.3.\n277\tThat stamp is compared whole and stays `{:yin.code/contract \"v3\"\n278\t:yin.k/version 0}`: the amendment changes handoff data and composition\n279\tadmission, not opcode semantics, so code stamps \"v3\", \"b2\" and \"r2\" and\n280\tmodule manifest schema 1 are unchanged, and cursor vectors keep their\n281\tbytes.\n282\t\n283\t**The version gate.** A resumer decodes the bytes, checks the tag, and\n284\tthen checks the body version before any other rule. A version it does\n285\tnot speak, an absent version, or a version that is not an integer is\n286\t`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`, carrying `:yin.k/version` as found and\n287\t`:yin.k/supported`, the set the resumer speaks. The gate runs before\n288\tthe remaining grammar, before any code hash is verified, before any\n289\tstream is attached, before any proposal is made, and before anything\n290\tis restored. An install child's body carries the version of the body\n291\tthat holds it. Validate every nested body's version and structural\n292\trole before attachment, proposal, or restoration; a supported but\n293\tmixed-version tree is `:yin.k/undecodable`. The integer check is on\n294\tthe canonical codec's integer kind, never on numeric equality: an\n295\tintegral float such as `1.0` must not validate as a version on any\n296\thost, Node included, where both would compare equal to 1. A reader\n297\tthat speaks only version 0 refuses a version-1 body by this gate and\n298\tnever reads it as fork data.\n299\t\n300\t**Version 0** is the grammar above and nothing more. It is a fork: it\n301\tnames no occurrence, proposes nothing, and runs through no fenced\n302\twriter. A version-0 body stays valid under this contract and is never\n303\tupgraded to fenced custody, by a resumer or by a carrier. A version-0\n304\treader ignores keys it does not know, so a custody key inside a\n305\tversion-0 body establishes nothing. A reader that speaks version 1\n306\tkeeps these fork semantics for a version-0 body exactly, and refuses\n307\ta version-0 body as `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` when exclusive custody\n308\tis required. Version 0 is fork only and version 1 is exclusive only.\n309\t\n310\t**Version 1** is the fenced-custody grammar. A version-1 root body of\n311\tkind `:blocked` or `:parked` adds a custody header:\n312\t\n313\t```clojure\n314\t{:yin.k/version     1\n315\t :yin.k/policy      :yin.k/exclusive\n316\t :yin.k/occurrence  O           ; the lease subject, 7.3.4 and 7.7.2\n317\t :yin.k/arbitration {:dao.stream/identity i\n318\t                     :dao.stream/descriptor d}     ; 7.7.3\n319\t :yin.k/origin      {:yin.k/occurrence P           ; the predecessor\n320\t                     :dao.lease/lease L            ; the grant it ran in\n321\t                     :yin.k/emitter a}             ; absent on first park\n322\t :yin.k/next-op-seq n}          ; 7.7.8\n323\t```\n324\t\n325\tThe header belongs to the **root** body alone. Validation is\n326\tcontext-sensitive: the root is validated as a root, and each nested\n327\tinstall child is validated as a child, with the root's occurrence,\n328\torigin, and counter passed down to it. Header rules, each\n329\t`:yin.k/undecodable` naming its path:\n330\t\n331\t- On a root of kind `:blocked` or `:parked`, `:yin.k/policy`,\n332\t  `:yin.k/occurrence`, `:yin.k/arbitration` and `:yin.k/next-op-seq`\n333\t  are required. The policy is exactly `:yin.k/exclusive`: version 1\n334\t  has no fork form, and fork uses version 0.\n335\t- `:yin.k/occurrence` is plain data in the canonical bytes domain,\n336\t  never nil, compared by its canonical bytes. It is stable across\n337\t  every publication retry and every restart of the emitter, distinct\n338\t  for distinct parks, and collision-resistant or made unique by the\n339\t  authority. The authority rejects a conflicting reuse: an offer of a\n340\t  known occurrence for a different park establishes nothing. The\n341\t  concrete form is fixed by M-next C, within these invariants.\n342\t  The concrete form (M-next C, slice C5): a UUID string in lowercase\n343\t  hex, one spelling, so canonical-byte equality is string equality.\n344\t  Any other form is `:malformed-occurrence`, in the root, its origin\n345\t  and every carried operation id. A reuse indistinguishable from a\n346\t  snapshot variant (equal occurrence, origin and baseline) is\n347\t  admitted as one.\n348\t- `:yin.k/origin` is absent on a first export and required on every\n349\t  successor root. Its occurrence differs from the body's own.\n350\t- `:yin.k/next-op-seq` is an exact integer of 7.7.8. It is 0 on a\n351\t  first export: that checkpoint is the recorded operation baseline.\n352\t- A root of kind `:halted` carries `:yin.k/origin` and none of the\n353\t  other header keys: a result is not a lease subject, and nothing\n354\t  remains to admit.\n355\t- An install child of any kind, halted included, carries none of the\n356\t  header keys, the origin among them. A child is part of its root's\n357\t  task, not a custody subject: its effects are admitted under the\n358\t  root's occurrence and drawn from the root's sequence, and its\n359\t  carried operation ids are checked against the root's origin and\n360\t  counter (7.4.3).\n361\t\n362\tNo body carries an epoch. A body is minted before any grant and its\n363\tbytes do not change on reclaim; the epoch is learned only from the\n364\tgrant binding of 7.7.8.\n365\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"23e67cb0-f183-4c2e-b3d6-2e5b6c44b814","uuid":"5516f1f7-8e55-4380-9505-472cd63ec0b7","timestamp":"2026-10-13T08:33:52.824Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7plan/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md","content":"### 7.2.1 The handoff body: the task wire grammar, versions 0 and 1\n\n**Scope (2026-10-11).** Body versions 0, 1 and 2 and every clause of\nthis document that admits, reads, recovers or interoperates with them\nare **historical**: they describe the stack-shaped machine's handoff,\nwhich leaves at `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md` §10 phase 8. The row\ncarrier of body version 3 (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md` as amended by\n`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md`) reads no other version and\ntakes **precedence** over any sentence below that implies otherwise; a\nconflict is resolved in its favour without further amendment. **Not\nhistorical**: the custody, fencing and operation-id semantics (§7.7.8,\n§7.9, §7.4.3's operation ids) travel in version 3 as the root's\n`custody` slot (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md` §5.5) and stay\noperative; where those sections say \"a version-1 body\" read, for version\n3, \"a root whose custody policy is `:yin.k/exclusive`\", and where they\nsay \"a version-0 body\" or \"fork\", \"a root whose policy is `:yin.k/fork`\"\n(§5.5's table). The code stamp `{:yin.code/contract \"v3\" :yin.k/version\n0}` wherever this document shows it (§7.2's envelope example, the body\nexample below, §7.3.3) is the stack-shaped machine's; the\nregister-shaped machine's contract is `\"v4\"`\n(`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md` §6), the one the version-3 profile map\nnames, and §7.2's envelope example is superseded for version 3 by the\nrow carrier's wire unit (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md` §3, ids under\nJing's default digest).\n\n(Amendment r5, 2026-10-04; `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.3 item 2.) The\nenvelope above is the design shape. The value a driver publishes is the\n**handoff body**: one task, the whole blocked machine of 7.6.3, as one\nmap in canonical CBOR (`dao.jing.cbor.md`) under its content address.\nWhere the envelope sketch and this grammar differ, this grammar is the\nwire contract. A task has an ordered wait set, so the body carries\nordered `:yin.k/frames`, never one `:yin.k/frame`. The scheduler slice of\n7.6.3 is flattened into top-level keys. Code travels as an address-keyed\nmap. The body carries no `:yin.k/id`: its address is the hash of the body\n(7.3.4). A halt is a body of kind `:halted`, the `:yin.k/result` sibling.\n\n```clojure\n{:yin.k/handoff       true      ; the tag\n :yin.k/version       0         ; the BODY's version: 0 or 1\n :yin.k/kind          :blocked  ; :blocked | :parked | :halted\n :yin.k/contract      {:yin.code/contract \"v3\" :yin.k/version 0}\n :yin.k/id-counter    n         ; 7.6.3 fresh-name state\n :yin.k/parked        {pid registers}      ; 7.6.3 parked records\n :yin.k/store         {encoded encoded}    ; 7.6.2 isolated slice\n :yin.k/module-stores {module {encoded encoded}}\n :yin.k/cells         {cursor-cell {:yin.k/stream marker\n                             :yin.k/position p}}   ; 7.5.3\n :yin.k/requires      {:yin.k/cursor-profiles #{profile}\n                       :yin.k/segments #{address}}\n :yin.k/code          {address vector}     ; 7.3.4, each verified\n ;; by kind\n :yin.k/frames        [{:yin.k/registers registers\n                        :yin.k/pending pending}]   ; in wait order\n :yin.k/parked-id     pid       ; :parked only: the active record\n :yin.k/result        encoded   ; :halted only\n ;; when the task holds live install children (7.4.3)\n :yin.k/installs      {module install}}\n```\n\nThe spellings `:yin.k/cells`, `:yin.k/cell`, `:yin.k/response-cell` and `:yin.k/c-<n>` are the body-version 0/1/2 wire spellings, as the version-2 code (`ucf/handoff.cljc`, `ucf/remote.cljc`) uses them; version 3, the row carrier of `yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`, spells the same cursor cells `:yin.k/cursor-cells`, `:yin.k/cursor-cell`, `:yin.k/response-cursor-cell` and `:yin.k/cc-<n>`.\n\n`registers` is `{:yin.k/segment address :yin.k/pc n :yin.k/env {name\nencoded} :yin.k/stack [encoded] :yin.k/k [frame]}`, every key required,\nthe segment a key of `:yin.k/code` and the pc inside that vector.\n`pending` is one variant of 7.4.3. Every value is in the 7.5 grammar.\n\nShape rules, each a `:yin.k/undecodable` refusal naming its path:\n\n- `:blocked` requires nonempty frames. `:halted` requires\n  `:yin.k/result` and forbids frames. `:parked` requires\n  `:yin.k/parked-id` naming a key of `:yin.k/parked`. A `:yin.k/result`\n  on any other kind is refused.\n- Frames restore in their carried order, for `:blocked` and for\n  `:parked` alike; a resumer never drops a carried frame (7.4.3).\n- A frame's resume pc is a static safepoint whose kinds admit the\n  pending's reason; a parked record's resume pc is an `:explicit-park`\n  safepoint (`yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md` section 8).\n- The cursor cells the body carries are exactly the cursor cells its roots name:\n  none missing, none extra. A body with cursor cells claims\n  `:dao.stream.remote/v1`, else `:yin.k/unsatisfied`.\n- Each code vector hashes to its key (`:yin.k/hash-mismatch`) and is\n  well formed under the stamp.\n\nLift adds these kinds to the `:yin.k/non-portable` set of 7.5.4:\n`:incomplete-install`, `:install-response`, `:incomplete-write`,\n`:reason-mismatch`, `:inconsistent-halt`, `:unaddressed-segment`,\n`:address-mismatch`, and, under version 1, `:unprotected-pending` and\n`:op-seq-exhausted` (7.4.3).\n\n**Two version keys.** `:yin.k/version` at the top of the body is the\nbody's own version and is what this amendment raises. The key of the\nsame name inside `:yin.k/contract` belongs to the code stamp of 7.3.3.\nThat stamp is compared whole and stays `{:yin.code/contract \"v3\"\n:yin.k/version 0}`: the amendment changes handoff data and composition\nadmission, not opcode semantics, so code stamps \"v3\", \"b2\" and \"r2\" and\nmodule manifest schema 1 are unchanged, and cursor vectors keep their\nbytes.\n\n**The version gate.** A resumer decodes the bytes, checks the tag, and\nthen checks the body version before any other rule. A version it does\nnot speak, an absent version, or a version that is not an integer is\n`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`, carrying `:yin.k/version` as found and\n`:yin.k/supported`, the set the resumer speaks. The gate runs before\nthe remaining grammar, before any code hash is verified, before any\nstream is attached, before any proposal is made, and before anything\nis restored. An install child's body carries the version of the body\nthat holds it. Validate every nested body's version and structural\nrole before attachment, proposal, or restoration; a supported but\nmixed-version tree is `:yin.k/undecodable`. The integer check is on\nthe canonical codec's integer kind, never on numeric equality: an\nintegral float such as `1.0` must not validate as a version on any\nhost, Node included, where both would compare equal to 1. A reader\nthat speaks only version 0 refuses a version-1 body by this gate and\nnever reads it as fork data.\n\n**Version 0** is the grammar above and nothing more. It is a fork: it\nnames no occurrence, proposes nothing, and runs through no fenced\nwriter. A version-0 body stays valid under this contract and is never\nupgraded to fenced custody, by a resumer or by a carrier. A version-0\nreader ignores keys it does not know, so a custody key inside a\nversion-0 body establishes nothing. A reader that speaks version 1\nkeeps these fork semantics for a version-0 body exactly, and refuses\na version-0 body as `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` when exclusive custody\nis required. Version 0 is fork only and version 1 is exclusive only.\n\n**Version 1** is the fenced-custody grammar. A version-1 root body of\nkind `:blocked` or `:parked` adds a custody header:\n\n```clojure\n{:yin.k/version     1\n :yin.k/policy      :yin.k/exclusive\n :yin.k/occurrence  O           ; the lease subject, 7.3.4 and 7.7.2\n :yin.k/arbitration {:dao.stream/identity i\n                     :dao.stream/descriptor d}     ; 7.7.3\n :yin.k/origin      {:yin.k/occurrence P           ; the predecessor\n                     :dao.lease/lease L            ; the grant it ran in\n                     :yin.k/emitter a}             ; absent on first park\n :yin.k/next-op-seq n}          ; 7.7.8\n```\n\nThe header belongs to the **root** body alone. Validation is\ncontext-sensitive: the root is validated as a root, and each nested\ninstall child is validated as a child, with the root's occurrence,\norigin, and counter passed down to it. Header rules, each\n`:yin.k/undecodable` naming its path:\n\n- On a root of kind `:blocked` or `:parked`, `:yin.k/policy`,\n  `:yin.k/occurrence`, `:yin.k/arbitration` and `:yin.k/next-op-seq`\n  are required. The policy is exactly `:yin.k/exclusive`: version 1\n  has no fork form, and fork uses version 0.\n- `:yin.k/occurrence` is plain data in the canonical bytes domain,\n  never nil, compared by its canonical bytes. It is stable across\n  every publication retry and every restart of the emitter, distinct\n  for distinct parks, and collision-resistant or made unique by the\n  authority. The authority rejects a conflicting reuse: an offer of a\n  known occurrence for a different park establishes nothing. The\n  concrete form is fixed by M-next C, within these invariants.\n  The concrete form (M-next C, slice C5): a UUID string in lowercase\n  hex, one spelling, so canonical-byte equality is string equality.\n  Any other form is `:malformed-occurrence`, in the root, its origin\n  and every carried operation id. A reuse indistinguishable from a\n  snapshot variant (equal occurrence, origin and baseline) is\n  admitted as one.\n- `:yin.k/origin` is absent on a first export and required on every\n  successor root. Its occurrence differs from the body's own.\n- `:yin.k/next-op-seq` is an exact integer of 7.7.8. It is 0 on a\n  first export: that checkpoint is the recorded operation baseline.\n- A root of kind `:halted` carries `:yin.k/origin` and none of the\n  other header keys: a result is not a lease subject, and nothing\n  remains to admit.\n- An install child of any kind, halted included, carries none of the\n  header keys, the origin among them. A child is part of its root's\n  task, not a custody subject: its effects are admitted under the\n  root's occurrence and drawn from the root's sequence, and its\n  carried operation ids are checked against the root's origin and\n  counter (7.4.3).\n\nNo body carries an epoch. A body is minted before any grant and its\nbytes do not change on reclaim; the epoch is learned only from the\ngrant binding of 7.7.8.\n","numLines":181,"startLine":185,"totalLines":3333}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01R67pvpjRKp6KtW2UiBjwo1","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
