 travels as the root's `custody` slot per the draft's table. Lease, epoch and holder never enter a body. Op-ids already have their pending slots.\n- Content stores verify bytes against the address on put, and a pack does not hash to its root id. So the checkpoint has two identities: the pack address for storage and the ledger's `:yin.k/id`, and the root id inside the baseline. `emit` must sort rows so one root gives one pack on every host.\n- The recovery object drops its second snapshot body (rows already carry everything) and keeps only holder-local engine state.\n- Crash cuts stay meaningful if the pack is stored as one blob.\n- `:resume-mode` needs no work: the version-2 lane is frozen, and `:act`/`:tail` rows subsume it.\n\n**Slicing and estimate.** 7a custody on rows and the rows inspector with regenerated fixtures, 7b holder export and recovery v2 and the driver's lower, 7c composition and the compose/fencing row tests, 7d Stage E on three hosts and the JVM/Node process matrix, 7e docs. Critical path is 7a through 7d. Median 12 working days, 17 at the worst.\n\n**Verdict: NOT READY.** Three owner questions block the start: fork spelling in the custody slot (nil as landed is recommended), the REPL custody gap until phase 8 (the REPL still composes the old kernel; accepting the gap is recommended), and whether the continuation §11 Q3 install checks land in 7b (recommended yes). Everything else is decided and 7a can start the moment the fork-spelling answer arrives.","ttft_ms":1862,"type":"result","duration_ms":649954,"uuid":"d1cc971d-ad8f-428b-a78d-e036c3277da2","ttft_stream_ms":1757,"time_to_request_ms":47,"first_content_frame_ms":1758,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
